-- ====================================================================================================
-- seat questions from part ccc10 (merged by the lead on 2026-11-12; unchanged text follows)
-- ====================================================================================================
-- ccc10-seat.l4 -- THE SEAT LAYER over the Criminal Code (Canada), R.S.C., 1985, c. C-46, sections 278.32 to 320.104 (part ccc10).
--
-- SEAT: a person who is, or may be, accused of an offence under the Criminal Code (the accused), asking on facts they can state whether what they
-- did is an offence, what its gravity and penalty are, what defences, rights and procedures apply at each stage from arrest through trial, sentence
-- and appeal, and what follows from a conviction. (Assumed, not ruled: Singapore's Penal Code is the model, as the maintainer instructed on 2026-11-09, but
-- this is a plain seat layer; no charge-generator contract.) Provisions that address only a judge are asked for what they mean to the accused.
--
-- Every question below calls a rule of the layer-1 modules and restates none. Every question name begins "Under section N," with the Act's own
-- numbering, and asks in the direction of the rule it calls: a rule that is a bar is asked as a bar; a rule that is a defence is asked as a defence.
-- A result is JUST TRUE (yes, on the text's words), JUST FALSE (the text says no in its own words) or NOTHING (not covered): NOTES.md,
-- "Reading a result: FALSE and NOTHING (ccc10)". The answers taken from other parts (s 278.3, s 2 complainant, s 787(1), the firearm terms of s 2 and s 2.1, s 83.01(1) listed entity, s 197(1) place) are
-- asked here as plain facts the person can state; a "no" they give is their statement of fact, and a seat answer never turns a NOTHING into a FALSE.
-- Where one rule of this part depends on another rule of this part (s 303 on s 297, s 300 on ss 298, 299 and 303 to 316, s 291 on s 290), the question calls both and asks for the facts of both;
-- the defences of ss 303 to 316 are asked for one plain fact (does one of them clearly apply to me), because their aggregation takes a list of answers.
-- Where an input is wanted only by one limb of a rule, the other inputs are still asked.


-- Layer 2. Warrants, forfeiture and orders against a computer system are asked for what they mean to the person whose publication or data is affected.
§ `Hate propaganda, hate crime and conversion therapy (ss 318 to 320.104)`

@export Under section 318(1), am I guilty of the offence of advocating genocide?
GIVEN `the person advocates or promotes genocide` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does this describe you or what you did: the person advocates or promotes genocide?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 318(1), am I guilty of the offence of advocating genocide` IS
    `s 318(1) the person is guilty of the offence of advocating genocide` `the person advocates or promotes genocide`

@export Under section 318(2), is the act genocide?
GIVEN `the act is killing members of the group` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it true that the act is killing members of the group?
