§ `DNA analysis: is the offence a designated offence (s 487.04)`

-- Situation: a DNA order or warrant is being sought about the offence you are charged with. Ask 'Is a DNA warrant or order being sought in your case?' before showing the group.

-- Under section 487.04, is the offence a primary designated offence.
@ref s 487.04
@export Under section 487.04, is the offence I am charged with a primary designated offence?
GIVEN `the section number of the offence` IS A NUMBER @desc What is the section number of the offence? (for example 271, or 83.18)
      `the subsection of the offence` IS A STRING @desc Which subsection of that section, as written in the Code, for example "(2)" or "(1)(d)"? (Leave empty if none.)
      `where the offence provision is found` IS A STRING @desc Which text is the offence in? One of: "this Act as it reads now", "this Act before the paragraph came into force", "this Act before July 1, 1990", "this Act before the conveyances amendment", "the 1970 Code before January 4, 1983", "the 1970 Code before January 1, 1988", "the 1970 Code as enacted by chapter 125", "the Foreign Interference and Security of Information Act", "the Cannabis Act", "the Controlled Drugs and Substances Act", "another instrument".
      `how I am charged` IS A STRING @desc How are you charged? One of: "with the offence itself", "with an attempt", "with a conspiracy".
      `the purpose` IS A STRING @desc What is the answer wanted for? One of: "a warrant under subsection 487.05(1)", "an order under section 487.051", "another purpose".
      `the offence may be prosecuted by indictment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc May the offence be prosecuted by indictment?
      `the offence is prosecuted by indictment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it being prosecuted by indictment?
      `the offence carries imprisonment for life` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the offence carry imprisonment for life?
      `the longest term of imprisonment, in years` IS A NUMBER @desc What is the longest term of imprisonment the offence carries, in years? (0 if the section states none.)
      `the term is less a day` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is that term written "less a day" (for example two years less a day)?
      `the intent of an assault with intent was to commit one of the listed sexual offences` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the offence is an assault with intent under subsection 246(1) of the 1970 Code, was the intent to commit one of the offences in sections 144 to 157?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 487.04, is the offence a primary designated offence` MEANS
    `s 487.04 the offence is a primary designated offence` (`s 487.04 seat: facts` `the section number of the offence` `the subsection of the offence` `where the offence provision is found` `how I am charged` `the purpose` `the offence may be prosecuted by indictment` `the offence is prosecuted by indictment` `the offence carries imprisonment for life` `the longest term of imprisonment, in years` `the term is less a day` `the intent of an assault with intent was to commit one of the listed sexual offences`)

-- Under section 487.04, is the offence a secondary designated offence.
@ref s 487.04
@export Under section 487.04, is the offence I am charged with a secondary designated offence?
GIVEN `the section number of the offence` IS A NUMBER @desc What is the section number of the offence? (for example 271, or 83.18)
      `the subsection of the offence` IS A STRING @desc Which subsection of that section, as written in the Code, for example "(2)" or "(1)(d)"? (Leave empty if none.)
      `where the offence provision is found` IS A STRING @desc Which text is the offence in? One of: "this Act as it reads now", "this Act before the paragraph came into force", "this Act before July 1, 1990", "this Act before the conveyances amendment", "the 1970 Code before January 4, 1983", "the 1970 Code before January 1, 1988", "the 1970 Code as enacted by chapter 125", "the Foreign Interference and Security of Information Act", "the Cannabis Act", "the Controlled Drugs and Substances Act", "another instrument".
      `how I am charged` IS A STRING @desc How are you charged? One of: "with the offence itself", "with an attempt", "with a conspiracy".
      `the purpose` IS A STRING @desc What is the answer wanted for? One of: "a warrant under subsection 487.05(1)", "an order under section 487.051", "another purpose".
      `the offence may be prosecuted by indictment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc May the offence be prosecuted by indictment?
      `the offence is prosecuted by indictment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it being prosecuted by indictment?
      `the offence carries imprisonment for life` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the offence carry imprisonment for life?
      `the longest term of imprisonment, in years` IS A NUMBER @desc What is the longest term of imprisonment the offence carries, in years? (0 if the section states none.)
      `the term is less a day` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is that term written "less a day" (for example two years less a day)?
      `the intent of an assault with intent was to commit one of the listed sexual offences` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the offence is an assault with intent under subsection 246(1) of the 1970 Code, was the intent to commit one of the offences in sections 144 to 157?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 487.04, is the offence a secondary designated offence` MEANS
    `s 487.04 the offence is a secondary designated offence` (`s 487.04 seat: facts` `the section number of the offence` `the subsection of the offence` `where the offence provision is found` `how I am charged` `the purpose` `the offence may be prosecuted by indictment` `the offence is prosecuted by indictment` `the offence carries imprisonment for life` `the longest term of imprisonment, in years` `the term is less a day` `the intent of an assault with intent was to commit one of the listed sexual offences`)

-- Under section 487.04, is the offence a designated offence.
@ref s 487.04
@export Under section 487.04, is the offence I am charged with a designated offence (a primary or a secondary designated offence)?
GIVEN `the section number of the offence` IS A NUMBER @desc What is the section number of the offence? (for example 271, or 83.18)
      `the subsection of the offence` IS A STRING @desc Which subsection of that section, as written in the Code, for example "(2)" or "(1)(d)"? (Leave empty if none.)
      `where the offence provision is found` IS A STRING @desc Which text is the offence in? One of: "this Act as it reads now", "this Act before the paragraph came into force", "this Act before July 1, 1990", "this Act before the conveyances amendment", "the 1970 Code before January 4, 1983", "the 1970 Code before January 1, 1988", "the 1970 Code as enacted by chapter 125", "the Foreign Interference and Security of Information Act", "the Cannabis Act", "the Controlled Drugs and Substances Act", "another instrument".
      `how I am charged` IS A STRING @desc How are you charged? One of: "with the offence itself", "with an attempt", "with a conspiracy".
      `the purpose` IS A STRING @desc What is the answer wanted for? One of: "a warrant under subsection 487.05(1)", "an order under section 487.051", "another purpose".
      `the offence may be prosecuted by indictment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc May the offence be prosecuted by indictment?
      `the offence is prosecuted by indictment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it being prosecuted by indictment?
      `the offence carries imprisonment for life` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the offence carry imprisonment for life?
      `the longest term of imprisonment, in years` IS A NUMBER @desc What is the longest term of imprisonment the offence carries, in years? (0 if the section states none.)
      `the term is less a day` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is that term written "less a day" (for example two years less a day)?
      `the intent of an assault with intent was to commit one of the listed sexual offences` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the offence is an assault with intent under subsection 246(1) of the 1970 Code, was the intent to commit one of the offences in sections 144 to 157?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 487.04, is the offence a designated offence` MEANS
    `s 487.04 the offence is a designated offence` (`s 487.04 seat: facts` `the section number of the offence` `the subsection of the offence` `where the offence provision is found` `how I am charged` `the purpose` `the offence may be prosecuted by indictment` `the offence is prosecuted by indictment` `the offence carries imprisonment for life` `the longest term of imprisonment, in years` `the term is less a day` `the intent of an assault with intent was to commit one of the listed sexual offences`)

-- ====================================================================================================
-- seat questions from part ccc17 (merged by the lead on 2026-11-12; unchanged text follows)
-- ====================================================================================================
-- ccc17-seat.l4 -- Criminal Code (Canada), R.S.C., 1985, c. C-46, sections 487.05 to 490.019: THE SEAT LAYER (layer 2) over the layer-1 modules of part ccc17.
--
-- SEAT: a person who is, or may be, accused of an offence under the Criminal Code (the accused), asking on facts they can state whether what they did is an offence, what its gravity and penalty
-- are, what defences, rights and procedures apply at each stage from arrest through trial, sentence and appeal, and what follows from a conviction. This part answers, for that person: DNA warrants,
-- orders and samples (ss 487.05-487.0911); other warrants and searches (ss 487.092-488); journalists (ss 488.01-488.02); solicitor-client privilege (s 488.1); seizure and detention of things
-- (ss 489-490.01); and the sex offender information registration order (ss 490.011-490.019). (Assumed, not ruled: Singapore's Penal Code is the model, as the maintainer instructed on 2026-11-09, but this
-- is a plain seat layer; no charge-generator contract.)
--
-- Every question's name begins "Under section N," and asks the question its body answers, in the same direction: a question about a duty or a bar is phrased as the duty or the bar ("must the court ...",
-- "is the officer barred from ..."), a question about a power as the power ("may ..."). Every answer is a MAYBE BOOLEAN (or a MAYBE of a date or a number): see NOTES.md "Reading a result": JUST TRUE is
-- "yes, on the text's own words", JUST FALSE is "no, the text says no in its own words", and NOTHING is "not covered: the text does not say" (never read it as no).
--
-- PLAIN INPUTS. Each question takes one input per fact the person can state, each with an @desc worded as that person would be asked. Judgments the Code hands to a judge or another official
-- ("is satisfied that ...") are asked as facts about what the official has found. Answers that another part computes (ccc16: whether an offence is a designated offence under section 487.04; ccc12:
-- whether a thing is a document) are asked as a yes or no and taken as JUST TRUE or JUST FALSE (the seam inputs of the layer-1 modules, taken here as plain facts: the merged seat layer can replace them
-- by calls). Choices from a list are STRING inputs whose @desc names the allowed values.

§ `Section 487.05 to 487.091 -- DNA warrants, orders, authorizations and samples (ask only if a DNA warrant, order or authorization has been sought, made or served on you)`

@ref s 487.05(1)
@export Under section 487.05(1), may a provincial court judge issue a warrant to take my bodily substances for forensic DNA analysis?
GIVEN `an application has been made to a provincial court judge, without notice to me, in Form 5.01` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has an application been made to a provincial court judge, without notice to you, on Form 5.01?
      `the judge is satisfied by information on oath` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the judge satisfied by information given on oath?
      `the offence alleged against me is a designated offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the offence alleged against you one of the designated offences under section 487.04 (the primary and secondary designated offences)?
      `there are reasonable grounds to believe the offence has been committed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are there reasonable grounds to believe that the designated offence has been committed?
      `a bodily substance was found at one of the four kinds of place` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was a bodily substance found at the place where the offence was committed, on or within the body of the victim, on anything the victim wore or carried, or on or within the body of any person or thing or at any place associated with the offence?
      `there are reasonable grounds to believe the substance was found there` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are there reasonable grounds to believe the bodily substance was found or obtained at such a place?
      `there are reasonable grounds to believe I was a party to the offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are there reasonable grounds to believe you were a party to the offence?
      `there are reasonable grounds to believe DNA analysis will show whether the substance came from me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are there reasonable grounds to believe that forensic DNA analysis of a bodily substance from you will provide evidence about whether the bodily substance found was from you?
      `the judge is satisfied that it is in the best interests of the administration of justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the judge satisfied that it is in the best interests of the administration of justice to issue the warrant?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 487.05(1), may a provincial court judge issue a warrant to take my bodily substances for forensic DNA analysis` IS
    `s 487.05(1) the provincial court judge may issue a warrant in Form 5.02`
        `an application has been made to a provincial court judge, without notice to me, in Form 5.01`
        `the judge is satisfied by information on oath`
        (IF `the offence alleged against me is a designated offence` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE)
        `there are reasonable grounds to believe the offence has been committed`
        (IF `a bodily substance was found at one of the four kinds of place` THEN `s 487.05(1)(b)(i) at the place where the offence was committed` ELSE `s 487.05(1)(b) somewhere else`)
        `there are reasonable grounds to believe the substance was found there`
        `there are reasonable grounds to believe I was a party to the offence`
        `there are reasonable grounds to believe DNA analysis will show whether the substance came from me`
        `the judge is satisfied that it is in the best interests of the administration of justice`

@ref s 487.051(1)
@export Under section 487.051(1), must the court order me to give samples of my bodily substances for DNA analysis, because my offence is a primary designated offence within paragraph (a) or (c.02) of the definition?
GIVEN `how I stand` IS A STRING @desc How do you stand? One of: "convicted", "discharged under section 730", "found guilty under the Youth Criminal Justice Act", "found guilty under the Young Offenders Act", "found not criminally responsible", "none of these"
      `my offence is a primary designated offence within paragraph (a) or (c.02)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc When you are sentenced or discharged, is your offence a primary designated offence within paragraph (a) or (c.02) of the definition in section 487.04?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 487.051(1), must the court order me to give samples of my bodily substances for DNA analysis because my offence is a primary designated offence within paragraph (a) or (c.02)` IS
    `s 487.051(1) the court shall make an order in Form 5.03` `how I stand as the section names it` (IF `my offence is a primary designated offence within paragraph (a) or (c.02)` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE)
    WHERE
        `how I stand as the section names it` MEANS
            BRANCH
                IF `how I stand` EQUALS "convicted" THEN `s 487.051 convicted`
                IF `how I stand` EQUALS "discharged under section 730" THEN `s 487.051 discharged under section 730`
                IF `how I stand` EQUALS "found guilty under the Youth Criminal Justice Act" THEN `s 487.051 found guilty under the Youth Criminal Justice Act`
                IF `how I stand` EQUALS "found guilty under the Young Offenders Act" THEN `s 487.051 found guilty under the Young Offenders Act`
                IF `how I stand` EQUALS "found not criminally responsible" THEN `s 487.051 found not criminally responsible on account of mental disorder`
                OTHERWISE `s 487.051 none of those`

@ref s 487.051(2)
@export Under section 487.051(2), must the court order me to give samples of my bodily substances for DNA analysis, because my offence is a primary designated offence within the other paragraphs, unless I show that the order would be grossly disproportionate?
GIVEN `how I stand` IS A STRING @desc How do you stand? One of: "convicted", "discharged under section 730", "found guilty under the Youth Criminal Justice Act", "found guilty under the Young Offenders Act", "found not criminally responsible", "none of these"
      `my offence is a primary designated offence within the other paragraphs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc When you are sentenced or discharged, is your offence a primary designated offence within any of paragraphs (a.1) to (c.01) and (c.03) to (d) of the definition in section 487.04?
      `the court is satisfied I have established gross disproportion` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the court satisfied that you have established that the impact of such an order on your privacy and security of the person would be grossly disproportionate to the public interest in the protection of society and the proper administration of justice?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 487.051(2), must the court order me to give samples of my bodily substances for DNA analysis because my offence is a primary designated offence within the other paragraphs` IS
    `s 487.051(2) the court shall make an order in Form 5.03` `how I stand as the section names it` (IF `my offence is a primary designated offence within the other paragraphs` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE) `the court is satisfied I have established gross disproportion`
    WHERE
        `how I stand as the section names it` MEANS
            BRANCH
                IF `how I stand` EQUALS "convicted" THEN `s 487.051 convicted`
                IF `how I stand` EQUALS "discharged under section 730" THEN `s 487.051 discharged under section 730`
                IF `how I stand` EQUALS "found guilty under the Youth Criminal Justice Act" THEN `s 487.051 found guilty under the Youth Criminal Justice Act`
                IF `how I stand` EQUALS "found guilty under the Young Offenders Act" THEN `s 487.051 found guilty under the Young Offenders Act`
                IF `how I stand` EQUALS "found not criminally responsible" THEN `s 487.051 found not criminally responsible on account of mental disorder`
                OTHERWISE `s 487.051 none of those`

@ref s 487.051(3)
@export Under section 487.051(3), may the court order me to give samples of my bodily substances for DNA analysis?
GIVEN `the prosecutor has applied for the order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the prosecutor applied for the order?
      `the court is satisfied that it is in the best interests of the administration of justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the court satisfied that it is in the best interests of the administration of justice to make the order?
      `how I stand` IS A STRING @desc How do you stand? One of: "convicted", "discharged under section 730", "found guilty under the Youth Criminal Justice Act", "found guilty under the Young Offenders Act", "found not criminally responsible", "none of these"
      `my offence is a designated offence when the finding was made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you were found not criminally responsible: is the offence a designated offence (primary or secondary) when the finding was made?
      `my offence is a secondary designated offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you were convicted, discharged or found guilty: is your offence a secondary designated offence under section 487.04 when you are sentenced or discharged?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 487.051(3), may the court order me to give samples of my bodily substances for DNA analysis` IS
    `s 487.051(3) the court may make an order in Form 5.04` `the prosecutor has applied for the order` `the court is satisfied that it is in the best interests of the administration of justice` `how I stand as the section names it` (IF `my offence is a designated offence when the finding was made` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE) (IF `my offence is a secondary designated offence` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE)
    WHERE
        `how I stand as the section names it` MEANS
            BRANCH
                IF `how I stand` EQUALS "convicted" THEN `s 487.051 convicted`
                IF `how I stand` EQUALS "discharged under section 730" THEN `s 487.051 discharged under section 730`
                IF `how I stand` EQUALS "found guilty under the Youth Criminal Justice Act" THEN `s 487.051 found guilty under the Youth Criminal Justice Act`
                IF `how I stand` EQUALS "found guilty under the Young Offenders Act" THEN `s 487.051 found guilty under the Young Offenders Act`
                IF `how I stand` EQUALS "found not criminally responsible" THEN `s 487.051 found not criminally responsible on account of mental disorder`
                OTHERWISE `s 487.051 none of those`

@ref s 487.054
@export Under section 487.054, may I, as the offender, appeal from a decision of the court under subsection 487.051(1), (2) or (3)?
GIVEN `the decision is one the court made under subsection 487.051(1), (2) or (3)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the decision you want to appeal one the court made under subsection 487.051(1), (2) or (3) (the orders for samples of your bodily substances)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 487.054, may I as the offender appeal from a decision of the court under subsection 487.051(1), (2) or (3)` IS
    `s 487.054 the person may appeal from the decision of the court` `s 487.054 the offender` (IF `the decision is one the court made under subsection 487.051(1), (2) or (3)` THEN `s 487.054 a decision under subsection 487.051(1)` ELSE `s 487.054 another decision`)

@ref s 487.055(1)
@export Under section 487.055(1), may a provincial court judge authorize the taking of samples of my bodily substances for DNA analysis because, before June 30, 2000, I had been declared a dangerous offender, convicted of murder, or convicted of another listed offence for which I am serving a sentence?
GIVEN `the application is made without notice to me in Form 5.05` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has an application been made to a provincial court judge, without notice to you, on Form 5.05?
      `the event happened before June 30, 2000` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the declaration or the conviction happen before June 30, 2000?
      `what I had been declared or convicted of` IS A STRING @desc One of: "dangerous offender" (declared under Part XXIV), "old dangerous offender" (declared under Part XXI of the 1970 Code, as it read before January 1, 1988), "murder", "attempted murder" (or conspiracy to commit murder), "sexual offence", "manslaughter", "none of these"
      `my offence is a sexual offence within section 487.055(3)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you were convicted of a sexual offence: is it one of the offences listed in subsection 487.055(3)?
      `I am serving a sentence of imprisonment for that offence on the date of the application` IS A BOOLEAN @desc On the date of the application, are you serving a sentence of imprisonment for that offence?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 487.055(1), may a provincial court judge authorize the taking of samples of my bodily substances for DNA analysis` IS
    `s 487.055(1) the provincial court judge may authorize in Form 5.06 the taking of samples of bodily substances` `the application is made without notice to me in Form 5.05` `the event happened before June 30, 2000` `my basis as the section names it` (IF `my offence is a sexual offence within section 487.055(3)` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE) `I am serving a sentence of imprisonment for that offence on the date of the application`
    WHERE
        `my basis as the section names it` MEANS
            BRANCH
                IF `what I had been declared or convicted of` EQUALS "dangerous offender" THEN `s 487.055(1)(a) had been declared a dangerous offender under Part XXIV`
                IF `what I had been declared or convicted of` EQUALS "old dangerous offender" THEN `s 487.055(1)(b) had been declared a dangerous offender or a dangerous sexual offender under Part XXI of the Criminal Code, chapter C-34 of the Revised Statutes of Canada, 1970, as it read from time to time before January 1, 1988`
                IF `what I had been declared or convicted of` EQUALS "murder" THEN `s 487.055(1)(c) had been convicted of murder`
                IF `what I had been declared or convicted of` EQUALS "attempted murder" THEN `s 487.055(1)(c.1) had been convicted of attempted murder or conspiracy to commit murder or to cause another person to be murdered`
                IF `what I had been declared or convicted of` EQUALS "sexual offence" THEN `s 487.055(1)(d) had been convicted of a sexual offence within the meaning of subsection (3)`
                IF `what I had been declared or convicted of` EQUALS "manslaughter" THEN `s 487.055(1)(e) had been convicted of manslaughter`
                OTHERWISE `s 487.055(1) none of those`

@ref s 487.0552(1)
@export Under section 487.0552(1), am I guilty of an offence because I failed, without reasonable excuse, to comply with an order or summons to report and give DNA samples?
GIVEN `the document is one of the orders or summonses the section names` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the document an order to report under subsection 487.051(4) or 487.055(3.11) (or section 196.14(4) or 196.24(4) of the National Defence Act), or a summons under subsection 487.055(4) or 487.091(3)?
      `I failed to comply with it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you fail to comply with it (for example, fail to report at the place, day and time)?
      `I had a reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you have a reasonable excuse for failing to comply?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 487.0552(1), am I guilty of an offence for failing to comply with an order or summons to report and give DNA samples` IS
    `s 487.0552(1) the person is guilty of an offence` (IF `the document is one of the orders or summonses the section names` THEN `s 487.0552(1) an order made under subsection 487.051(4) of this Act` ELSE `s 487.0552(1) another document`) `I failed to comply with it` `I had a reasonable excuse`

@ref s 487.0552(1)
@export Under section 487.0552(1), what is the longest term of imprisonment, in years, that I can be given if I am prosecuted by indictment for failing to comply with an order or summons to report and give DNA samples?
GIVEN `I am prosecuted by indictment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you being prosecuted by indictment (rather than on summary conviction)?
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
DECIDE `Under section 487.0552(1), what is the longest term of imprisonment in years if I am prosecuted by indictment for failing to comply` IS
    IF `I am prosecuted by indictment`
    THEN CONSIDER `s 487.0552(1) the punishments`
         WHEN first FOLLOWED BY rest THEN `the years of the longest term` first
         OTHERWISE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the list of punishments has no imprisonment term first
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the summary-conviction limb states no penalty itself
    WHERE
        `the years of the longest term` p MEANS
            CONSIDER p's `maximum imprisonment`
            WHEN `imprisonment for a term of` t THEN JUST (t's years)
            OTHERWISE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the first punishment is not a term of imprisonment

@ref s 487.07(1)
@export Under section 487.07(1), must the peace officer tell me, before taking my samples, that the results of forensic DNA analysis may be used in evidence?
GIVEN `the peace officer is about to take samples of my bodily substances under a DNA warrant, order or authorization` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is a peace officer about to take samples of your bodily substances (or have them taken) under a warrant under section 487.05, an order under section 487.051 or an authorization under section 487.055 or 487.091?
      `the samples are taken in execution of a warrant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are the samples taken under a DNA warrant (section 487.05), rather than under an order or an authorization?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 487.07(1), must the peace officer tell me before taking my samples that the results of forensic DNA analysis may be used in evidence` IS
    `s 487.07(1) the peace officer shall inform the person of the matter before taking the samples` `s 487.07(1)(e)(i) the possibility that the results of forensic DNA analysis may be used in evidence` `the peace officer is about to take samples of my bodily substances under a DNA warrant, order or authorization` `the samples are taken in execution of a warrant` FALSE

@ref s 487.07(2)
@export Under section 487.07(2), may I be detained for a reasonable period so that samples of my bodily substances can be taken?
GIVEN `samples of my bodily substances are to be taken` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are samples of your bodily substances to be taken from you under a DNA warrant, order or authorization?
      `the period of detention is reasonable in the circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the period for which you are detained reasonable in the circumstances?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 487.07(2), may I be detained for a reasonable period so that samples of my bodily substances can be taken` IS
    `s 487.07(2)(a) the person may be detained for the purpose of taking the samples` `samples of my bodily substances are to be taken` `the period of detention is reasonable in the circumstances`

@ref s 487.071(2)
@export Under section 487.071(2), is the peace officer barred from taking bodily substances from me because my DNA profile is already in the convicted offenders index of the national DNA data bank?
GIVEN `samples are to be taken under a DNA order or authorization` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are samples to be taken from you under an order under section 487.051 or an authorization under section 487.055 or 487.091?
      `my DNA profile is in the convicted offenders index` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your DNA profile already in the convicted offenders index of the national DNA data bank?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 487.071(2), is the peace officer barred from taking bodily substances from me because my DNA profile is already in the data bank` IS
    `s 487.071(2) the peace officer shall not take any bodily substances from the person` `samples are to be taken under a DNA order or authorization` `my DNA profile is in the convicted offenders index`

@ref s 487.09(1)
@export Under section 487.09(1), must my bodily substances, taken under a DNA warrant, and the results of the analysis be destroyed without delay?
GIVEN `the bodily substances were taken from me under a DNA warrant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were the bodily substances taken from you in execution of a warrant under section 487.05?
      `a judge has ordered that they not be destroyed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a provincial court judge ordered under subsection 487.09(2) that they not be destroyed?
      `the results show the bodily substance found was not from me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do the results of the analysis establish that the bodily substance found was not from you?
      `I have been finally acquitted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you been finally acquitted of the designated offence and any other offence in respect of the same transaction?
      `one year has passed since I was discharged after a preliminary inquiry, the charge was dismissed or withdrawn, or the proceedings were stayed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has one year passed since you were discharged after a preliminary inquiry, the charge was dismissed (other than by acquittal) or withdrawn, or the proceeding was stayed under section 579, 572 or 795?
      `during that year a new information was laid, an indictment preferred or the proceeding recommenced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc During that year, was a new information laid, an indictment preferred or the proceeding recommenced?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 487.09(1), must my bodily substances taken under a DNA warrant and the results of the analysis be destroyed without delay` IS
    `s 487.09(1) the bodily substances and the results of forensic DNA analysis shall be destroyed without delay` `the bodily substances were taken from me under a DNA warrant` `a judge has ordered that they not be destroyed` `my ground` `one year has passed since I was discharged after a preliminary inquiry, the charge was dismissed or withdrawn, or the proceedings were stayed` `during that year a new information was laid, an indictment preferred or the proceeding recommenced`
    WHERE
        `my ground` MEANS
            BRANCH
                IF `the results show the bodily substance found was not from me` THEN `s 487.09(1)(a) the results of the analysis establish that the bodily substance referred to in paragraph 487.05(1)(b) was not from that person`
                IF `I have been finally acquitted` THEN `s 487.09(1)(b) the person is finally acquitted of the designated offence and any other offence in respect of the same transaction`
                IF `one year has passed since I was discharged after a preliminary inquiry, the charge was dismissed or withdrawn, or the proceedings were stayed` THEN `s 487.09(1)(c)(i) one year after the person is discharged after a preliminary inquiry into the designated offence or any other offence in respect of the same transaction`
                OTHERWISE `s 487.09(1) none of those`

@ref s 487.09(1)(c)
@export Under section 487.09(1)(c), on what day does the one year end after which my bodily substances must be destroyed, counted from the day I was discharged after a preliminary inquiry, the charge was dismissed or withdrawn, or the proceedings were stayed?
GIVEN `the day of the discharge, the dismissal or withdrawal, or the stay` IS A DATE @desc On what day were you discharged after a preliminary inquiry, was the charge dismissed (other than by acquittal) or withdrawn, or were the proceedings stayed?
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `Under section 487.09(1)(c), on what day does the one year end after which my bodily substances must be destroyed` IS
    `s 487.09(1)(c) the day on which one year after the event expires` `the day of the discharge, the dismissal or withdrawal, or the stay`

@ref s 487.091(1)
@export Under section 487.091(1), may a provincial court judge authorize the taking of additional samples of my bodily substances because a DNA profile could not be derived or the information was lost?
GIVEN `the application is made without notice to me in Form 5.08` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has an application been made to a provincial court judge, without notice to you, on Form 5.08?
      `a DNA profile cannot be derived from the samples already taken` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can a DNA profile not be derived from the bodily substances already taken from you under an order or authorization?
      `the information or bodily substances required by the regulations were not transmitted properly or were lost` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were the information or bodily substances required by the regulations under the DNA Identification Act not transmitted as the regulations require, or lost?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 487.091(1), may a provincial court judge authorize the taking of additional samples of my bodily substances` IS
    `s 487.091(1) the provincial court judge may authorize in Form 5.09 the taking of additional samples of bodily substances` `the application is made without notice to me in Form 5.08` `a DNA profile cannot be derived from the samples already taken` `the information or bodily substances required by the regulations were not transmitted properly or were lost`

§ `Section 487.092 to 488 -- other warrants, telecommunication, searches without a warrant, publication, sealing, time of execution (ask only if a warrant or search is in issue)`

@ref s 487.092(1)
@export Under section 487.092(1), may a justice issue a warrant to obtain a print or impression of my body, such as a fingerprint or footprint?
GIVEN `the justice is satisfied by information on oath in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the justice satisfied by information on oath in writing that there are reasonable grounds to believe an offence against an Act of Parliament has been committed and that information about it will be obtained by the print or impression?
      `the justice is satisfied that it is in the best interests of the administration of justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the justice satisfied that it is in the best interests of the administration of justice to issue the warrant?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 487.092(1), may a justice issue a warrant to obtain a print or impression of my body` IS
    `s 487.092(1) the justice may issue a warrant in writing authorizing a peace officer to obtain a print or impression of the body of the person` `the justice is satisfied by information on oath in writing` `the justice is satisfied that it is in the best interests of the administration of justice`

@ref s 487.093(1)
@export Under section 487.093(1), must the person executing a search warrant give me a copy of the warrant and a notice in Form 5.1, or leave them in a prominent place?
GIVEN `the section number under which the warrant was issued` IS A NUMBER @desc Under which section of the Criminal Code was the warrant issued (for example 487 or 395 or 199 or 117.04)?
      `the subsection under which the warrant was issued` IS A STRING @desc Under which subsection (for example "(1)" or "(5)" or "(6)")?
      `a person is present and in control of the place` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is a person present and ostensibly in control of the building, receptacle or place to be searched?
      `the warrant is for the search of a person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it a warrant under subsection 395(1) for the search of a person?
      `the warrant authorizes the search of something already lawfully seized and detained` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the warrant authorize the search of something that is detained under the Act after it was lawfully seized?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 487.093(1), must the person executing a search warrant give me a copy of the warrant and a notice in Form 5.1, or affix them` IS
    CONSIDER `s 487.093(1) what the person executing the warrant shall do during the execution` (`Provision of the Code` OF `the section number under which the warrant was issued`, `the subsection under which the warrant was issued`) `a person is present and in control of the place` `the warrant is for the search of a person` `the warrant authorizes the search of something already lawfully seized and detained`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the warrant is not one the subsection names
    WHEN JUST `s 487.093(2) subsection (1) does not apply because the warrant authorizes the search of a thing detained after it was lawfully seized` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Subsection (1) does not apply if the warrant authorizes the search of anything that is detained under this Act after it has been lawfully seized."
    OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

@ref s 487.1(5)
@export Under section 487.1(5), may the police submit their application for a warrant to a judge or justice by a means of telecommunication that does not produce a writing, such as a telephone call?
GIVEN `it would be impracticable to submit it by a means of telecommunication that produces a writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Would it be impracticable in the circumstances to submit the application by a means of telecommunication that produces a writing (such as a fax or email)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 487.1(5), may the police submit their application by a means of telecommunication that does not produce a writing` IS
    `s 487.1(5) the applicant may submit the application by a means of telecommunication that does not produce a writing` `it would be impracticable to submit it by a means of telecommunication that produces a writing`

@ref s 487.11
@export Under section 487.11, may a peace officer or public officer search, or seize under section 487(1) or 492.1(1), without a warrant because it is impracticable to obtain one in exigent circumstances?
GIVEN `the person is a peace officer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the person who searched a peace officer?
      `the person is a public officer appointed or designated to enforce a law, including an Act of Parliament` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the person a public officer appointed or designated to administer or enforce a federal or provincial law, whose duties include the enforcement of this or any other Act of Parliament?
      `the person acted in the course of their duties` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the person act in the course of their duties?
      `the conditions for obtaining a warrant existed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the conditions for obtaining a warrant exist?
      `exigent circumstances made it impracticable to obtain a warrant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Because of exigent circumstances, was it impracticable to obtain a warrant?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 487.11, may a peace officer or public officer search or seize without a warrant in exigent circumstances` IS
    `s 487.11 the officer may exercise the powers described in subsection 487(1) or 492.1(1) without a warrant` (IF `the person is a peace officer` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE) `the person is a public officer appointed or designated to enforce a law, including an Act of Parliament` `the person acted in the course of their duties` `the conditions for obtaining a warrant existed` `exigent circumstances made it impracticable to obtain a warrant`

@ref s 487.2
@export Under section 487.2, is it an offence to publish or broadcast the location of the place searched under a search warrant, or the identity of a person who occupies it or is suspected, without the consent of every person referred to in paragraph 487.2(b), before a charge is laid?
GIVEN `a search warrant was issued under section 487 or a search made under one` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was a search warrant issued under section 487, or a search made under such a warrant?
      `the person publishes, broadcasts or transmits the information` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does someone publish in a document, broadcast or transmit the information?
      `the information is the location of the place or the identity of a person who occupies it or is suspected` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the information the location of the place searched or to be searched, or the identity of a person who occupies it, is in possession or control of it, or is suspected of an offence in relation to which the warrant was issued?
      `every person referred to in paragraph (b) consents` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does every such person (including you) consent to the publication?
      `a charge has been laid in respect of an offence in relation to which the warrant was issued` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a charge been laid in respect of any offence in relation to which the warrant was issued?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 487.2, is it an offence to publish the location of the place searched or the identity of a person without consent before a charge is laid` IS
    `s 487.2 the person is guilty of an offence punishable on summary conviction` `a search warrant was issued under section 487 or a search made under one` `the person publishes, broadcasts or transmits the information` `the information is the location of the place or the identity of a person who occupies it or is suspected` `the information is the location of the place or the identity of a person who occupies it or is suspected` `every person referred to in paragraph (b) consents` `a charge has been laid in respect of an offence in relation to which the warrant was issued`

@ref s 487.3(1)
@export Under section 487.3(1), may a justice or judge order that access to, and disclosure of, information about the warrant, order or authorization be prohibited?
GIVEN `the person who makes the order` IS A STRING @desc Who makes the order? One of: "justice", "judge of a superior court of criminal jurisdiction", "judge of the Court of Quebec", "someone else"
      `the application is made with or after the application for the warrant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the application made at the time of the application for the warrant, order or authorization, or at a later time?
      `the ends of justice would be subverted by disclosure or the information might be used for an improper purpose` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Would the ends of justice be subverted by disclosure (for one of the reasons in subsection 487.3(2)), or might the information be used for an improper purpose?
      `that reason outweighs in importance the access to the information` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does that reason outweigh in importance the access to the information?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 487.3(1), may a justice or judge prohibit access to, and disclosure of, information about the warrant, order or authorization` IS
    `s 487.3(1) the justice or judge may make an order prohibiting access to, and the disclosure of, any information relating to the warrant, order or authorization` `who makes it` `the application is made with or after the application for the warrant` `the ends of justice would be subverted by disclosure or the information might be used for an improper purpose` `that reason outweighs in importance the access to the information`
    WHERE
        `who makes it` MEANS
            BRANCH
                IF `the person who makes the order` EQUALS "justice" THEN `s 487.3(1) a justice`
                IF `the person who makes the order` EQUALS "judge of a superior court of criminal jurisdiction" THEN `s 487.3(1) a judge of a superior court of criminal jurisdiction`
                IF `the person who makes the order` EQUALS "judge of the Court of Quebec" THEN `s 487.3(1) a judge of the Court of Quebec`
                OTHERWISE `s 487.3(1) someone else`

@ref s 488
@export Under section 488, may a search warrant issued under section 487 be executed at this time of day?
GIVEN `the time of day, in minutes after midnight` IS A NUMBER @desc At what time was, or will, the warrant be executed, in minutes after midnight (for example 11 pm is 1380)?
      `the justice was satisfied there are reasonable grounds to execute it by night` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the justice satisfied that there are reasonable grounds for the warrant to be executed by night?
      `the reasonable grounds are included in the information` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are the reasonable grounds included in the information?
      `the warrant authorizes execution by night` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the warrant itself authorize that it be executed by night?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 488, may a search warrant issued under section 487 be executed at this time of day` IS
    `s 488 the warrant may be executed at the time of day` `the time of day, in minutes after midnight` `the justice was satisfied there are reasonable grounds to execute it by night` `the reasonable grounds are included in the information` `the warrant authorizes execution by night`

§ `Section 488.01 and 488.02 -- journalists (ask only if you are a journalist, or the application concerns a journalist's communications or sources)`

@ref s 488.01(2)
@export Under section 488.01(2), must the police apply to a judge of a superior court of criminal jurisdiction (or a judge as defined in section 552) for a warrant, authorization or order that relates to a journalist?
GIVEN `the applicant knows the application relates to a journalist's communications or to material in a journalist's possession` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the applicant know that the application relates to a journalist's communications, or to an object, document or data relating to or in the possession of a journalist?
      `the section number of the warrant, authorization or order` IS A NUMBER @desc Under which section of the Criminal Code is the warrant, authorization or order sought (for example 487.01, 492.1, 184.2, 186, 188 or 487.015)?
      `it is a search warrant under the Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it a search warrant under the Criminal Code (for example under section 487)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 488.01(2), must the police apply to a superior court judge for a warrant, authorization or order that relates to a journalist` IS
    `s 488.01(2) the applicant shall make the application to a judge of a superior court of criminal jurisdiction or a judge as defined in section 552` `the applicant knows the application relates to a journalist's communications or to material in a journalist's possession` `the section number of the warrant, authorization or order` `it is a search warrant under the Act`

@ref s 488.01(3)
@export Under section 488.01(3), may the judge issue a warrant, authorization or order that relates to my communications as a journalist, only if there is no other reasonable way to get the information and the public interest outweighs my right to privacy?
GIVEN `the usual conditions for the issue are met` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are the conditions required for the issue of the warrant, authorization or order met?
      `the judge is satisfied there is no other way to get the information reasonably` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the judge satisfied that there is no other way by which the information can reasonably be obtained?
      `the judge is satisfied the public interest outweighs the journalist's right to privacy` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the judge satisfied that the public interest in the investigation and prosecution of a criminal offence outweighs the journalist's right to privacy in gathering and disseminating information?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 488.01(3), may the judge issue the warrant, authorization or order that relates to a journalist` IS
    `s 488.01(3) the judge may issue the warrant, authorization or order` `the usual conditions for the issue are met` `the judge is satisfied there is no other way to get the information reasonably` `the judge is satisfied the public interest outweighs the journalist's right to privacy`

@ref s 488.02(3)
@export Under section 488.02(3), may I, as a journalist, still apply within 10 days of receiving the officer's notice for an order that a sealed document not be disclosed to an officer?
GIVEN `I am the journalist or the relevant media outlet` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you the journalist, or the media outlet, who received the notice of the officer's intention to examine or reproduce the document?
      `my application is on the ground that the document identifies or is likely to identify a journalistic source` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your application on the ground that the document identifies or is likely to identify a journalistic source?
      `the day I received the notice` IS A DATE @desc On what day did you receive the notice?
      `the day I apply` IS A DATE @desc On what day do you apply?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 488.02(3), may I as a journalist still apply within 10 days of receiving the officer's notice for an order that the document not be disclosed` IS
    `s 488.02(3) the journalist or media outlet may apply to a judge of the court that issued the warrant, authorization or order for an order that the document is not to be disclosed to an officer` (IF `I am the journalist or the relevant media outlet` THEN `s 488.02(3) the journalist` ELSE `s 488.02(3) someone else`) `my application is on the ground that the document identifies or is likely to identify a journalistic source` `the day I received the notice` `the day I apply`

@ref s 488.02(3)
@export Under section 488.02(3), what is the last day on which I may apply to a judge for an order that a sealed document not be disclosed to an officer, counting 10 days from the day I received the notice?
GIVEN `the day I received the notice` IS A DATE @desc On what day did you receive the officer's notice of intention to examine or reproduce the document?
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `Under section 488.02(3), what is the last day on which I may apply for an order that the document not be disclosed` IS
    `s 488.02(3) the last day on which the application may be made` `the day I received the notice`

§ `Section 488.1 -- solicitor-client privilege (ask only if a document in a lawyer's possession is being searched or seized and privilege is claimed)`

@ref s 488.1(2)
@export Under section 488.1(2), must an officer, without examining or copying the document, seize it and place it in a sealed and identified package, because the lawyer claims solicitor-client privilege for it?
GIVEN `an officer is about to examine, copy or seize a document` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is an officer, acting under an Act of Parliament, about to examine, copy or seize a document?
      `the document is in a lawyer's possession` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the document in the possession of a lawyer?
      `the lawyer claims a named client has solicitor-client privilege` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the lawyer claim that a named client has solicitor-client privilege in respect of the document?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 488.1(2), must the officer, without examining or copying the document, seize it and place it in a sealed and identified package when the lawyer claims solicitor-client privilege` IS
    `s 488.1(2) the officer shall, without examining or making copies of the document, seize it and place it in a package, suitably sealed and identified` `an officer is about to examine, copy or seize a document` `the document is in a lawyer's possession` `the lawyer claims a named client has solicitor-client privilege`

@ref s 488.1(3)(a)
@export Under section 488.1(3)(a), may I, as the client (or my lawyer for me), still apply to a judge within 14 days from the day the document was placed in custody, on two days notice of motion?
GIVEN `I am the client, my lawyer acting for me, or the Attorney General` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you the client, the lawyer acting on behalf of the client, or the Attorney General?
      `the document was seized and placed in custody under subsection 488.1(2)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the document seized and placed in custody under subsection 488.1(2)?
      `the day the document was placed in custody` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the document placed in custody?
      `the day of the application` IS A DATE @desc On what day do you apply?
      `the days of notice of motion given to all other persons entitled to apply` IS A NUMBER @desc How many days of notice of motion have you given to all other persons entitled to make an application?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 488.1(3)(a), may the client or the lawyer for the client still apply to a judge within 14 days of the document being placed in custody` IS
    `s 488.1(3)(a) the person may apply to a judge for an order appointing a place and a day for the determination of the question and requiring the custodian to produce the document` (IF `I am the client, my lawyer acting for me, or the Attorney General` THEN `s 488.1(3) the client` ELSE `s 488.1(3) someone else`) `the document was seized and placed in custody under subsection 488.1(2)` `the day the document was placed in custody` `the day of the application` `the days of notice of motion given to all other persons entitled to apply`

@ref s 488.1(3)(a)
@export Under section 488.1(3)(a), what is the last day on which I may apply to a judge, counting 14 days from the day the document was placed in custody?
GIVEN `the day the document was placed in custody` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the document placed in custody?
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `Under section 488.1(3)(a), what is the last day on which I may apply to a judge about the document in custody` IS
    `s 488.1(3)(a) the last day to apply to a judge` `the day the document was placed in custody`

@ref s 488.1(5)
@export Under section 488.1(5), does a document that the judge has determined to be privileged remain privileged and inadmissible as evidence?
GIVEN `the judge determined under paragraph 488.1(4)(d) that solicitor-client privilege exists` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the judge determine under paragraph 488.1(4)(d) that a solicitor-client privilege exists in respect of the document?
      `the client consents to its admission in evidence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the client consent to its admission in evidence?
      `the privilege is otherwise lost` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the privilege otherwise lost?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 488.1(5), does the document that the judge determined to be privileged remain privileged and inadmissible as evidence` IS
    `s 488.1(5) the document remains privileged and inadmissible as evidence` `the judge determined under paragraph 488.1(4)(d) that solicitor-client privilege exists` `the client consents to its admission in evidence` `the privilege is otherwise lost`

@ref s 488.1(8)
@export Under section 488.1(8), is an officer barred from examining, copying or seizing a document without giving a reasonable opportunity for a claim of solicitor-client privilege to be made?
GIVEN `the officer gave a reasonable opportunity for a claim of privilege to be made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the officer give a reasonable opportunity for a claim of solicitor-client privilege to be made before examining, copying or seizing the document?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 488.1(8), is an officer barred from examining, copying or seizing a document without giving a reasonable opportunity for a claim of solicitor-client privilege` IS
    `s 488.1(8) the officer is barred from examining, making copies of or seizing the document` `the officer gave a reasonable opportunity for a claim of privilege to be made`

§ `Section 489 to 490.01 -- seizure, return and detention of things seized (ask only if something of yours has been seized)`

@ref s 489(1)
@export Under section 489(1), may a person executing a search warrant seize things that the warrant does not mention?
GIVEN `the person is executing a warrant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the person who seized the thing executing a warrant?
      `the person believes on reasonable grounds the thing was obtained by an offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the person believe on reasonable grounds that the thing was obtained by the commission of an offence against an Act of Parliament?
      `the person believes on reasonable grounds the thing was used in an offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the person believe on reasonable grounds that the thing was used in the commission of an offence against an Act of Parliament?
      `the person believes on reasonable grounds the thing will afford evidence of an offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the person believe on reasonable grounds that the thing will afford evidence in respect of an offence against an Act of Parliament?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 489(1), may a person executing a search warrant seize things that the warrant does not mention` IS
    `s 489(1) the person who executes a warrant may seize the thing in addition to the things mentioned in the warrant` `the person is executing a warrant` `the person believes on reasonable grounds the thing was obtained by an offence` `the person believes on reasonable grounds the thing was used in an offence` `the person believes on reasonable grounds the thing will afford evidence of an offence`

@ref s 489.1(1)
@export Under section 489.1(1), must the peace officer return the thing seized, on being issued a receipt, to the person lawfully entitled to its possession, because there is no dispute about who is entitled to it and it is not needed for any investigation or proceeding?
GIVEN `a peace officer seized the thing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did a peace officer seize the thing under a warrant, under section 487.11 or 489, or otherwise in the execution of duties?
      `the officer is satisfied there is no dispute about who is lawfully entitled to it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the officer satisfied that there is no dispute as to who is lawfully entitled to possession of the thing?
      `the officer is satisfied its continued detention is not required` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the officer satisfied that its continued detention is not required for any investigation or for a preliminary inquiry, trial or other proceeding?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 489.1(1), must the peace officer return the thing seized, on being issued a receipt, to the person lawfully entitled to its possession` IS
    CONSIDER `s 489.1(1) what the peace officer shall do as soon as is practicable` `a peace officer seized the thing` `the officer is satisfied there is no dispute about who is lawfully entitled to it` `the officer is satisfied its continued detention is not required`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording)
    WHEN JUST `s 489.1(1)(a) return the thing seized, on being issued a receipt for it, to the person lawfully entitled to its possession and report to a justice having jurisdiction in respect of the matter` THEN JUST TRUE
    OTHERWISE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if the peace officer is not satisfied as described in subparagraphs (a)(i) and (ii)"

@ref s 490(1)
@export Under section 490(1), must the justice order the thing seized returned to the lawful owner or the person lawfully entitled to possession, unless the prosecutor or the officer satisfies the justice that its detention is required?
GIVEN `the thing was brought before a justice or a report was made to a justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the thing brought before a justice, or a report in respect of it made to a justice, under paragraph 489.1(1)(b) or subsection 489.1(2)?
      `the lawful owner or the person entitled to possession is known` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the lawful owner or person lawfully entitled to possession known?
      `the prosecutor or the officer has satisfied the justice that detention is required` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the prosecutor, or the peace officer or person having custody, satisfied the justice that the detention of the thing is required for any investigation or a preliminary inquiry, trial or other proceeding?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490(1), must the justice order the thing seized returned to the lawful owner unless detention is shown to be required` IS
    CONSIDER `s 490(1) the order the justice shall make` `the thing was brought before a justice or a report was made to a justice` `the lawful owner or the person entitled to possession is known` `the prosecutor or the officer has satisfied the justice that detention is required`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (silent)
    WHEN JUST `s 490(1)(a) order the thing seized to be returned to the lawful owner or the person lawfully entitled to possession` THEN JUST TRUE
    OTHERWISE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "unless the prosecutor, or the peace officer or other person having custody of the thing seized, satisfies the justice that the detention of the thing seized is required"

@ref s 490(2)
@export Under section 490(2), is the detention of the thing seized from me barred, because more than 180 days have passed since the day of the seizure and no extension has been ordered and no proceedings have been started?
GIVEN `the day of the seizure` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the thing seized?
      `the day I am asking about` IS A DATE @desc For what day are you asking whether the thing may still be detained?
      `an application for further detention has been made and is not yet decided` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has an application for an order for further detention under paragraph 490(2)(a) been made and not yet decided?
      `a justice has ordered further detention and that period has not ended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a justice ordered further detention for a specified period, and has that period not ended?
      `proceedings have been instituted in which the thing may be required` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have proceedings been instituted in which the thing detained may be required?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490(2), is the detention of the thing seized barred because more than 180 days have passed since the seizure` IS
    `s 490(2) the section bars detaining the thing for more than 180 days after the day of the seizure` `the day of the seizure` `the day I am asking about` `an application for further detention has been made and is not yet decided` `a justice has ordered further detention and that period has not ended` `proceedings have been instituted in which the thing may be required`

@ref s 490(2)
@export Under section 490(2), what is the last day on which the thing seized from me may be detained without a further order, counting 180 days from the day of the seizure?
GIVEN `the day of the seizure` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the thing seized?
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `Under section 490(2), what is the last day on which the thing seized may be detained without a further order` IS
    `s 490(2) the last day on which the thing may be detained without a further order` `the day of the seizure`

@ref s 490(3)
@export Under section 490(3), is the detention of the thing seized from me barred, because more than one year has passed since the day of the seizure and no judge has ordered further detention and no proceedings have been started?
GIVEN `the day of the seizure` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the thing seized?
      `the day I am asking about` IS A DATE @desc For what day are you asking whether the thing may still be detained?
      `an application for further detention by a judge has been made and is not yet decided` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has an application to a judge for further detention under paragraph 490(3)(a) been made and not yet decided?
      `a judge has ordered further detention and that period has not ended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a judge ordered further detention for a specified period, and has that period not ended?
      `proceedings have been instituted in which the thing may be required` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have proceedings been instituted in which the thing detained may be required?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490(3), is the detention of the thing seized barred because more than one year has passed since the seizure` IS
    `s 490(3) the section bars detaining the thing for more than one year from the day of the seizure` `the day of the seizure` `the day I am asking about` `an application for further detention by a judge has been made and is not yet decided` `a judge has ordered further detention and that period has not ended` `proceedings have been instituted in which the thing may be required`

@ref s 490(7)
@export Under section 490(7), may I, the person from whom the thing was seized, apply summarily to a justice or judge for an order that it be returned to me?
GIVEN `the periods of detention have expired` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have the periods of detention provided for or ordered under subsections 490(1) to (3) expired?
      `the court has allowed me to apply early because hardship will result` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a judge or justice allowed you to apply before the expiration of the periods, under subsection 490(8), because hardship will result?
      `the clear days of notice I gave to the Attorney General` IS A NUMBER @desc How many clear days of notice have you given to the Attorney General?
      `the Attorney General agreed to a shorter notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Attorney General agreed under subsection 490(18) that the application be made before the three days have passed?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490(7), may I apply summarily for an order that the thing seized from me be returned` IS
    `s 490(7) the person from whom anything has been seized may apply summarily for an order under paragraph (9)(c) that the thing be returned` `the periods of detention have expired` `the court has allowed me to apply early because hardship will result` `the clear days of notice I gave to the Attorney General` `the Attorney General agreed to a shorter notice`

@ref s 490(9)
@export Under section 490(9), must the justice or judge order the thing seized returned to me, the person from whom it was seized, because my possession of it was lawful?
GIVEN `the judge or justice is satisfied the periods have expired, or detention is no longer required` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the judge or justice satisfied that the periods of detention have expired and proceedings have not been instituted in which the thing may be required, or that its continued detention will not be required?
      `my possession of the thing was lawful` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was your possession of the thing lawful?
      `my possession of the thing was unlawful` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was your possession of the thing unlawful?
      `it was seized when it was not in anyone's possession` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was it seized when it was not in the possession of any person?
      `the lawful owner is known` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the lawful owner, or the person lawfully entitled to its possession, known?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490(9), must the justice or judge order the thing seized returned to me because my possession of it was lawful` IS
    CONSIDER `s 490(9) the order the judge or justice shall make` `the judge or justice is satisfied the periods have expired, or detention is no longer required` `my possession of the thing was lawful` `my possession of the thing was unlawful` `it was seized when it was not in anyone's possession` `the lawful owner is known`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (silent)
    WHEN JUST `s 490(9)(c) order the thing returned to the person from whom it was seized` THEN JUST TRUE
    OTHERWISE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(c) if possession of it by the person from whom it was seized is lawful"

@ref s 490(12)
@export Under section 490(12), must nothing be returned, forfeited or disposed of while an application or appeal is pending or within 30 days after an order is made?
GIVEN `an application or appeal under section 490 is pending` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is an application made, or an appeal taken, under section 490 in respect of the thing (or the proceeding in which the right of seizure is questioned) still pending?
      `the day an order in respect of the thing was made` IS A DATE @desc On what day was an order in respect of the thing made under section 490?
      `the day I am asking about` IS A DATE @desc For what day are you asking?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490(12), must nothing be returned, forfeited or disposed of while an application or appeal is pending or within 30 days after an order` IS
    `s 490(12) nothing shall be returned, forfeited or disposed of` `an application or appeal under section 490 is pending` `the day an order in respect of the thing was made` `the day I am asking about`

@ref s 490.01
@export Under section 490.01(b), may the person who seized a perishable thing of mine dispose of it or destroy it, with a justice's authorization given on an ex parte application?
GIVEN `the thing is perishable or likely to depreciate rapidly` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the thing seized perishable or likely to depreciate rapidly?
      `the person is the one who seized it or has custody of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the person the one who seized the thing, or another person having custody of it?
      `a justice authorized it on ex parte application` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did a justice authorize it, on an application made without notice?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.01(b), may the person who seized a perishable thing dispose of it or destroy it with a justice's authorization` IS
    `s 490.01(b) the person may dispose of the thing, or destroy it` `the thing is perishable or likely to depreciate rapidly` `the person is the one who seized it or has custody of it` `a justice authorized it on ex parte application`

§ `Section 490.011 to 490.019 -- the sex offender information registration order (ask only if you are convicted of, or found not criminally responsible for, a sexual or related offence)`

@ref s 490.011(1)
@export Under section 490.011(1), is my offence a designated offence (a primary or a secondary offence)?
GIVEN `the section number of my offence` IS A NUMBER @desc What is the section number of the Criminal Code provision of your offence (for example 271 or 151 or 346)?
      `the subsection of my offence` IS A STRING @desc What is the subsection (for example "(2)", "(1)(d)"), or "" if none?
      `which Code the provision is from` IS A STRING @desc One of: "this Act" (the Criminal Code as it reads now), "1970 Code before 1983", "1970 Code as enacted by chapter 125", "1970 Code before 1988", "this Act before December 6, 2014"
      `my offence is an attempt or a conspiracy` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your offence an attempt or conspiracy to commit the offence of that provision?
      `the intent was to commit one of the offences in subparagraphs (b)(i) to (iv)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If it is subsection 246(1) of the 1970 Code (assault with intent): was the intent to commit rape, attempted rape, or indecent assault on a female or a male?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.011(1), is my offence a designated offence, that is a primary or a secondary offence` IS
    `s 490.011(1) designated offence` (`s 490.011(1) offence` OF `my enactment`, (`Provision of the Code` OF `the section number of my offence`, `the subsection of my offence`), `my offence is an attempt or a conspiracy`, `the intent was to commit one of the offences in subparagraphs (b)(i) to (iv)`)
    WHERE
        `my enactment` MEANS
            BRANCH
                IF `which Code the provision is from` EQUALS "1970 Code before 1983" THEN `s 490.011(1) the Criminal Code, chapter C-34 of the Revised Statutes of Canada, 1970, as it read from time to time before January 4, 1983`
                IF `which Code the provision is from` EQUALS "1970 Code as enacted by chapter 125" THEN `s 490.011(1) the Criminal Code, chapter C-34 of the Revised Statutes of Canada, 1970, as enacted by section 19 of chapter 125 of the Statutes of Canada, 1980-81-82-83`
                IF `which Code the provision is from` EQUALS "1970 Code before 1988" THEN `s 490.011(1) the Criminal Code, chapter C-34 of the Revised Statutes of Canada, 1970, as it read from time to time before January 1, 1988`
                IF `which Code the provision is from` EQUALS "this Act before December 6, 2014" THEN `s 490.011(1) this Act, as it read from time to time before December 6, 2014`
                OTHERWISE `s 490.011(1) this Act`

@ref s 490.012(1)
@export Under section 490.012(1), must the court, when it sentences me, order me to comply with the Sex Offender Information Registration Act, because I was prosecuted by indictment, sentenced to two years or more, and the victim was under 18?
GIVEN `the court is sentencing me for a designated offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the court imposing a sentence on you for a designated offence?
      `my offence is a designated offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your offence a designated offence under section 490.011(1)?
      `the order is limited by subsection 490.012(5)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your offence only a secondary offence, with the prosecutor not having applied and established beyond a reasonable doubt that you committed it with the intent to commit a primary offence?
      `I was prosecuted by indictment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the designated offence prosecuted by indictment?
      `the term of imprisonment, in months` IS A NUMBER @desc For how many months were you sentenced to imprisonment (24 months is two years)?
      `the age of the victim` IS A NUMBER @desc How old was the victim, in years?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.012(1), must the court order me to comply with the Sex Offender Information Registration Act because of an indictable offence, a sentence of two years or more and a victim under 18` IS
    `s 490.012(1) the court shall make an order in Form 52 requiring the person to comply with the Sex Offender Information Registration Act` `the court is sentencing me for a designated offence` (IF `my offence is a designated offence` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE) (IF `the order is limited by subsection 490.012(5)` THEN JUST FALSE ELSE NOTHING) `I was prosecuted by indictment` `the term of imprisonment, in months` `the age of the victim`

@ref s 490.012(2)
@export Under section 490.012(2), must the court, when it sentences me, order me to comply with the Sex Offender Information Registration Act, because the prosecutor establishes that I was previously convicted of a primary offence or was already subject to an order or obligation to comply?
GIVEN `the court is sentencing me for a designated offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the court imposing a sentence on you for a designated offence?
      `my offence is a designated offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your offence a designated offence under section 490.011(1)?
      `the order is limited by subsection 490.012(5)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your offence only a secondary offence, with the prosecutor not having applied and established beyond a reasonable doubt that you committed it with the intent to commit a primary offence?
      `the prosecutor establishes I was previously convicted of a primary offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the prosecutor establish that you were previously convicted of a primary offence (or under section 130 of the National Defence Act in respect of one)?
      `the prosecutor establishes I am or was subject to an order or obligation to comply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the prosecutor establish that you are or were, as a result of a conviction, subject to an order or obligation under an Act of Parliament to comply with the Sex Offender Information Registration Act?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.012(2), must the court order me to comply with the Sex Offender Information Registration Act because of a previous primary offence or a previous order or obligation` IS
    `s 490.012(2) the court shall make an order in Form 52 requiring the person to comply with the Sex Offender Information Registration Act` `the court is sentencing me for a designated offence` (IF `my offence is a designated offence` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE) (IF `the order is limited by subsection 490.012(5)` THEN JUST FALSE ELSE NOTHING) `the prosecutor establishes I was previously convicted of a primary offence` `the prosecutor establishes I am or was subject to an order or obligation to comply`

@ref s 490.012(3)
@export Under section 490.012(3), must the court order me to comply with the Sex Offender Information Registration Act, unless I establish that there would be no connection to the purpose of the Act or that the impact on me would be grossly disproportionate?
GIVEN `the court is sentencing me, or finding me not criminally responsible, for a designated offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the court imposing a sentence on you, or rendering a verdict of not criminally responsible on account of mental disorder, for a designated offence?
      `the verdict is not criminally responsible` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it a verdict of not criminally responsible on account of mental disorder?
      `my offence is a designated offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your offence a designated offence under section 490.011(1)?
      `the order is limited by subsection 490.012(5)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your offence only a secondary offence, with the prosecutor not having applied and established beyond a reasonable doubt that you committed it with the intent to commit a primary offence?
      `subsection (1) applies to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does subsection 490.012(1) apply to you (indictable offence, two years or more, victim under 18)?
      `subsection (2) applies to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does subsection 490.012(2) apply to you (previous primary offence, or previous order or obligation)?
      `the court is satisfied I established no connection` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the court satisfied that you have established that there would be no connection between making the order and the purpose of helping police services prevent or investigate crimes of a sexual nature?
      `the court is satisfied I established gross disproportion` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the court satisfied that you have established that the impact of the order on you, including on your privacy or liberty, would be grossly disproportionate to the public interest in protecting society?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.012(3), must the court order me to comply with the Sex Offender Information Registration Act unless I establish no connection or gross disproportion` IS
    `s 490.012(3) the court shall make an order in Form 52 requiring the person to comply with the Sex Offender Information Registration Act` `the court is sentencing me, or finding me not criminally responsible, for a designated offence` `the verdict is not criminally responsible` (IF `my offence is a designated offence` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE) (IF `the order is limited by subsection 490.012(5)` THEN JUST FALSE ELSE NOTHING) (IF `subsection (1) applies to me` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE) (IF `subsection (2) applies to me` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE) `the court is satisfied I established no connection` `the court is satisfied I established gross disproportion`

@ref s 490.013
@export Under section 490.013, does the order to comply with the Sex Offender Information Registration Act apply to me for life?
GIVEN `my order was made under` IS A STRING @desc Under which subsection of section 490.012 was your order made? One of: "(1)", "(2)", "(3)"
      `my offence was prosecuted summarily` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was your offence prosecuted summarily?
      `the maximum term of imprisonment for my offence, in years` IS A NUMBER @desc What is the maximum term of imprisonment for your offence, in years (use 0 if it is life)?
      `the maximum is life imprisonment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the maximum term of imprisonment for your offence life?
      `I was convicted of two or more designated offences in the same proceeding` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you convicted of, or found not criminally responsible for, two or more designated offences in the same proceeding, for which an order may be made?
      `the court is satisfied they show a pattern of increased risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the court satisfied that those offences demonstrate, or form part of, a pattern of behaviour showing that you present an increased risk of reoffending by committing a crime of a sexual nature?
      `I was previously convicted of a primary offence or am or was subject to an order or obligation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you previously convicted of (or found not criminally responsible for) a primary offence, or are you, or were you ever, subject to an order or obligation to comply with the Sex Offender Information Registration Act?
      `the longest maximum term among my designated offences, in years` IS A NUMBER @desc If you have several designated offences: what is the longest maximum term of imprisonment among them, in years (0 if it is life)?
      `that longest maximum is life imprisonment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is that longest maximum term life imprisonment?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.013, does the order to comply with the Sex Offender Information Registration Act apply to me for life` IS
    CONSIDER `s 490.013 the duration of the order` `how it was made` (`s 490.013 facts of an offence` OF `my offence was prosecuted summarily`, `the maximum term of imprisonment for my offence, in years`, `the maximum is life imprisonment`) (`s 490.013 facts of an offence` OF FALSE, `the longest maximum term among my designated offences, in years`, `that longest maximum is life imprisonment`) `I was convicted of two or more designated offences in the same proceeding` `the court is satisfied they show a pattern of increased risk` `I was previously convicted of a primary offence or am or was subject to an order or obligation`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (silent)
    WHEN JUST `s 490.013(2)(c) the order applies for life` THEN JUST TRUE
    OTHERWISE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "ends 10 years after it was made if the offence in connection with which it was made was prosecuted summarily"
    WHERE
        `how it was made` MEANS
            BRANCH
                IF `my order was made under` EQUALS "(2)" THEN `s 490.013 subsection 490.012(2)`
                IF `my order was made under` EQUALS "(3)" THEN `s 490.013 subsection 490.012(3)`
                OTHERWISE `s 490.013 subsection 490.012(1)`

@ref s 490.013(2)
@export Under section 490.013(2), on what day does my order to comply with the Sex Offender Information Registration Act end, if it lasts 10 or 20 years?
GIVEN `the day the order was made` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the order made?
      `my order lasts` IS A STRING @desc How long does your order last? One of: "10 years", "20 years", "life"
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
DECIDE `Under section 490.013(2), on what day does my order to comply with the Sex Offender Information Registration Act end` IS
    `s 490.013(2) the day on which the order ends` `the day the order was made` `its duration`
    WHERE
        `its duration` MEANS
            BRANCH
                IF `my order lasts` EQUALS "10 years" THEN `s 490.013(2)(a) the order ends 10 years after it was made`
                IF `my order lasts` EQUALS "20 years" THEN `s 490.013(2)(b) the order ends 20 years after it was made`
                OTHERWISE `s 490.013(2)(c) the order applies for life`

@ref s 490.014
@export Under section 490.014, may I appeal from the court's decision about an order under section 490.012 or 490.013, on a question of law or of mixed law and fact?
GIVEN `I am subject to the order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you the person who is subject to an order made under section 490.012?
      `the decision is under section 490.012 or 490.013` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the decision a decision of the court under section 490.012 or 490.013?
      `my ground raises a question of law or of mixed law and fact` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does your ground of appeal raise a question of law, or of mixed law and fact (rather than a question of fact alone)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.014, may I appeal from the court's decision about an order under section 490.012 or 490.013` IS
    `s 490.014 the person may appeal from the decision of the court` (IF `I am subject to the order` THEN `s 490.014 a person who is subject to an order made under section 490.012` ELSE `s 490.014 someone else`) `the decision is under section 490.012 or 490.013` (IF `my ground raises a question of law or of mixed law and fact` THEN `s 490.014 a question of law` ELSE `s 490.014 another ground`)

@ref s 490.015(1)
@export Under section 490.015(1), may I now apply for a termination order, because five, ten or twenty years (according to how long my order lasts) have elapsed since it was made?
GIVEN `my order lasts` IS A STRING @desc How long does your order last? One of: "10 years", "20 years", "life"
      `the day the order was made` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the order made?
      `the day I am asking about` IS A DATE @desc For what day are you asking?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.015(1), may I now apply for a termination order because the time has elapsed since my order was made` IS
    `s 490.015(1) the person may apply for a termination order` `my kind of order` `the day the order was made` `the day I am asking about`
    WHERE
        `my kind of order` MEANS
            BRANCH
                IF `my order lasts` EQUALS "10 years" THEN `s 490.015(1)(a) an order referred to in paragraph 490.013(2)(a)`
                IF `my order lasts` EQUALS "20 years" THEN `s 490.015(1)(b) an order referred to in paragraph 490.013(2)(b)`
                OTHERWISE `s 490.015(1)(c) an order referred to in paragraph 490.013(2)(c) or in any of subsections 490.013(3), (5) or (6)`

@ref s 490.015(1)
@export Under section 490.015(1), on what day may I first apply for a termination order, counting five, ten or twenty years from the day my order was made?
GIVEN `my order lasts` IS A STRING @desc How long does your order last? One of: "10 years", "20 years", "life"
      `the day the order was made` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the order made?
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
DECIDE `Under section 490.015(1), on what day may I first apply for a termination order` IS
    `s 490.015(1) the earliest day on which the person may apply for a termination order` `my kind of order` `the day the order was made`
    WHERE
        `my kind of order` MEANS
            BRANCH
                IF `my order lasts` EQUALS "10 years" THEN `s 490.015(1)(a) an order referred to in paragraph 490.013(2)(a)`
                IF `my order lasts` EQUALS "20 years" THEN `s 490.015(1)(b) an order referred to in paragraph 490.013(2)(b)`
                OTHERWISE `s 490.015(1)(c) an order referred to in paragraph 490.013(2)(c) or in any of subsections 490.013(3), (5) or (6)`

@ref s 490.016(1)
@export Under section 490.016(1), must the court make a termination order, because I have established that continuing my order has no connection to the purpose of the Act or would have a grossly disproportionate impact on me?
GIVEN `the court is satisfied I established no connection` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the court satisfied that you have established that there would be no connection between continuing the order or obligation and the purpose of helping police services prevent or investigate crimes of a sexual nature?
      `the court is satisfied I established gross disproportion` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the court satisfied that you have established that the impact on you of continuing the order or obligation, including on your privacy or liberty, would be grossly disproportionate to the public interest?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.016(1), must the court make a termination order because I have established no connection or gross disproportion` IS
    `s 490.016(1) the court shall make a termination order` `the court is satisfied I established no connection` `the court is satisfied I established gross disproportion`

@ref s 490.018(4)
@export Under section 490.018(4), on what day is the earliest on which the person in charge of the place where I am held must give me a copy of the order, counting ten days before my release or discharge?
GIVEN `the day of my release or discharge` IS A DATE @desc On what day will you be released or discharged?
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `Under section 490.018(4), on what day is the earliest on which the person in charge must give me a copy of the order before my release or discharge` IS
    `s 490.018(4) the earliest day on which the person in charge shall give the person a copy of the order` `the day of my release or discharge`

@ref s 490.019
@export Under section 490.019, must I comply with the Sex Offender Information Registration Act because I was served with a notice in Form 53, unless a court has made an exemption order?
GIVEN `I was served with a notice in Form 53` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you served with a notice in Form 53?
      `I am within the applicable period under section 490.022` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you still within the applicable period specified in section 490.022?
      `a court made an exemption order under subsection 490.023(2)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a court made an exemption order under subsection 490.023(2) in your case?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.019, must I comply with the Sex Offender Information Registration Act because I was served with a notice in Form 53` IS
    `s 490.019 the person shall comply with the Sex Offender Information Registration Act` `I was served with a notice in Form 53` (IF `I am within the applicable period under section 490.022` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE) `a court made an exemption order under subsection 490.023(2)`

-- ====================================================================================================
-- seat questions from part ccc18 (merged by the lead on 2026-11-12; unchanged text follows)
-- ====================================================================================================
-- ccc18-seat.l4 -- the SEAT layer over the ccc18 modules: the Criminal Code (Canada), R.S.C., 1985, c. C-46, sections 490.02 to 492.2 (sex offender registration orders and
-- obligations, forfeiture of offence-related property, forfeiture of weapons and restitution, photographic evidence, seizure of explosives, tracking device and transmission
-- data warrants). Written by part agent ccc18; the text is a plain-text rendering of the Justice Laws Website consolidation (current to 2026-09-30), not the official text.
--
-- SEAT: a person who is, or may be, accused of an offence under the Criminal Code (the accused), asking on facts they can state whether what they did is an offence, what its
-- gravity and penalty are, what defences, rights and procedures apply at each stage, and what follows from a conviction. (Assumed, not ruled: Singapore's Penal Code is the
-- model, as the maintainer instructed on 2026-11-09, but this is a plain seat layer; no charge-generator contract.) Provisions that address only an official are asked for what they
-- mean to the person: what may be done to them, what they must be given, and by when. There is never a seat for an official.
--
-- PLAIN INPUTS. Every question takes plain inputs (yes or no, a number, a date, a choice from a list), one for each fact the person can state, each with a @desc worded as the
-- person would be asked. The questions call the layer-1 rules and never restate one. Where a layer-1 rule takes an answer from another part as a MAYBE BOOLEAN (PLAN.md section 4),
-- the question asks the person's own fact as a yes or no and wraps it in JUST: that is the person's fact, not a conversion of NOTHING to FALSE (NOTES.md, Seams).
-- Where the text hands a judgment to a court ("if it is satisfied that ..."), the question asks it as a conditional input: "If the court is satisfied that ...".
--
-- READING AN ANSWER. Questions answer MAYBE BOOLEAN: JUST TRUE (the text says yes on these facts), JUST FALSE (the text says no in its own words), NOTHING (not covered: the text is
-- silent, or the case falls outside a limit in its own wording). A caller must never read NOTHING as no. Every question is named for what it asks, in the direction its body answers:
-- a question that asks about a bar ("is ... barred") is answered by the bar; a question that asks about a power ("may ...") by the power.

§ `Sex offender registration: a notice served on you for an earlier conviction (sections 490.02 to 490.029)`
-- Only if you have been, or may be, served with a notice in Form 53 under the Sex Offender Information Registration Act: ask 'Has this happened to you?' before showing this group.

@export Under section 490.02, may the Attorney General or minister of justice serve me with a notice in Form 53 (sections 490.02(1) and (2))?
GIVEN `I was convicted of a designated offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you convicted of, or found not criminally responsible on account of mental disorder for, an offence referred to in paragraph (a), (c), (c.1), (d) or (e) of the definition designated offence in subsection 490.011(1)?
      `I was subject to a sentence or had no absolute discharge on the commencement day` IS A BOOLEAN @desc On the day the Sex Offender Information Registration Act came into force, were you subject to a sentence for the offence, or had you not received an absolute discharge under Part XX.1 from it?
      `my name was in the Ontario registry before the commencement day` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did your name appear in connection with the offence, immediately before that Act came into force, in the sex offender registry established under the Ontario Act?
      `I was a resident of Ontario between 23 April 2001 and the commencement day` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you a resident of Ontario at any time between April 23, 2001 and the day that Act came into force?
      `I committed the offence in Ontario` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you commit the offence in Ontario?
      `I was finally acquitted or pardoned of every offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you been finally acquitted of, or received a free pardon (royal prerogative of mercy or section 748) for, every offence for which a notice may be served on you?
      `an application for an order under 490.012(3) has been made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has an application been made for an order under subsection 490.012(3) (or 227.01(3) of the National Defence Act) in relation to any such offence?
      `I proved a pardon under the Ontario Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If your name was in the Ontario registry, have you provided proof of a pardon in accordance with subsection 9(1) of the Ontario Act?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.02, may the Attorney General or minister of justice serve me with a notice` IS
    `s 490.02 the notice may be served, subject to subsection (2)` (`s 490.02(1) the Attorney General or minister of justice may serve the person with a notice` (JUST `I was convicted of a designated offence`) `I was subject to a sentence or had no absolute discharge on the commencement day` `my name was in the Ontario registry before the commencement day` `I was a resident of Ontario between 23 April 2001 and the commencement day` `I committed the offence in Ontario`) (`s 490.02(2) a notice shall not be served on the person` `I was finally acquitted or pardoned of every offence` `an application for an order under 490.012(3) has been made` `I proved a pardon under the Ontario Act`)

@export Under section 490.021(1), was my notice served within one year after the day the Sex Offender Information Registration Act came into force?
GIVEN `the day the Sex Offender Information Registration Act came into force` IS A DATE @desc On what day did the Sex Offender Information Registration Act come into force? (A date, for example 2004-12-15.)
      `the day I was served with the notice` IS A DATE @desc On what day were you served with the notice?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.021(1), was my notice served within one year after the day the Sex Offender Information Registration Act came into force` IS
    `s 490.021(1) the notice is served within one year after the day on which the Sex Offender Information Registration Act comes into force` `the day the Sex Offender Information Registration Act came into force` `the day I was served with the notice`

@export Under section 490.021, is the way my notice was served one the section allows (personal service, or registered mail in the cases of subsections (2) to (4))?
GIVEN `how the notice was served` IS A `s 490.021(1) manner of service` @desc How was the notice served? One of: `s 490.021(1) personal service`, `s 490.021(2) registered mail at the last known address`, `s 490.021(1) another manner of service`.
      `I am a person referred to in paragraph 490.02(1)(a)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you, on the commencement day, subject to a sentence or without an absolute discharge (paragraph 490.02(1)(a))?
      `I was unlawfully at large` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you unlawfully at large?
      `I was in breach of a residence term` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you in breach of any terms of your sentence or discharge, or of conditions under the Act or Part III of the National Defence Act, that relate to residence?
      `I am a person referred to in paragraph 490.02(1)(b)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you a person in the Ontario registry (paragraph 490.02(1)(b))?
      `I was not in compliance with section 3 of the Ontario Act on the commencement day` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you not in compliance with section 3 of the Ontario Act on the day the Sex Offender Information Registration Act came into force?
      `I was in compliance with section 3 and subsection 7(2) of the Ontario Act on the commencement day` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you in compliance with section 3 and subsection 7(2) of the Ontario Act on that day?
      `I failed to comply with subsection 3(1) or 7(2) of the Ontario Act within one year after that day` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you fail to comply with subsection 3(1) or 7(2) of the Ontario Act within one year after that day?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.021, is the way my notice was served one the section allows` IS
    `s 490.021 the manner of service is one the section allows` `how the notice was served` (`s 490.021(2) the notice may be served by registered mail at the person's last known address` `I am a person referred to in paragraph 490.02(1)(a)` `I was unlawfully at large` `I was in breach of a residence term`) (`s 490.021(3) the notice may be served by registered mail at the person's last known address, the person not being in compliance with the Ontario Act` `I am a person referred to in paragraph 490.02(1)(b)` `I was not in compliance with section 3 of the Ontario Act on the commencement day`) (`s 490.021(4) the notice may be served by registered mail at the person's last known address, the person having failed to comply` `I am a person referred to in paragraph 490.02(1)(b)` `I was in compliance with section 3 and subsection 7(2) of the Ontario Act on the commencement day` `I failed to comply with subsection 3(1) or 7(2) of the Ontario Act within one year after that day`)

@export Under section 490.022(1), on what day does my obligation to comply with the Sex Offender Information Registration Act begin?
GIVEN `the day I was served with the notice` IS A DATE @desc On what day were you served with the notice?
      `an exemption order was refused` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was an exemption order refused under subsection 490.023(2)?
      `the day the exemption order was refused` IS A DATE @desc If it was refused, on what day? (Any date if it was not.)
      `an exemption order was quashed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was an exemption order quashed?
      `the day the exemption order was quashed` IS A DATE @desc If it was quashed, on what day? (Any date if it was not.)
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `Under section 490.022(1), on what day does my obligation to comply with the Sex Offender Information Registration Act begin` IS
    `s 490.022(1) the day on which the obligation under section 490.019 begins` `the day I was served with the notice` (IF `an exemption order was refused` THEN JUST `the day the exemption order was refused` ELSE NOTHING) (IF `an exemption order was quashed` THEN JUST `the day the exemption order was quashed` ELSE NOTHING)

@export Under section 490.022, when does my obligation under section 490.019 end, or does it apply for life (subsections (2) and (3))?
GIVEN `an exemption order was made on appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was an exemption order made on an appeal from a decision under subsection 490.023(2)?
      `the day the exemption order was made on appeal` IS A DATE @desc If so, on what day? (Any date if not.)
      `my Ontario obligation ended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you were in the Ontario registry: did your obligation under section 3 of the Ontario Act end under paragraph 7(1)(a) of that Act?
      `the day my Ontario obligation ended` IS A DATE @desc If so, on what day? (Any date if not.)
      `I provided proof of a pardon or record suspension` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you were in the Ontario registry: did you provide satisfactory proof of a pardon or record suspension at a registration centre?
      `the day I provided that proof` IS A DATE @desc If so, on what day? (Any date if not.)
      `the offence was prosecuted summarily` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the offence prosecuted summarily?
      `the most serious maximum term of imprisonment for the offence is life` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the maximum term of imprisonment for the offence life?
      `the maximum term of imprisonment for the offence in years` IS A NUMBER @desc What is the maximum term of imprisonment for the offence, in whole years (0 if it is life or none is stated)?
      `the day I was sentenced or found not criminally responsible` IS A DATE @desc On what day were you sentenced, or found not criminally responsible on account of mental disorder, for the offence listed in the notice?
      `I was convicted of more than one listed offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc At any time, were you convicted of (or found not criminally responsible for) more than one of the listed offences?
      `more than one of those offences is listed in my notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is more than one of those offences listed in the notice?
GIVETH A MAYBE `s 490.022(3) end of the obligation`
DECIDE `Under section 490.022, when does my obligation end, or does it apply for life` IS
    `s 490.022 the obligation ends, or applies for life` (`s 490.022(2) the earliest day on which the obligation ends` (IF `an exemption order was made on appeal` THEN JUST `the day the exemption order was made on appeal` ELSE NOTHING) (IF `my Ontario obligation ended` THEN JUST `the day my Ontario obligation ended` ELSE NOTHING) (IF `I provided proof of a pardon or record suspension` THEN JUST `the day I provided that proof` ELSE NOTHING)) (`s 490.022(3) the obligation, if none of paragraphs (2)(a) to (c) applies earlier, ends or applies for life` `the offence was prosecuted summarily` `the maximum term` `the day I was sentenced or found not criminally responsible` `I was convicted of more than one listed offence` `more than one of those offences is listed in my notice`)
    WHERE
        `the maximum term` MEANS IF `the most serious maximum term of imprisonment for the offence is life` THEN `imprisonment for life` ELSE IF `the maximum term of imprisonment for the offence in years` EQUALS 0 THEN `no imprisonment is stated` ELSE `imprisonment for a term of` (`Length of time` OF `the maximum term of imprisonment for the offence in years`, 0, 0, FALSE)

@export Under section 490.023(1), may I apply for an order exempting me from the obligation to comply (within one year after I am served with a notice)?
GIVEN `an order under section 490.012 is in force against me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you subject to an order under section 490.012 of the Code or section 227.01 of the National Defence Act?
      `the day I was served with the notice` IS A DATE @desc On what day were you served with the notice?
      `the day I apply` IS A DATE @desc On what day do you apply for the exemption order?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.023(1), may I apply for an exemption order` IS
    `s 490.023(1) the person may apply for an exemption order` (JUST `an order under section 490.012 is in force against me`) `the day I was served with the notice` `the day I apply`

@export Under section 490.023(2), must the court make an exemption order if it is satisfied that I have established that the impact of the obligation on me would be grossly disproportionate to the public interest?
GIVEN `the court is satisfied that the impact of the obligation on me would be grossly disproportionate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the court is satisfied that you have established that the impact of the obligation on you, including on your privacy or liberty, would be grossly disproportionate to the public interest in protecting society through the effective prevention or investigation of crimes of a sexual nature?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.023(2), must the court make an exemption order` IS
    `s 490.023(2) the court shall make an exemption order` `the court is satisfied that the impact of the obligation on me would be grossly disproportionate`

@export Under section 490.026(2), may I apply for a termination order now (five, 10 or 20 years after my sentence, by the maximum term of the offence)?
GIVEN `the offence was prosecuted summarily` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the offence prosecuted summarily?
      `the most serious maximum term of imprisonment for the offence is life` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the maximum term of imprisonment for the offence life?
      `the maximum term of imprisonment for the offence in years` IS A NUMBER @desc What is the maximum term of imprisonment for the offence, in whole years (0 if it is life or none is stated)?
      `the day I was sentenced or found not criminally responsible` IS A DATE @desc On what day were you sentenced, or found not criminally responsible on account of mental disorder, for the offence listed in the notice?
      `the day I apply` IS A DATE @desc On what day do you apply for a termination order?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.026(2), may I apply for a termination order now, the period since my sentence having elapsed` IS
    `s 490.026(2) the person may apply for a termination order, the period since sentence having elapsed` `the offence was prosecuted summarily` `the maximum term` `the day I was sentenced or found not criminally responsible` `the day I apply`
    WHERE
        `the maximum term` MEANS IF `the most serious maximum term of imprisonment for the offence is life` THEN `imprisonment for life` ELSE IF `the maximum term of imprisonment for the offence in years` EQUALS 0 THEN `no imprisonment is stated` ELSE `imprisonment for a term of` (`Length of time` OF `the maximum term of imprisonment for the offence in years`, 0, 0, FALSE)

@export Under section 490.026(5), may I apply again for a termination order after my application was refused?
GIVEN `my application was refused` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was your application for a termination order refused?
      `the day I made the previous application` IS A DATE @desc On what day did you make the previous application?
      `the day I apply again` IS A DATE @desc On what day do you apply again?
      `I have received a pardon` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you received a pardon since?
      `a record suspension has been ordered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a record suspension been ordered since?
      `I have since become subject to another obligation or order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Since the previous application, have you become subject to an obligation under section 490.02901 (or 227.06 of the National Defence Act or 36.1 of the International Transfer of Offenders Act) or an order under section 490.012 (or 227.01 of the National Defence Act)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.026(5), may I apply again for a termination order` IS
    `s 490.026(5) the person may apply again for a termination order` `my application was refused` `the day I made the previous application` `the day I apply again` `I have received a pardon` `a record suspension has been ordered` `I have since become subject to another obligation or order`

@export Under section 490.027(1), must the court make an order terminating my obligation if it is satisfied that I have established that there would be no connection, or that the impact on me would be grossly disproportionate?
GIVEN `the court is satisfied that there would be no connection between continuing the obligation and the purpose of the Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the court is satisfied that you have established that there would be no connection between continuing the obligation and the purpose of helping police services prevent or investigate crimes of a sexual nature by requiring the registration of information relating to sex offenders?
      `the court is satisfied that the impact on me of continuing the obligation would be grossly disproportionate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the court is satisfied that you have established that the impact on you of continuing the obligation, including on your privacy or liberty, would be grossly disproportionate to the public interest in protecting society through the effective prevention or investigation of crimes of a sexual nature?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.027(1), must the court make an order terminating my obligation` IS
    `s 490.027(1) the court shall make an order terminating the obligation` `the court is satisfied that there would be no connection between continuing the obligation and the purpose of the Act` `the court is satisfied that the impact on me of continuing the obligation would be grossly disproportionate`

§ `Sex offender registration: a conviction outside Canada (sections 490.02901 to 490.02911)`
-- Only if you were convicted of, or found not criminally responsible for, an offence outside Canada and arrived in Canada: ask 'Has this happened to you?' before showing this group.

@export Under section 490.02902, may the Attorney General or minister of justice serve me with a notice in Form 54 for a conviction outside Canada?
GIVEN `the day I arrived in Canada` IS A DATE @desc On what day did you arrive in Canada?
      `I was convicted or found not criminally responsible for an offence outside Canada` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you convicted of, or found not criminally responsible on account of mental disorder for, an offence outside Canada?
      `the offence is a service offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the offence a service offence as defined in subsection 2(1) of the National Defence Act?
      `the Attorney General or minister considers the offence equivalent to a primary offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc In the opinion of the Attorney General or minister of justice, is the offence equivalent to an offence referred to in paragraph (a) of the definition primary offence in subsection 490.011(1)?
      `I was acquitted of every such offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you been acquitted of every offence for which a notice may be served on you under section 490.02903?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.02902, may the Attorney General or minister of justice serve me with a notice in Form 54` IS
    `s 490.02902 the notice in Form 54 may be served, subject to subsection (2)` (`s 490.02902(1) the Attorney General or minister of justice may serve the person with a notice in Form 54` `the day I arrived in Canada` `I was convicted or found not criminally responsible for an offence outside Canada` `the offence is a service offence` `the Attorney General or minister considers the offence equivalent to a primary offence`) (`s 490.02902(2) a notice shall not be served on the person` `I was acquitted of every such offence`)

@export Under section 490.02903(1), is the way my notice in Form 54 was served the one the subsection requires (personal service)?
GIVEN `the notice was personally served` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the notice personally served on you?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.02903(1), is the way my notice in Form 54 was served the one the subsection requires` IS
    `s 490.02903(1) the manner of service is the one the subsection requires` `the notice was personally served`

@export Under section 490.02904(3), if subsection (2) does not apply (no exemption order has been made), when does my obligation under section 490.02901 end, or does it apply for life (by the maximum term in Canadian law for the equivalent offence)?
GIVEN `the most serious maximum term of imprisonment for the offence is life` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the maximum term of imprisonment for the offence life?
      `the maximum term of imprisonment for the offence in years` IS A NUMBER @desc What is the maximum term of imprisonment for the offence, in whole years (0 if it is life or none is stated)?
      `the day I was sentenced or found not criminally responsible` IS A DATE @desc On what day were you sentenced, or found not criminally responsible on account of mental disorder, for the offence?
      `I was convicted outside Canada of more than one equivalent offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc At any time, were you convicted of (or found not criminally responsible for) more than one offence outside Canada that is, in the opinion of the Attorney General or minister, equivalent to a primary offence?
      `more than one of those offences is listed in my notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is more than one of those offences listed in the notice?
GIVETH A MAYBE `s 490.022(3) end of the obligation`
DECIDE `Under section 490.02904(3), if subsection (2) does not apply, when does my obligation under section 490.02901 end, or does it apply for life` IS
    `s 490.02904(3) the obligation, if subsection (2) does not apply, ends or applies for life` `the maximum term` `the day I was sentenced or found not criminally responsible` `I was convicted outside Canada of more than one equivalent offence` `more than one of those offences is listed in my notice`
    WHERE
        `the maximum term` MEANS IF `the most serious maximum term of imprisonment for the offence is life` THEN `imprisonment for life` ELSE IF `the maximum term of imprisonment for the offence in years` EQUALS 0 THEN `no imprisonment is stated` ELSE `imprisonment for a term of` (`Length of time` OF `the maximum term of imprisonment for the offence in years`, 0, 0, FALSE)

@export Under section 490.02905(1), may I apply for an order exempting me from the obligation (within one year after I am served with a notice in Form 54)?
GIVEN `I was served with a notice in Form 54` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you served with a notice in Form 54 under section 490.02903?
      `the day I was served` IS A DATE @desc On what day were you served?
      `the day I apply` IS A DATE @desc On what day do you apply?
      `I apply to a court of criminal jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you apply to a court of criminal jurisdiction?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.02905(1), may I apply for an exemption order from a notice in Form 54` IS
    `s 490.02905(1) the person may apply for an exemption order` `I was served with a notice in Form 54` `the day I was served` `the day I apply` `I apply to a court of criminal jurisdiction`

@export Under section 490.02908(2), may I apply for a termination order now (five, 10 or 20 years after my sentence, by the maximum term in Canadian law for the equivalent offence)?
GIVEN `the most serious maximum term of imprisonment for the offence is life` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the maximum term of imprisonment for the offence life?
      `the maximum term of imprisonment for the offence in years` IS A NUMBER @desc What is the maximum term of imprisonment for the offence, in whole years (0 if it is life or none is stated)?
      `the day I was sentenced or found not criminally responsible` IS A DATE @desc On what day were you sentenced, or found not criminally responsible on account of mental disorder, for the offence?
      `the day I apply` IS A DATE @desc On what day do you apply for a termination order?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.02908(2), may I apply for a termination order of the Form 54 obligation, the period since my sentence having elapsed` IS
    `s 490.02908(2) the person may apply for a termination order, the period since sentence having elapsed` `the maximum term` `the day I was sentenced or found not criminally responsible` `the day I apply`
    WHERE
        `the maximum term` MEANS IF `the most serious maximum term of imprisonment for the offence is life` THEN `imprisonment for life` ELSE IF `the maximum term of imprisonment for the offence in years` EQUALS 0 THEN `no imprisonment is stated` ELSE `imprisonment for a term of` (`Length of time` OF `the maximum term of imprisonment for the offence in years`, 0, 0, FALSE)

@export Under section 490.02911(1), must I advise a police service of my conviction abroad within seven days after I arrive in Canada?
GIVEN `I was convicted or found not criminally responsible for an offence outside Canada` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you convicted of, or found not criminally responsible on account of mental disorder for, an offence outside Canada?
      `the offence is equivalent to a primary offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the offence equivalent to one referred to in paragraph (a) of the definition primary offence in subsection 490.011(1)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.02911(1), must I advise a police service after I arrive in Canada` IS
    `s 490.02911(1) the person must advise a police service` `I was convicted or found not criminally responsible for an offence outside Canada` `the offence is equivalent to a primary offence`

@export Under section 490.02911(1), did I advise a police service within seven days after the day I arrived in Canada?
GIVEN `the day I arrived in Canada` IS A DATE @desc On what day did you arrive in Canada?
      `the day I advised a police service` IS A DATE @desc On what day did you advise a police service?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.02911(1), did I advise a police service within seven days after the day I arrived in Canada` IS
    `s 490.02911(1) the person advised a police service within seven days after the day on which they arrived in Canada` `the day I arrived in Canada` `the day I advised a police service`

@export Under section 490.02911(2), must I advise a police service of a change in my address within seven days?
GIVEN `I am in Canada` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you in Canada?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.02911(2), must I advise a police service of a change in address` IS
    `s 490.02911(2) the person must advise a police service of a change in address` `I am in Canada`

@export Under section 490.02911(2), did I advise a police service of the change in my address within seven days after the day the change was made?
GIVEN `the day my address changed` IS A DATE @desc On what day did your address change?
      `the day I advised a police service` IS A DATE @desc On what day did you advise a police service of the change?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.02911(2), did I advise a police service of the change in address within seven days after the change` IS
    `s 490.02911(2) the person advised a police service of the change in address within seven days after the day on which the change was made` `the day my address changed` `the day I advised a police service`

§ `Sex offender registration: a transfer to Canada under the International Transfer of Offenders Act (sections 490.029111 to 490.02915)`
-- Only if you were transferred to Canada under that Act: ask 'Has this happened to you?' before showing this group.

@export Under section 490.029111(1), may I apply for an order exempting me from the obligation (within one year after the day of my transfer to Canada)?
GIVEN `I am subject to an obligation under section 36.1 of the International Transfer of Offenders Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you subject to an obligation under section 36.1 of the International Transfer of Offenders Act?
      `the day of my transfer to Canada` IS A DATE @desc On what day were you transferred to Canada?
      `the day I apply` IS A DATE @desc On what day do you apply?
      `I apply to a court of criminal jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you apply to a court of criminal jurisdiction?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.029111(1), may I apply for an exemption order after my transfer to Canada` IS
    `s 490.029111(1) the person may apply for an exemption order` `I am subject to an obligation under section 36.1 of the International Transfer of Offenders Act` `the day of my transfer to Canada` `the day I apply` `I apply to a court of criminal jurisdiction`

@export Under section 490.02912(2), may I apply for a termination order now (five, 10 or 20 years after my sentence, by the maximum term in Canadian law for the equivalent offence)?
GIVEN `the most serious maximum term of imprisonment for the offence is life` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the maximum term of imprisonment for the offence life?
      `the maximum term of imprisonment for the offence in years` IS A NUMBER @desc What is the maximum term of imprisonment for the offence, in whole years (0 if it is life or none is stated)?
      `the day I was sentenced or found not criminally responsible` IS A DATE @desc On what day were you sentenced, or found not criminally responsible on account of mental disorder, for the offence?
      `the day I apply` IS A DATE @desc On what day do you apply for a termination order?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.02912(2), may I apply for a termination order of the section 36.1 obligation, the period since my sentence having elapsed` IS
    `s 490.02912(2) the person may apply for a termination order, the period since sentence having elapsed` `the maximum term` `the day I was sentenced or found not criminally responsible` `the day I apply`
    WHERE
        `the maximum term` MEANS IF `the most serious maximum term of imprisonment for the offence is life` THEN `imprisonment for life` ELSE IF `the maximum term of imprisonment for the offence in years` EQUALS 0 THEN `no imprisonment is stated` ELSE `imprisonment for a term of` (`Length of time` OF `the maximum term of imprisonment for the offence in years`, 0, 0, FALSE)

@export Under section 490.02915(2), must the Review Board cause a copy of the Form 1 to be given to me when it discharges me?
GIVEN `the Review Board discharged me absolutely under paragraph 672.54(a)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the Review Board direct under paragraph 672.54(a) that you be discharged absolutely?
      `the Review Board discharged me subject to conditions under paragraph 672.54(b)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the Review Board direct under paragraph 672.54(b) that you be discharged subject to conditions?
      `the conditions prevent me from complying` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do the conditions restrict your liberty in a manner and to an extent that prevent you from complying with sections 4, 4.1, 4.3 and 6 of the Sex Offender Information Registration Act?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.02915(2), must the Review Board cause a copy of the Form 1 to be given to me` IS
    `s 490.02915(2) the Review Board must cause a copy of the Form 1 to be given to the person` `the Review Board discharged me absolutely under paragraph 672.54(a)` `the Review Board discharged me subject to conditions under paragraph 672.54(b)` `the conditions prevent me from complying`

§ `Offences about the registration orders and obligations, and the warrant (sections 490.03 to 490.03121)`
-- Only if you are subject to a registration order or obligation, or have been given a notice: ask 'Has this happened to you?' before showing this group.

@export Under section 490.031(1), am I guilty of an offence if I, without reasonable excuse, fail to comply with an order or obligation to comply with the Sex Offender Information Registration Act?
GIVEN `I failed to comply with an order or obligation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you fail to comply with an order made under section 490.012 (or 227.01 of the National Defence Act) or an obligation under section 490.019 or 490.02901 (or 227.06 of that Act or 36.1 of the International Transfer of Offenders Act)?
      `I had a reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you have a reasonable excuse?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.031(1), am I guilty of an offence for failing to comply with an order or obligation` IS
    `s 490.031(1) the person is guilty of an offence` `I failed to comply with an order or obligation` `I had a reasonable excuse`

@export Under section 490.031(1)(a), what is the most I can be punished on conviction on indictment (a fine of not more than $10,000 or imprisonment for not more than two years, or both)?
GIVETH A Punishment
DECIDE `Under section 490.031(1), what is the most I can be punished on conviction on indictment` IS
    `s 490.031(1)(a) the punishment on conviction on indictment`

@export Under section 490.031(1)(b), what is the most I can be punished on summary conviction (a fine of not more than $10,000 or imprisonment for not more than two years less a day, or both)?
GIVETH A Punishment
DECIDE `Under section 490.031(1), what is the most I can be punished on summary conviction` IS
    `s 490.031(1)(b) the punishment on summary conviction`

@export Under section 490.031(5), is a certificate of failure to report barred from being received in evidence against me?
GIVEN `the party gave me a copy of the certificate before the trial began` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Before the trial began, did the party who intends to produce the certificate give you a copy of it?
      `the party gave me reasonable notice before the trial began` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Before the trial began, did that party give you reasonable notice of their intention to produce it?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.031(5), is a certificate barred from being received in evidence against me` IS
    `s 490.031(5) the certificate is not to be received in evidence` `the party gave me a copy of the certificate before the trial began` `the party gave me reasonable notice before the trial began`

@export Under section 490.0311, am I guilty of an offence if I knowingly give false or misleading information under subsection 5(1) or 6(1) or (1.01) of the Sex Offender Information Registration Act?
GIVEN `I knowingly gave false or misleading information` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you knowingly provide false or misleading information under subsection 5(1) or 6(1) or (1.01) of that Act?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.0311, am I guilty of an offence for knowingly giving false or misleading information` IS
    `s 490.0311 the person is guilty of an offence` `I knowingly gave false or misleading information`

@export Under section 490.0312, am I guilty of an offence if I, without reasonable excuse, fail to advise a police service as subsection 490.02911(1) or (2) requires?
GIVEN `I failed to advise a police service as required` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you fail to comply with an obligation under subsection 490.02911(1) or (2)?
      `I had a reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you have a reasonable excuse?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.0312, am I guilty of an offence for failing to advise a police service` IS
    `s 490.0312 the person is guilty of an offence` `I failed to advise a police service as required` `I had a reasonable excuse`

@export Under section 490.03121(1), may a justice issue a warrant to arrest me and bring me to a registration centre to remedy a contravention?
GIVEN `the justice is satisfied that there are reasonable grounds to believe that I contravened any of sections 4 to 5.1 of the Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If a justice is satisfied that there are reasonable grounds to believe that you contravened any of sections 4 to 5.1 of the Sex Offender Information Registration Act?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.03121(1), may a justice issue a warrant in Form 6.4 to arrest me` IS
    `s 490.03121(1) the justice may issue a warrant in Form 6.4 to arrest the person` `the justice is satisfied that there are reasonable grounds to believe that I contravened any of sections 4 to 5.1 of the Act`

@export Under section 490.03121(5), is a charge barred for a contravention of sections 4 to 5.1 that I remedied after the warrant was issued?
GIVEN `the contravention is of one of sections 4 to 5.1` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the contravention one of sections 4 to 5.1 of the Sex Offender Information Registration Act?
      `I remedied it after the warrant was issued` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you remedy the contravention after the warrant was issued?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.03121(5), is a charge barred for a contravention I remedied after the warrant was issued` IS
    `s 490.03121(5) no charge shall be laid against the person in respect of the contravention` `the contravention is of one of sections 4 to 5.1` `I remedied it after the warrant was issued`

§ `Additional orders for earlier orders and obligations (sections 490.04 to 490.07)`
-- Only if you are subject to an order under section 490.012 made on or after 15 April 2011, or an obligation that began before the amendments: ask 'Has this happened to you?' before showing this group.

@export Under section 490.04(1), may I apply to a court for an order exempting me from an earlier order or obligation?
GIVEN `I am subject to an order made under section 490.012 on or after 15 April 2011 but before the paragraph came into force` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you subject to an order made under section 490.012 on or after April 15, 2011 but before the day paragraph 490.04(1)(a) came into force?
      `I am subject to an obligation under section 490.02901 or 36.1 that began before the paragraph came into force` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you subject to an obligation under section 490.02901, or under section 36.1 of the International Transfer of Offenders Act, that began before the day paragraph 490.04(1)(b) came into force?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.04(1), may I apply to a court for an exemption order` IS
    `s 490.04(1) the person may apply to a court for an exemption order` `I am subject to an order made under section 490.012 on or after 15 April 2011 but before the paragraph came into force` `I am subject to an obligation under section 490.02901 or 36.1 that began before the paragraph came into force`

@export Under section 490.04(4), is the court barred from making an exemption order on my application under paragraph (1)(a)?
GIVEN `my application is under paragraph 490.04(1)(a)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your application for an exemption from an order made under section 490.012?
      `the designated offence was prosecuted by indictment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the designated offence that is the basis of the order prosecuted by indictment?
      `my sentence was two years or more` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the sentence for that offence a term of imprisonment of two years or more?
      `the victim was under 18` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the victim of that offence under the age of 18 years?
      `I was convicted of another primary offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Before or after the order was made, were you convicted of another primary offence (or an offence under section 130 of the National Defence Act in respect of one)?
      `I am or was subject to another registration order or obligation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you, or were you, as a result of a conviction, subject to another order or obligation under this or another Act of Parliament to comply with the Sex Offender Information Registration Act?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.04(4), is the court barred from making an exemption order` IS
    `s 490.04(4) the court shall not make an exemption order` `my application is under paragraph 490.04(1)(a)` `the designated offence was prosecuted by indictment` `my sentence was two years or more` `the victim was under 18` `I was convicted of another primary offence` `I am or was subject to another registration order or obligation`

§ `Forfeiture of offence-related property, restraint orders and management orders (sections 490.1 to 490.9)`
-- Only if property of yours has been seized, restrained or is the subject of a forfeiture application: ask 'Has this happened to you?' before showing this group.

@export Under section 490.1(1), must the court order my property forfeited after my conviction, subject to sections 490.3 to 490.41?
GIVEN `I was convicted, or discharged under section 730, of an indictable offence under the Code or the Corruption of Foreign Public Officials Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you convicted, or discharged under section 730, of an indictable offence under the Criminal Code or the Corruption of Foreign Public Officials Act?
      `the court is satisfied, on the Attorney General's application and a balance of probabilities, that the property is related to the offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the Attorney General applies: is the court satisfied, on a balance of probabilities, that the property is related to the commission of the offence?
      `the property is offence-related property` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the property offence-related property (property by means of or in respect of which the offence was committed, used in connection with it, or intended to be used for committing it)?
      `the court returns the property under subsection 490.4(3)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the court order the property returned to a person under subsection 490.4(3)?
      `the court decides under subsection 490.41(3) not to order the forfeiture` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the court decide under subsection 490.41(3) not to order the forfeiture because its impact would be disproportionate?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.1(1), must the court order my property forfeited, subject to sections 490.3 to 490.41` IS
    `s 490.1(1) the court shall order the property forfeited, subject to sections 490.3 to 490.41` (`s 490.1(1) the court shall order the property forfeited` `I was convicted, or discharged under section 730, of an indictable offence under the Code or the Corruption of Foreign Public Officials Act` `the court is satisfied, on the Attorney General's application and a balance of probabilities, that the property is related to the offence` (JUST `the property is offence-related property`)) `the court returns the property under subsection 490.4(3)` `the court decides under subsection 490.41(3) not to order the forfeiture`

@export Under section 490.2(2), must a judge order property forfeited when the accused charged with the offence has died or absconded, subject to sections 490.3 to 490.41?
GIVEN `the judge is satisfied beyond a reasonable doubt that the property is offence-related property` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the judge is satisfied beyond a reasonable doubt that the property is offence-related property?
      `the judge is satisfied that proceedings in respect of an indictable offence in relation to the property were commenced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the judge is satisfied that proceedings in respect of an indictable offence under the Code or the Corruption of Foreign Public Officials Act in relation to the property were commenced?
      `the judge is satisfied that the accused has died or absconded` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the judge is satisfied that the accused charged with the offence has died or absconded?
      `the court returns the property under subsection 490.4(3)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the court order the property returned to a person under subsection 490.4(3)?
      `the court decides under subsection 490.41(3) not to order the forfeiture` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the court decide under subsection 490.41(3) not to order the forfeiture because its impact would be disproportionate?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.2(2), must a judge order the property forfeited because the accused has died or absconded, subject to sections 490.3 to 490.41` IS
    `s 490.2(2) the judge shall order the property forfeited, subject to sections 490.3 to 490.41` (`s 490.2(2) the judge shall order the property forfeited` `the judge is satisfied beyond a reasonable doubt that the property is offence-related property` `the judge is satisfied that proceedings in respect of an indictable offence in relation to the property were commenced` `the judge is satisfied that the accused has died or absconded`) `the court returns the property under subsection 490.4(3)` `the court decides under subsection 490.41(3) not to order the forfeiture`

@export Under section 490.3, may the court set aside a transfer of the property made after the seizure or restraint order, before it orders forfeiture?
GIVEN `the transfer was made after the seizure or a restraint order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the conveyance or transfer made after the seizure of the property, or the making of a restraint order in respect of it?
      `the transfer was for valuable consideration to a person acting in good faith` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the transfer for valuable consideration to a person acting in good faith?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.3, may the court set aside a transfer of the property` IS
    `s 490.3 the court may set aside the conveyance or transfer` `the transfer was made after the seizure or a restraint order` `the transfer was for valuable consideration to a person acting in good faith`

@export Under section 490.4(3), may the court order that the property be returned to me as its lawful owner, if I appear innocent of any complicity?
GIVEN `I was charged with an indictable offence under the Code or the Corruption of Foreign Public Officials Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you charged with an indictable offence under the Criminal Code or the Corruption of Foreign Public Officials Act?
      `I got title from a person charged, to avoid the forfeiture` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you acquire title to or a right of possession of the property from such a person in circumstances that give rise to a reasonable inference that it was transferred to avoid forfeiture?
      `the court is satisfied that I am the lawful owner or entitled to possession` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the court is satisfied that you are the lawful owner or lawfully entitled to possession of all or part of the property?
      `the court is satisfied that I appear innocent of any complicity or collusion` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the court is satisfied that you appear innocent of any complicity in, or collusion in relation to, the offence?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.4(3), may the court order that the property be returned to me` IS
    `s 490.4(3) the court may order that the property be returned to the person` `I was charged with an indictable offence under the Code or the Corruption of Foreign Public Officials Act` `I got title from a person charged, to avoid the forfeiture` `the court is satisfied that I am the lawful owner or entitled to possession` `the court is satisfied that I appear innocent of any complicity or collusion`

@export Under section 490.41(3), may the court decide not to order the forfeiture of the property, or part of it, because the impact would be disproportionate?
GIVEN `the court is satisfied that the impact of the forfeiture would be disproportionate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the court is satisfied that the impact of an order of forfeiture would be disproportionate to the nature and gravity of the offence, the circumstances surrounding its commission and the criminal record, if any, of the person charged or convicted?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.41(3), may the court decide not to order the forfeiture because it would be disproportionate` IS
    `s 490.41(3) (decision) the court may decide not to order the forfeiture of the property or part of the property` `the court is satisfied that the impact of the forfeiture would be disproportionate`

@export Under section 490.5(1), may I apply to a judge, within thirty days after the forfeiture, for an order that my interest in the forfeited property is not affected?
GIVEN `the property was forfeited under` IS A `s 490.5(1) order under which the property was forfeited` @desc Under which order was the property forfeited? One of: `s 490.5(1) an order under subsection 490.1(1)`, `s 490.5(1) an order under subsection 490.2(2)`.
      `I claim an interest in the property` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you claim an interest in the property?
      `I was convicted of the indictable offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you convicted of the indictable offence in relation to which the property was forfeited?
      `I was charged with the indictable offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you charged with the indictable offence in relation to which the property was forfeited?
      `I took title from such a person to avoid the forfeiture` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you acquire title or a right of possession from a person convicted or charged, in circumstances that give rise to a reasonable inference that it was transferred to avoid forfeiture?
      `the day of the forfeiture` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the property forfeited?
      `the day I apply` IS A DATE @desc On what day do you apply?
      `I apply by notice in writing to a judge` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you apply by notice in writing to a judge?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.5(1), may I apply for an order that my interest in the forfeited property is not affected` IS
    `s 490.5(1) the person may apply for an order under subsection (4)` `the property was forfeited under` `I claim an interest in the property` `I was convicted of the indictable offence` `I was charged with the indictable offence` `I took title from such a person to avoid the forfeiture` `the day of the forfeiture` `the day I apply` `I apply by notice in writing to a judge`

@export Under section 490.5(4), may the judge declare that my interest in the property is not affected by the forfeiture?
GIVEN `the judge is satisfied that I am not excluded and appear innocent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Where the judge is satisfied that you are not a person referred to in paragraph (1)(a), (b) or (c) and appear innocent of any complicity or collusion?
      `the judge is satisfied that I exercised all reasonable care` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Where the judge is satisfied that you exercised all reasonable care to be satisfied that the property was not likely to be used in connection with an unlawful act by the person you let take possession, or from whom you took possession?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.5(4), may the judge declare that my interest is not affected by the forfeiture` IS
    `s 490.5(4) the judge may make an order declaring that the interest of the applicant is not affected by the forfeiture` `the judge is satisfied that I am not excluded and appear innocent` `the judge is satisfied that I exercised all reasonable care`

@export Under section 490.7, is the property barred from being disposed of or otherwise dealt with until thirty days have expired after the order was made?
GIVEN `the day the order was made` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the order of forfeiture or restoration made?
      `the day the property is disposed of or dealt with` IS A DATE @desc On what day is the property disposed of or otherwise dealt with?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.7, is the property barred from being disposed of until thirty days have expired` IS
    `s 490.7 the property shall not be disposed of or otherwise dealt with` `the day the order was made` `the day the property is disposed of or dealt with`

@export Under section 490.8(3), may a judge make a restraint order prohibiting me from disposing of, or dealing with any interest in, the property?
GIVEN `the judge is satisfied that there are reasonable grounds to believe that the property is offence-related property` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the judge is satisfied that there are reasonable grounds to believe that the property is offence-related property?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.8(3), may a judge make a restraint order prohibiting me from dealing with the property` IS
    `s 490.8(3) the judge may make a restraint order` `the judge is satisfied that there are reasonable grounds to believe that the property is offence-related property`

@export Under section 490.8(9), am I guilty of an offence if, while a restraint order is in force and served on me, I act in contravention of it or fail to comply with it?
GIVEN `the restraint order was served on me in accordance with the section` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was a restraint order made under section 490.8 served on you in accordance with that section?
      `the order was in force` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the order in force?
      `I acted in contravention of the order or failed to comply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you act in contravention of the order or fail to comply with it?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 490.8(9), am I guilty of an offence for breaching a restraint order` IS
    `s 490.8(9) the person is guilty of an offence` `the restraint order was served on me in accordance with the section` `the order was in force` `I acted in contravention of the order or failed to comply`

@export Under section 490.8(9)(a), what is the most I can be punished on conviction on indictment (imprisonment for not more than five years)?
GIVETH A Punishment
DECIDE `Under section 490.8(9), what is the most I can be punished on conviction on indictment for breaching a restraint order` IS
    `s 490.8(9)(a) the punishment on conviction on indictment`

@export Under section 490.8(9)(b), what is the most I can be punished on summary conviction (the paragraph states no penalty)?
GIVETH A Punishment
DECIDE `Under section 490.8(9), what is the most I can be punished on summary conviction for breaching a restraint order` IS
    `s 490.8(9)(b) the punishment on summary conviction`

§ `Forfeiture of weapons, restitution of property obtained by crime, photographic evidence and explosives (sections 491 to 492)`
-- Only if a weapon, ammunition or other property of yours has been seized, or you are tried for an offence involving property: ask 'Has this happened to you?' before showing this group.

DECLARE `ccc18 seat kind of thing` IS ONE OF
    `ccc18 seat the thing is a weapon`
    `ccc18 seat the thing is a imitation firearm`
    `ccc18 seat the thing is a prohibited device`
    `ccc18 seat the thing is a firearm part`
    `ccc18 seat the thing is a ammunition`
    `ccc18 seat the thing is a prohibited ammunition`
    `ccc18 seat the thing is a explosive substance`
    `ccc18 seat the thing is a firearm`
    `ccc18 seat the thing is a cross-bow`
    `ccc18 seat the thing is a prohibited weapon`
    `ccc18 seat the thing is a restricted weapon`
    `ccc18 seat the thing is none of these`

@export Under section 491(1), is a weapon, firearm, ammunition or explosive substance seized from me forfeited to Her Majesty?
GIVEN `a court has determined that the thing was used in the commission of an offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a court determined that the thing was used in the commission of an offence?
      `a court has determined that I committed an offence that involves the thing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a court determined that you committed an offence that involves, or whose subject-matter is, the thing?
      `the thing has been seized and detained` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the thing been seized and detained?
      `what kind of thing it is` IS A `ccc18 seat kind of thing` @desc What kind of thing is it? One of: `ccc18 seat the thing is a weapon`, `ccc18 seat the thing is a imitation firearm`, `ccc18 seat the thing is a prohibited device`, `ccc18 seat the thing is a firearm part`, `ccc18 seat the thing is a ammunition`, `ccc18 seat the thing is a prohibited ammunition`, `ccc18 seat the thing is a explosive substance`, `ccc18 seat the thing is a firearm`, `ccc18 seat the thing is a cross-bow`, `ccc18 seat the thing is a prohibited weapon`, `ccc18 seat the thing is a restricted weapon`, `ccc18 seat the thing is none of these`.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 491(1), is a thing seized from me forfeited to Her Majesty` IS
    `s 491(1) the thing is forfeited to Her Majesty` `a court has determined that the thing was used in the commission of an offence` `a court has determined that I committed an offence that involves the thing` `the thing has been seized and detained` `the thing classified under sections 2 and 2.1`
    WHERE
        `the thing classified under sections 2 and 2.1` MEANS `s 491(1) the thing` OF JUST (`what kind of thing it is` EQUALS `ccc18 seat the thing is a weapon`), JUST (`what kind of thing it is` EQUALS `ccc18 seat the thing is a imitation firearm`), JUST (`what kind of thing it is` EQUALS `ccc18 seat the thing is a prohibited device`), JUST (`what kind of thing it is` EQUALS `ccc18 seat the thing is a firearm part`), JUST (`what kind of thing it is` EQUALS `ccc18 seat the thing is a ammunition`), JUST (`what kind of thing it is` EQUALS `ccc18 seat the thing is a prohibited ammunition`), JUST (`what kind of thing it is` EQUALS `ccc18 seat the thing is a explosive substance`), JUST (`what kind of thing it is` EQUALS `ccc18 seat the thing is a firearm`), JUST (`what kind of thing it is` EQUALS `ccc18 seat the thing is a cross-bow`), JUST (`what kind of thing it is` EQUALS `ccc18 seat the thing is a prohibited weapon`), JUST (`what kind of thing it is` EQUALS `ccc18 seat the thing is a restricted weapon`)

@export Under section 491(2), what order must the court make for me, as the lawful owner of a thing that may be forfeited, if I was not a party to the offence?
GIVEN `the court is satisfied that I was not a party to the offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the court is satisfied that you, the lawful owner, were not a party to the offence?
      `the court is satisfied that I had no reasonable grounds to believe the thing would be used in an offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the court is satisfied that you had no reasonable grounds to believe that the thing would or might be used in the commission of an offence?
      `what has become of the thing` IS A `s 491(2) state of the thing` @desc What has become of the thing? One of: `s 491(2) the thing is still held`, `s 491(2) the thing was sold`, `s 491(2) the thing was destroyed`.
GIVETH A `s 491(2) order`
DECIDE `Under section 491(2), what order must the court make for me as the lawful owner of a seized thing` IS
    `s 491(2) the order the court shall make` `the court is satisfied that I was not a party to the offence` `the court is satisfied that I had no reasonable grounds to believe the thing would be used in an offence` `what has become of the thing`

@export Under section 491.1(2), what order must the court make about property obtained by the commission of the offence of which I am tried?
GIVEN `the circumstances of section 491.1(1) exist` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you tried for an offence, has the court determined that an offence was committed (whether or not you were convicted), and is the property obtained by it before the court and not needed as evidence elsewhere?
      `the lawful owner or person lawfully entitled to possession is known` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the lawful owner or person lawfully entitled to possession of the property known?
GIVETH A MAYBE `s 491.1(2) order`
DECIDE `Under section 491.1(2), what order must the court make about property obtained by crime` IS
    `s 491.1(2) the order the court shall make` `the circumstances of section 491.1(1) exist` `the lawful owner or person lawfully entitled to possession is known`

@export Under section 491.1(3), is an order under subsection (2) barred (an agent charged under section 330, 331, 332 or 336, or property of the kinds in paragraph (b))?
GIVEN `I am an agent entrusted with possession of goods or documents of title to goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are the proceedings against a trustee, banker, merchant, attorney, factor, broker or other agent entrusted with the possession of goods or documents of title to goods?
      `the section number of the offence` IS A NUMBER @desc Under which section of the Criminal Code is the offence? (A number, for example 330.)
      `someone in good faith got lawful title for valuable consideration` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the property property to which a person acting in good faith and without notice has acquired lawful title for valuable consideration?
      `it is a valuable security paid or discharged in good faith` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it a valuable security that has been paid or discharged in good faith by a person who was liable to pay or discharge it?
      `it is a negotiable instrument taken in good faith for valuable consideration` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it a negotiable instrument taken or received in good faith for valuable consideration by a person who had no notice and no reasonable cause to suspect that an offence had been committed?
      `ownership or possession is disputed by claimants other than the accused` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is there a dispute as to ownership or right of possession by claimants other than the accused or defendant?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 491.1(3), is an order for return or forfeiture of the property barred` IS
    `s 491.1(3) an order shall not be made under subsection (2)` `I am an agent entrusted with possession of goods or documents of title to goods` (`Provision of the Code` OF `the section number of the offence`, "") `someone in good faith got lawful title for valuable consideration` `it is a valuable security paid or discharged in good faith` `it is a negotiable instrument taken in good faith for valuable consideration` `ownership or possession is disputed by claimants other than the accused`

@export Under section 491.2(5), is a photograph, certificate, affidavit or solemn declaration barred from being received in evidence against me?
GIVEN `the court orders otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the court order that it may be received?
      `the prosecutor gave me a copy before the trial` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Before the trial or other proceeding, did the prosecutor give you a copy of it?
      `the prosecutor gave me reasonable notice before the trial` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Before the trial or other proceeding, did the prosecutor give you reasonable notice of the intention to produce it in evidence?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 491.2(5), is the photograph, certificate, affidavit or solemn declaration barred from being received in evidence against me` IS
    `s 491.2(5) the photograph, certificate, affidavit or solemn declaration shall not be received in evidence` `the court orders otherwise` `the prosecutor gave me a copy before the trial` `the prosecutor gave me reasonable notice before the trial`

@export Under section 492(1), may a person executing a warrant issued under section 487 seize an explosive substance of mine that they suspect is intended for an unlawful purpose?
GIVEN `the person is executing a warrant issued under section 487` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the person executing a warrant issued under section 487?
      `the thing is an explosive substance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the thing an explosive substance?
      `the person suspects it is intended for an unlawful purpose` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the person suspect that it is intended to be used for an unlawful purpose?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 492(1), may the person executing a warrant seize my explosive substance` IS
    `s 492(1) the person may seize the thing` `the person is executing a warrant issued under section 487` (JUST `the thing is an explosive substance`) `the person suspects it is intended for an unlawful purpose`

@export Under section 492(2), is an explosive substance seized from me forfeited if I am convicted of an offence in respect of it?
GIVEN `I am convicted of an offence in respect of the thing seized` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you convicted of an offence in respect of the thing seized under subsection (1)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 492(2), is the thing seized forfeited on my conviction` IS
    `s 492(2) the thing is forfeited and shall be dealt with as the court directs` `I am convicted of an offence in respect of the thing seized`

§ `Tracking device and transmission data warrants (sections 492.1 and 492.2)`
-- Only if a warrant to track you, your vehicle or your communications has been sought or issued: ask 'Has this happened to you?' before showing this group.

@export Under section 492.1(1), may a justice or judge issue a warrant authorizing the tracking of a transaction, or the location or movement of a thing of mine, including a vehicle?
GIVEN `the issuer is a justice or a judge` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the person issuing the warrant a justice or a judge?
      `the justice or judge is satisfied by information on oath that there are reasonable grounds to suspect an offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the justice or judge is satisfied by information on oath that there are reasonable grounds to suspect that an offence has been or will be committed under the Code or any other Act of Parliament?
      `the justice or judge is satisfied that tracking will assist in the investigation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the justice or judge is satisfied that tracking the location of transactions or the location or movement of the thing will assist in the investigation of the offence?
      `the warrant authorizes a peace officer or a public officer to obtain the tracking data by a tracking device` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the warrant authorize a peace officer or a public officer to obtain that tracking data by means of a tracking device?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 492.1(1), may a justice or judge issue a warrant to track a transaction or a thing of mine` IS
    `s 492.1(1) the justice or judge may issue a warrant for a tracking device` `the issuer is a justice or a judge` `the justice or judge is satisfied by information on oath that there are reasonable grounds to suspect an offence` `the justice or judge is satisfied that tracking will assist in the investigation` `the warrant authorizes a peace officer or a public officer to obtain the tracking data by a tracking device`

@export Under section 492.1(2), may a justice or judge issue a warrant authorizing the tracking of my movement by identifying the location of a thing I usually carry or wear?
GIVEN `the issuer is a justice or a judge` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the person issuing the warrant a justice or a judge?
      `the justice or judge is satisfied by information on oath that there are reasonable grounds to believe an offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the justice or judge is satisfied by information on oath that there are reasonable grounds to believe that an offence has been or will be committed under the Code or any other Act of Parliament?
      `the justice or judge is satisfied that tracking my movement will assist in the investigation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the justice or judge is satisfied that tracking your movement by identifying the location of a thing you usually carry or wear will assist in the investigation of the offence?
      `the warrant authorizes a peace officer or a public officer to obtain the tracking data by a tracking device` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the warrant authorize a peace officer or a public officer to obtain that tracking data by means of a tracking device?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 492.1(2), may a justice or judge issue a warrant to track me as an individual` IS
    `s 492.1(2) the justice or judge may issue a warrant for a tracking device, for an individual` `the issuer is a justice or a judge` `the justice or judge is satisfied by information on oath that there are reasonable grounds to believe an offence` `the justice or judge is satisfied that tracking my movement will assist in the investigation` `the warrant authorizes a peace officer or a public officer to obtain the tracking data by a tracking device`

@export Under section 492.1(5), is a tracking device warrant valid for the period specified in it because that period ends no more than 60 days after the day it was issued (a longer period is for subsection (6))?
GIVEN `the day the warrant was issued` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the warrant issued?
      `the day the period specified in the warrant ends` IS A DATE @desc On what day does the period specified in the warrant end?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 492.1(5), is a tracking device warrant valid for the period it specifies because that period ends within 60 days` IS
    `s 492.1(5) the warrant is valid for the period specified in it` `the day the warrant was issued` `the day the period specified in the warrant ends`

@export Under section 492.1(6), is a tracking device warrant valid for the period it specifies, up to one year, because it relates to a criminal organization or terrorism offence?
GIVEN `the day the warrant was issued` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the warrant issued?
      `the day the period specified in the warrant ends` IS A DATE @desc On what day does the period specified in the warrant end?
      `the section number of the offence` IS A NUMBER @desc Under which section of the Criminal Code is the offence? (A number, for example 467.11.)
      `the offence was committed for the benefit of, at the direction of, or in association with a criminal organization` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the offence committed for the benefit of, at the direction of, or in association with a criminal organization?
      `it is a terrorism offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it a terrorism offence?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 492.1(6), is a tracking device warrant valid for up to one year for an organized crime or terrorism offence` IS
    `s 492.1(6) the warrant is valid for the period specified in it, for organized crime and terrorism offences` `the day the warrant was issued` `the day the period specified in the warrant ends` (`Provision of the Code` OF `the section number of the offence`, "") `the offence was committed for the benefit of, at the direction of, or in association with a criminal organization` (JUST `it is a terrorism offence`)

@export Under section 492.2(1), may a justice or judge issue a warrant authorizing a transmission data recorder to obtain the dialling, routing, addressing or signalling data of my communications?
GIVEN `the issuer is a justice or a judge` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the person issuing the warrant a justice or a judge?
      `the justice or judge is satisfied by information on oath that there are reasonable grounds to suspect an offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the justice or judge is satisfied by information on oath that there are reasonable grounds to suspect that an offence has been or will be committed against the Code or any other Act of Parliament?
      `the justice or judge is satisfied that transmission data will assist in the investigation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the justice or judge is satisfied that transmission data will assist in the investigation of the offence?
      `the warrant authorizes a peace officer or a public officer to obtain the transmission data by a transmission data recorder` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the warrant authorize a peace officer or a public officer to obtain the transmission data by means of a transmission data recorder?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 492.2(1), may a justice or judge issue a warrant for a transmission data recorder on my communications` IS
    `s 492.2(1) the justice or judge may issue a warrant for a transmission data recorder` `the issuer is a justice or a judge` `the justice or judge is satisfied by information on oath that there are reasonable grounds to suspect an offence` `the justice or judge is satisfied that transmission data will assist in the investigation` `the warrant authorizes a peace officer or a public officer to obtain the transmission data by a transmission data recorder`

@export Under section 492.2(3), is a warrant for a transmission data recorder barred because its purpose is to obtain tracking data?
GIVEN `the purpose of the warrant is to obtain tracking data` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the purpose of the warrant to obtain tracking data?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 492.2(3), is a transmission data recorder warrant barred because its purpose is to obtain tracking data` IS
    `s 492.2(3) no warrant shall be issued under this section` `the purpose of the warrant is to obtain tracking data`

@export Under section 492.2(4), is a transmission data recorder warrant valid for the period specified in it because that period ends no more than 60 days after the day it was issued (a longer period is for subsection (5))?
GIVEN `the day the warrant was issued` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the warrant issued?
      `the day the period specified in the warrant ends` IS A DATE @desc On what day does the period specified in the warrant end?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 492.2(4), is a transmission data recorder warrant valid for the period it specifies because that period ends within 60 days` IS
    `s 492.2(4) the warrant is valid for the period specified in it` `the day the warrant was issued` `the day the period specified in the warrant ends`

-- ====================================================================================================
-- seat questions from part ccc19 (merged by the lead on 2026-11-12; unchanged text follows)
-- ====================================================================================================
-- ccc19-seat.l4 -- LAYER 2, the seat layer, over ccc19's layer-1 modules: Criminal Code (Canada), R.S.C., 1985, c. C-46, PART XV.1 Unreasonable Delay, ss 492.21 to 492.31.
--
-- SEAT: a person who is, or may be, accused of an offence under the Criminal Code (the accused), asking, on facts they can state, whether what they did is an offence, what its gravity and penalty are,
-- what defences, rights and procedures apply at each stage from arrest through trial, sentence and appeal, and what follows from a conviction. (Assumed, not ruled: Singapore's Penal Code is the model,
-- as the maintainer instructed on 2026-11-09, but this is a plain seat layer; no charge-generator contract.) This range answers the part of that seat about DELAY: what the court must count and must not count against
-- the Crown when it decides whether the accused has waited too long for trial, who must tell the victims, what stops a court staying the case, and what the court must weigh before it does.
-- Part XV.1 does not itself say who may apply for a finding of unreasonable delay, or when a stay is the right remedy; it governs how the application is decided (see NOTES.md).
--
-- Nothing here is new law. Every question calls a layer-1 rule with the facts the person states; no rule is restated. Every question takes plain inputs, one for each fact the person can state, each with an @desc
-- worded as the person would be asked. Where a rule's input is a section 2 term (the clerk of the court) or a judgment the text gives to the court (the court is satisfied; the court finds a factor relevant;
-- the court has determined a period is attributable to late filing), it is asked as a fact. A date asked of a person who may not have done the thing (filed a copy; had an objection decided) is paired with a yes/no,
-- and the date is ignored when the answer is no. Dates are `Date day month year` in the layer-1 rules and ISO dates (YYYY-MM-DD) at the boundary.
-- Every answer is a MAYBE BOOLEAN: JUST TRUE (yes, on the text's own words), JUST FALSE (no, in the text's own words), or NOTHING (not covered: the text is silent or limits itself, or the case leaves the text).
-- A caller must never read NOTHING as no. A question named for a bar ("is the court barred ...") answers JUST TRUE for "barred"; its JUST FALSE means only that this section does not bar it, not that the court may.
-- Each question is named for what its answer says: "must" for a duty, "is barred" for a bar, "does ... deprive / preclude" for a negative, and so on, in the same direction as the layer-1 rule it calls.
-- Every question begins "Under section N," so that no two parts' questions share a name and each states the scope its answer claims.
-- A question's name does not state as a given a condition its rule tests and answers JUST FALSE when it fails (that "no" would read as "not barred even where ..."): the condition is an input, asked in its @desc.
-- Assumed, not ruled: the phrase that names the act itself stays in the name ("in deciding unreasonable delay", ss 492.27-492.29; "in deciding whether a remedy other than a stay ... is appropriate and just", s 492.31(2); "in determining the days ...", s 492.3).

§ `ccc19: Part XV.1 Unreasonable Delay -- the accused's questions`

§ `Part XV.1 Unreasonable Delay: what counts as unreasonable delay, who keeps jurisdiction, and when a stay is possible`
-- Arises only if delay is being raised in your case: ask first "Has an application for a finding of unreasonable delay been, or is it about to be, filed in your case?"

-- s 492.21 "unreasonable delay": a delay that exceeds the reasonable time to be tried under paragraph 11(b) of the Charter. The Charter's answer is the fact you state.
@ref Criminal Code s 492.21
@export Under section 492.21, is the delay in my case an unreasonable delay for the purposes of Part XV.1?
GIVEN `the delay in my case exceeds the reasonable time for me to be tried in accordance with paragraph 11(b) of the Charter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the delay in your case exceed the reasonable time for you, as a person charged with an offence, to be tried in accordance with paragraph 11(b) of the Canadian Charter of Rights and Freedoms?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 492.21, is the delay in my case an unreasonable delay for the purposes of Part XV.1` MEANS
    `s 492.21 unreasonable delay` (JUST `the delay in my case exceeds the reasonable time for me to be tried in accordance with paragraph 11(b) of the Charter`)

-- s 492.21 "court": a court seized of an application for a determination of unreasonable delay.
@ref Criminal Code s 492.21
@export Under section 492.21, is the body I am before a court for the purposes of Part XV.1?
GIVEN `the body is a court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the body you are before a court?
      `the court is seized of an application for a determination of unreasonable delay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it seized of an application for a determination of unreasonable delay?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 492.21, is the body I am before a court for the purposes of Part XV.1` MEANS
    `s 492.21 court` `the body is a court` `the court is seized of an application for a determination of unreasonable delay`

-- s 492.22: a finding of unreasonable delay does not deprive a court seized with the proceedings of jurisdiction over the offence, the accused or the offender.
@ref Criminal Code s 492.22
@export Under section 492.22, does a finding of unreasonable delay deprive the court seized with my proceedings of jurisdiction?
GIVEN `a court has made a finding of unreasonable delay in respect of the proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a court made a finding of unreasonable delay in respect of your proceedings?
      `the court asked about is seized with those proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the court you are asking about seized with those proceedings?
      matter IS A `s 492.22 matter of jurisdiction` @desc Jurisdiction over what? One of: `s 492.22 the offence`, `s 492.22 the accused`, `s 492.22 the offender`, `s 492.22 anything else`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 492.22, does a finding of unreasonable delay deprive the court seized with my proceedings of jurisdiction` MEANS
    `s 492.22 a finding of unreasonable delay deprives the court of jurisdiction` `a court has made a finding of unreasonable delay in respect of the proceedings` `the court asked about is seized with those proceedings` matter

-- s 492.23: a bar with an exception. TRUE = barred; FALSE = this section does not bar it (the stay is in accordance with Part XV.1), which is not a grant of a stay.
@ref Criminal Code s 492.23
@export Under section 492.23, is the court barred from ordering this stay of proceedings as a result of a finding of unreasonable delay?
GIVEN `the stay of proceedings would be ordered as a result of a finding of unreasonable delay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Would the stay of proceedings be ordered as a result of a finding of unreasonable delay (and not on some other ground)?
      `the stay would be ordered in accordance with this Part` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Would the stay be ordered in accordance with Part XV.1 (Unreasonable Delay) of the Criminal Code?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 492.23, is the court barred from ordering this stay of proceedings as a result of a finding of unreasonable delay` MEANS
    `s 492.23 the court is barred from ordering a stay of proceedings` `the stay of proceedings would be ordered as a result of a finding of unreasonable delay` `the stay would be ordered in accordance with this Part`

-- s 492.24: common-law rules and principles continue to apply except insofar as altered by, or inconsistent with, Part XV.1. Asked of one rule, and of the part of it in issue.
@ref Criminal Code s 492.24
@export Under section 492.24, does a rule or principle of the common law in respect of a determination of unreasonable delay continue to apply?
GIVEN `it is a rule or principle of the common law in respect of a determination of unreasonable delay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the rule or principle you are asking about a rule or principle of the common law in respect of a determination of unreasonable delay?
      `the part in issue is altered by this Part` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the part of it in issue altered by Part XV.1?
      `the part in issue is inconsistent with this Part` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the part of it in issue inconsistent with Part XV.1?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 492.24, does a rule or principle of the common law in respect of a determination of unreasonable delay continue to apply` MEANS
    `s 492.24 the rule or principle of the common law continues to apply` `it is a rule or principle of the common law in respect of a determination of unreasonable delay` `the part in issue is altered by this Part` `the part in issue is inconsistent with this Part`

§ `Notice to victims (s 492.25)`
-- Arises only once an application for a finding of unreasonable delay has been filed in your case.

-- s 492.25(1): a duty of the prosecutor, as soon as feasible, on the filing of the application. The duty is the prosecutor's; it is asked here because of what it means for the application you file. Whether there is a victim is worked out by the section 2 definition (`s 2 victim`) from the three facts about the person; its fourth input, whether the purpose is section 672.5, 722 or 745.63, is fixed FALSE here because the purpose is section 492.25.
@ref Criminal Code s 492.25(1)
@export Under section 492.25(1), must the prosecutor take reasonable steps to inform the victims of the filing of an application for a finding of unreasonable delay?
GIVEN `an application for a finding of unreasonable delay is filed with the court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is an application for a finding of unreasonable delay filed with the court?
      `an offence has been committed, or is alleged to have been committed, against the person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Think of the person who may be the victim. Has an offence been committed, or is it alleged to have been committed, against that person?
      `the person has suffered, or is alleged to have suffered, physical or emotional harm, property damage or economic loss as the result of the commission or alleged commission of the offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has that person suffered, or is it alleged that they suffered, physical or emotional harm, property damage or economic loss as the result of the offence?
      `the person has suffered physical or emotional harm, property damage or economic loss as the result of the commission of an offence against any other person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has that person suffered physical or emotional harm, property damage or economic loss as the result of an offence committed against some other person?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 492.25(1), must the prosecutor take reasonable steps to inform the victims of the filing of an application for a finding of unreasonable delay` MEANS
    `s 492.25(1) the prosecutor must take reasonable steps to inform the victims of the filing` `an application for a finding of unreasonable delay is filed with the court` `the answer of s 2 victim`
    WHERE
        `the answer of s 2 victim` MEANS
            `s 2 victim`
                `an offence has been committed, or is alleged to have been committed, against the person`
                `the person has suffered, or is alleged to have suffered, physical or emotional harm, property damage or economic loss as the result of the commission or alleged commission of the offence`
                FALSE
                `the person has suffered physical or emotional harm, property damage or economic loss as the result of the commission of an offence against any other person`

-- s 492.25(2): a duty of the court at the hearing of the application.
@ref Criminal Code s 492.25(2)
@export Under section 492.25(2), must the court inquire of the prosecutor whether reasonable steps were taken to inform the victims of the filing of the application?
GIVEN `the application for a finding of unreasonable delay is being heard` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the application for a finding of unreasonable delay in your case being heard now?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 492.25(2), must the court inquire of the prosecutor whether reasonable steps were taken to inform the victims of the filing of the application` MEANS
    `s 492.25(2) the court must inquire of the prosecutor whether reasonable steps were taken to inform the victims` `the application for a finding of unreasonable delay is being heard`

-- s 492.25(3): a duty of the prosecutor after the decision. Whether there are victims is worked out by `s 2 victim` as for s 492.25(1) (its purpose input fixed FALSE: the purpose is section 492.25).
@ref Criminal Code s 492.25(3)
@export Under section 492.25(3), must the prosecutor take reasonable steps to inform the victims of the decision on my application?
GIVEN `a decision has been made on the application` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a decision been made on your application?
      `an offence has been committed, or is alleged to have been committed, against the person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Think of the person who may be the victim. Has an offence been committed, or is it alleged to have been committed, against that person?
      `the person has suffered, or is alleged to have suffered, physical or emotional harm, property damage or economic loss as the result of the commission or alleged commission of the offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has that person suffered, or is it alleged that they suffered, physical or emotional harm, property damage or economic loss as the result of the offence?
      `the person has suffered physical or emotional harm, property damage or economic loss as the result of the commission of an offence against any other person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has that person suffered physical or emotional harm, property damage or economic loss as the result of an offence committed against some other person?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 492.25(3), must the prosecutor take reasonable steps to inform the victims of the decision on my application` MEANS
    `s 492.25(3) the prosecutor must take reasonable steps to inform the victims of the decision` `a decision has been made on the application` `the answer of s 2 victim`
    WHERE
        `the answer of s 2 victim` MEANS
            `s 2 victim`
                `an offence has been committed, or is alleged to have been committed, against the person`
                `the person has suffered, or is alleged to have suffered, physical or emotional harm, property damage or economic loss as the result of the commission or alleged commission of the offence`
                FALSE
                `the person has suffered physical or emotional harm, property damage or economic loss as the result of the commission of an offence against any other person`

-- s 492.25(4): FALSE = the failure does not preclude the court from deciding your application.
@ref Criminal Code s 492.25(4)
@export Under section 492.25(4), does the prosecutor's failure to take reasonable steps to inform the victims of my application preclude the court from making a decision on it?
GIVEN `the prosecutor failed to take reasonable steps to inform the victims, if any, of the application` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the prosecutor fail to take reasonable steps to inform the victims, if there are any, of your application?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 492.25(4), does the prosecutor's failure to take reasonable steps to inform the victims of my application preclude the court from making a decision on it` MEANS
    `s 492.25(4) the failure of the prosecutor precludes the court from making a decision on the application` `the prosecutor failed to take reasonable steps to inform the victims, if any, of the application`

§ `How complex the case is (s 492.26)`
-- Arises only while the court is deciding whether there has been or will be unreasonable delay in your case.

-- s 492.26(1): a duty of the court in determining unreasonable delay.
@ref Criminal Code s 492.26(1)
@export Under section 492.26(1), must the court consider whether any relevant factors contributed or will contribute to making my case complex?
GIVEN `the court is deciding whether there has been or will be unreasonable delay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the court deciding, in your case, whether there has been or will be unreasonable delay?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 492.26(1), must the court consider whether any relevant factors contributed or will contribute to making my case complex` MEANS
    `s 492.26(1) the court must consider whether any relevant factors contributed or will contribute to making the case complex` `the court is deciding whether there has been or will be unreasonable delay`

-- s 492.26(2): where the case involved or will involve applications or motions; paragraph (h) is any factor the court finds relevant, which is the fact you state.
@ref Criminal Code s 492.26(2)
@export Under section 492.26(2), must the court also consider this factor in assessing whether my case is complex?
GIVEN `the case involved or will involve applications or motions, either before, during or after the trial` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did your case involve, or will it involve, applications or motions, either before, during or after the trial?
      factor IS A `s 492.26(2) factor` @desc Which factor? One of: `s 492.26(2)(a) the number of applications or motions`, `s 492.26(2)(b) whether any of the applications or motions required or will require scheduling court dates separately and in advance of trial dates`, `s 492.26(2)(c) whether any adjournments were or will be required in order to complete the steps required by any of the applications or motions`, `s 492.26(2)(d) whether more than one judicial decision was or will be needed to complete the steps required by any of the applications or motions`, `s 492.26(2)(e) the amount of cumulative court time that was or will be required to adjudicate the applications or motions`, `s 492.26(2)(f) the need for trial continuation dates arising from the applications or motions that required more time than anticipated`, `s 492.26(2)(g) the need for trial continuation dates arising from the applications or motions that were not scheduled in advance of the trial`, `s 492.26(2)(h) another factor, not named in paragraphs (a) to (g)`
      `the court finds the factor relevant in assessing the complexity of the applications or motions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the factor is another factor, has the court found it relevant in assessing the complexity of the applications or motions? (Answer false if the factor is one of (a) to (g).)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 492.26(2), must the court also consider this factor in assessing whether my case is complex` MEANS
    `s 492.26(2) the court must also consider the factor in assessing whether the case is complex` `the case involved or will involve applications or motions, either before, during or after the trial` factor `the court finds the factor relevant in assessing the complexity of the applications or motions`

§ `Days the court may not count against the delay (ss 492.27 to 492.3)`
-- Arises only while the court is deciding whether there has been or will be unreasonable delay in your case, and only where one of the applications or objections listed was made.

-- s 492.27(a): TRUE = the court shall not take the day into account. Whether section 492.3 applies is worked out by calling that section's rule with the facts below.
@ref Criminal Code s 492.27(a)
@export Under section 492.27(a), is the court barred from taking this day into account in deciding unreasonable delay, under the rule for an application made under section 276.01?
GIVEN `the court is deciding whether there has been or will be unreasonable delay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the court deciding, in your case, whether there has been or will be unreasonable delay?
      `a frivolous or dilatory action, or an action not made in good faith, was taken in relation to this application` IS A BOOLEAN @desc In relation to this application, was there any frivolous or dilatory action, or any action not made in good faith?
      `who took that action` IS A `s 492.3 who took the action` @desc Who took that action? One of: `s 492.3 the prosecutor`, `s 492.3 counsel representing the Attorney General of Canada`, `s 492.3 a person acting on behalf of the prosecutor or the Attorney General of Canada`, `s 492.3 someone else` (choose this one too if there was no such action)
      `I filed a copy of the application with the clerk of the court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you filed a copy of your application under section 276.01 with anyone?
      `the date I filed the copy` IS A DATE @desc On what date did you file the copy? (If you have not filed one, enter any date: it is ignored.)
      `the person I filed the copy with` IS A `s 2 clerk of the court kind` @desc Who did you file it with? One of: `a clerk of the court`, `a person who, by whatever name or title they are designated, from time to time performs the duties of a clerk of the court`, `a person who neither is a clerk of the court nor performs a clerk of the court's duties` (ignored if you have not filed one)
      `the date of the hearing referred to in section 276.02` IS A DATE @desc On what date is the hearing referred to in section 276.02?
      `the kind of day` IS A `s 492.27 kind of day` @desc Which describes the day you are asking about? One of: `s 492.27(i) a day it took to hear the application`, `s 492.27(ii) a day in another period that the court has determined is attributable to the fact that a copy of the application was not filed or served at least 60 days before the hearing`, `s 492.27 a day that is in neither period`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 492.27(a), is the court barred from taking this day into account in deciding unreasonable delay, under the rule for an application made under section 276.01` MEANS
    `s 492.27(a) the court shall not take the day into account`
        `the court is deciding whether there has been or will be unreasonable delay`
        `the answer of s 492.3`
        `the date I filed, if I did`
        `the answer of s 2 clerk of the court`
        `the date of the hearing referred to in section 276.02`
        `the kind of day`
    WHERE
        `the answer of s 492.3` MEANS
            `s 492.3: the court must take into account a frivolous or dilatory action or an action not made in good faith`
                TRUE
                `a frivolous or dilatory action, or an action not made in good faith, was taken in relation to this application`
                `who took that action`
        `the date I filed, if I did` MEANS
            IF `I filed a copy of the application with the clerk of the court`
            THEN JUST `the date I filed the copy`
            ELSE NOTHING
        `the answer of s 2 clerk of the court` MEANS `s 2 clerk of the court` `the person I filed the copy with`

-- s 492.27(b): TRUE = the court shall not take the day into account.
@ref Criminal Code s 492.27(b)
@export Under section 492.27(b), is the court barred from taking this day into account in deciding unreasonable delay, under the rule for an application made under section 278.12 or 278.21?
GIVEN `the court is deciding whether there has been or will be unreasonable delay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the court deciding, in your case, whether there has been or will be unreasonable delay?
      `a frivolous or dilatory action, or an action not made in good faith, was taken in relation to this application` IS A BOOLEAN @desc In relation to this application, was there any frivolous or dilatory action, or any action not made in good faith?
      `who took that action` IS A `s 492.3 who took the action` @desc Who took that action? One of: `s 492.3 the prosecutor`, `s 492.3 counsel representing the Attorney General of Canada`, `s 492.3 a person acting on behalf of the prosecutor or the Attorney General of Canada`, `s 492.3 someone else` (choose this one too if there was no such action)
      `a copy of the application has been served on every one of the persons referred to in subsection 278.12(5) or 278.21(5)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a copy of your application under section 278.12 or 278.21 been served on every one of the persons referred to in subsection 278.12(5) or 278.21(5)?
      `the date the last of them was served` IS A DATE @desc On what date was the last of them served? (If not all have been served, enter any date: it is ignored.)
      `the date of the hearing referred to in subsection 278.13(1) or 278.22(1)` IS A DATE @desc On what date is the hearing referred to in subsection 278.13(1) or 278.22(1)?
      `the kind of day` IS A `s 492.27 kind of day` @desc Which describes the day you are asking about? One of: `s 492.27(i) a day it took to hear the application`, `s 492.27(ii) a day in another period that the court has determined is attributable to the fact that a copy of the application was not filed or served at least 60 days before the hearing`, `s 492.27 a day that is in neither period`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 492.27(b), is the court barred from taking this day into account in deciding unreasonable delay, under the rule for an application made under section 278.12 or 278.21` MEANS
    `s 492.27(b) the court shall not take the day into account`
        `the court is deciding whether there has been or will be unreasonable delay`
        `the answer of s 492.3`
        `the date a copy had been served on every one of them, if it has been`
        `the date of the hearing referred to in subsection 278.13(1) or 278.22(1)`
        `the kind of day`
    WHERE
        `the answer of s 492.3` MEANS
            `s 492.3: the court must take into account a frivolous or dilatory action or an action not made in good faith`
                TRUE
                `a frivolous or dilatory action, or an action not made in good faith, was taken in relation to this application`
                `who took that action`
        `the date a copy had been served on every one of them, if it has been` MEANS
            IF `a copy of the application has been served on every one of the persons referred to in subsection 278.12(5) or 278.21(5)`
            THEN JUST `the date the last of them was served`
            ELSE NOTHING

-- s 492.27(c): TRUE = the court shall not take the day into account.
@ref Criminal Code s 492.27(c)
@export Under section 492.27(c), is the court barred from taking this day into account in deciding unreasonable delay, under the rule for an application made under section 278.3?
GIVEN `the court is deciding whether there has been or will be unreasonable delay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the court deciding, in your case, whether there has been or will be unreasonable delay?
      `a frivolous or dilatory action, or an action not made in good faith, was taken in relation to this application` IS A BOOLEAN @desc In relation to this application, was there any frivolous or dilatory action, or any action not made in good faith?
      `who took that action` IS A `s 492.3 who took the action` @desc Who took that action? One of: `s 492.3 the prosecutor`, `s 492.3 counsel representing the Attorney General of Canada`, `s 492.3 a person acting on behalf of the prosecutor or the Attorney General of Canada`, `s 492.3 someone else` (choose this one too if there was no such action)
      `I filed a copy of the application with the clerk of the court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you filed a copy of your application under section 278.3 with anyone?
      `the date I filed the copy` IS A DATE @desc On what date did you file the copy? (If you have not filed one, enter any date: it is ignored.)
      `the person I filed the copy with` IS A `s 2 clerk of the court kind` @desc Who did you file it with? One of: `a clerk of the court`, `a person who, by whatever name or title they are designated, from time to time performs the duties of a clerk of the court`, `a person who neither is a clerk of the court nor performs a clerk of the court's duties` (ignored if you have not filed one)
      `the date of the hearing referred to in section 278.31` IS A DATE @desc On what date is the hearing referred to in section 278.31?
      `the kind of day` IS A `s 492.27 kind of day` @desc Which describes the day you are asking about? One of: `s 492.27(i) a day it took to hear the application`, `s 492.27(ii) a day in another period that the court has determined is attributable to the fact that a copy of the application was not filed or served at least 60 days before the hearing`, `s 492.27 a day that is in neither period`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 492.27(c), is the court barred from taking this day into account in deciding unreasonable delay, under the rule for an application made under section 278.3` MEANS
    `s 492.27(c) the court shall not take the day into account`
        `the court is deciding whether there has been or will be unreasonable delay`
        `the answer of s 492.3`
        `the date I filed, if I did`
        `the answer of s 2 clerk of the court`
        `the date of the hearing referred to in section 278.31`
        `the kind of day`
    WHERE
        `the answer of s 492.3` MEANS
            `s 492.3: the court must take into account a frivolous or dilatory action or an action not made in good faith`
                TRUE
                `a frivolous or dilatory action, or an action not made in good faith, was taken in relation to this application`
                `who took that action`
        `the date I filed, if I did` MEANS
            IF `I filed a copy of the application with the clerk of the court`
            THEN JUST `the date I filed the copy`
            ELSE NOTHING
        `the answer of s 2 clerk of the court` MEANS `s 2 clerk of the court` `the person I filed the copy with`

-- s 492.28(a): TRUE = the court shall not take the day into account. The period begins on the day the objection was made and ends on the day it was finally determined; both days are in it.
@ref Criminal Code s 492.28(a)
@export Under section 492.28(a), is the court barred from taking this day into account in deciding unreasonable delay, under the rule for an objection made under subsection 37(1) of the Canada Evidence Act before a superior court?
GIVEN `the court is deciding whether there has been or will be unreasonable delay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the court deciding, in your case, whether there has been or will be unreasonable delay?
      `a frivolous or dilatory action, or an action not made in good faith, was taken in relation to this application` IS A BOOLEAN @desc In relation to this application, was there any frivolous or dilatory action, or any action not made in good faith?
      `who took that action` IS A `s 492.3 who took the action` @desc Who took that action? One of: `s 492.3 the prosecutor`, `s 492.3 counsel representing the Attorney General of Canada`, `s 492.3 a person acting on behalf of the prosecutor or the Attorney General of Canada`, `s 492.3 someone else` (choose this one too if there was no such action)
      `the objection was made under subsection 37(1) of the Canada Evidence Act before a superior court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was an objection made under subsection 37(1) of the Canada Evidence Act before a superior court?
      `the day the objection was made` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the objection made?
      `the objection has been finally determined` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the objection been finally determined?
      `the day the objection was finally determined` IS A DATE @desc On what day was it finally determined? (If it has not been, enter any date: it is ignored.)
      `the day I am asking about` IS A DATE @desc Which day are you asking about?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 492.28(a), is the court barred from taking this day into account in deciding unreasonable delay, under the rule for an objection made under subsection 37(1) of the Canada Evidence Act before a superior court` MEANS
    `s 492.28(a) the court shall not take the day into account`
        `the court is deciding whether there has been or will be unreasonable delay`
        `the answer of s 492.3`
        `the objection was made under subsection 37(1) of the Canada Evidence Act before a superior court`
        `the day the objection was made`
        `the day the objection was finally determined, if it has been`
        `the day I am asking about`
    WHERE
        `the answer of s 492.3` MEANS
            `s 492.3: the court must take into account a frivolous or dilatory action or an action not made in good faith`
                TRUE
                `a frivolous or dilatory action, or an action not made in good faith, was taken in relation to this application`
                `who took that action`
        `the day the objection was finally determined, if it has been` MEANS
            IF `the objection has been finally determined`
            THEN JUST `the day the objection was finally determined`
            ELSE NOTHING

-- s 492.28(b): TRUE = the court shall not take the day into account. The period begins on the day the objection was made and ends on the day the APPLICATION was finally determined.
@ref Criminal Code s 492.28(b)
@export Under section 492.28(b), is the court barred from taking this day into account in deciding unreasonable delay, under the rule for an objection made under subsection 37(1) of the Canada Evidence Act before a court, person or body other than a superior court, with an application under subsection 37(3) in respect of it?
GIVEN `the court is deciding whether there has been or will be unreasonable delay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the court deciding, in your case, whether there has been or will be unreasonable delay?
      `a frivolous or dilatory action, or an action not made in good faith, was taken in relation to this application` IS A BOOLEAN @desc In relation to this application, was there any frivolous or dilatory action, or any action not made in good faith?
      `who took that action` IS A `s 492.3 who took the action` @desc Who took that action? One of: `s 492.3 the prosecutor`, `s 492.3 counsel representing the Attorney General of Canada`, `s 492.3 a person acting on behalf of the prosecutor or the Attorney General of Canada`, `s 492.3 someone else` (choose this one too if there was no such action)
      `an objection was made under subsection 37(1) of that Act before a court, person or body other than a superior court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was an objection made under subsection 37(1) of the Canada Evidence Act before a court, person or body other than a superior court?
      `an application was made under subsection 37(3) of that Act in respect of the objection` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was an application made under subsection 37(3) of that Act in respect of the objection?
      `the day the objection was made` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the objection made?
      `the application under subsection 37(3) has been finally determined` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the application under subsection 37(3) been finally determined?
      `the day the application under subsection 37(3) was finally determined` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the application finally determined? (If it has not been, enter any date: it is ignored.)
      `the day I am asking about` IS A DATE @desc Which day are you asking about?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 492.28(b), is the court barred from taking this day into account in deciding unreasonable delay, under the rule for an objection made under subsection 37(1) of the Canada Evidence Act before a court, person or body other than a superior court, with an application under subsection 37(3) in respect of it` MEANS
    `s 492.28(b) the court shall not take the day into account`
        `the court is deciding whether there has been or will be unreasonable delay`
        `the answer of s 492.3`
        `an objection was made under subsection 37(1) of that Act before a court, person or body other than a superior court`
        `an application was made under subsection 37(3) of that Act in respect of the objection`
        `the day the objection was made`
        `the day the application under subsection 37(3) was finally determined, if it has been`
        `the day I am asking about`
    WHERE
        `the answer of s 492.3` MEANS
            `s 492.3: the court must take into account a frivolous or dilatory action or an action not made in good faith`
                TRUE
                `a frivolous or dilatory action, or an action not made in good faith, was taken in relation to this application`
                `who took that action`
        `the day the application under subsection 37(3) was finally determined, if it has been` MEANS
            IF `the application under subsection 37(3) has been finally determined`
            THEN JUST `the day the application under subsection 37(3) was finally determined`
            ELSE NOTHING

-- s 492.28(c): TRUE = the court shall not take the day into account. The period begins on the day the application was made and ends on the day it was finally determined.
@ref Criminal Code s 492.28(c)
@export Under section 492.28(c), is the court barred from taking this day into account in deciding unreasonable delay, under the rule for an application made under subsection 38.04(1) or (2) of the Canada Evidence Act?
GIVEN `the court is deciding whether there has been or will be unreasonable delay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the court deciding, in your case, whether there has been or will be unreasonable delay?
      `a frivolous or dilatory action, or an action not made in good faith, was taken in relation to this application` IS A BOOLEAN @desc In relation to this application, was there any frivolous or dilatory action, or any action not made in good faith?
      `who took that action` IS A `s 492.3 who took the action` @desc Who took that action? One of: `s 492.3 the prosecutor`, `s 492.3 counsel representing the Attorney General of Canada`, `s 492.3 a person acting on behalf of the prosecutor or the Attorney General of Canada`, `s 492.3 someone else` (choose this one too if there was no such action)
      `an application was made under subsection 38.04(1) or (2) of that Act in respect of a notice arising from the prosecution of the offence given under any of subsections 38.01(1) to (4) of that Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was an application made under subsection 38.04(1) or (2) of the Canada Evidence Act in respect of a notice, arising from the prosecution of the offence, given under any of subsections 38.01(1) to (4)?
      `the day the application was made` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the application made?
      `the application has been finally determined` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the application been finally determined?
      `the day the application was finally determined` IS A DATE @desc On what day was it finally determined? (If it has not been, enter any date: it is ignored.)
      `the day I am asking about` IS A DATE @desc Which day are you asking about?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 492.28(c), is the court barred from taking this day into account in deciding unreasonable delay, under the rule for an application made under subsection 38.04(1) or (2) of the Canada Evidence Act` MEANS
    `s 492.28(c) the court shall not take the day into account`
        `the court is deciding whether there has been or will be unreasonable delay`
        `the answer of s 492.3`
        `an application was made under subsection 38.04(1) or (2) of that Act in respect of a notice arising from the prosecution of the offence given under any of subsections 38.01(1) to (4) of that Act`
        `the day the application was made`
        `the day the application was finally determined, if it has been`
        `the day I am asking about`
    WHERE
        `the answer of s 492.3` MEANS
            `s 492.3: the court must take into account a frivolous or dilatory action or an action not made in good faith`
                TRUE
                `a frivolous or dilatory action, or an action not made in good faith, was taken in relation to this application`
                `who took that action`
        `the day the application was finally determined, if it has been` MEANS
            IF `the application has been finally determined`
            THEN JUST `the day the application was finally determined`
            ELSE NOTHING

-- s 492.29: TRUE = the court shall not take the day into account. The period begins on the day the application was made and ends on the day it was finally determined.
@ref Criminal Code s 492.29
@export Under section 492.29, is the court barred from taking this day into account in deciding unreasonable delay, under the rule for an application made under subsection 18.1(4) of the Canadian Security Intelligence Service Act?
GIVEN `the court is deciding whether there has been or will be unreasonable delay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the court deciding, in your case, whether there has been or will be unreasonable delay?
      `a frivolous or dilatory action, or an action not made in good faith, was taken in relation to this application` IS A BOOLEAN @desc In relation to this application, was there any frivolous or dilatory action, or any action not made in good faith?
      `who took that action` IS A `s 492.3 who took the action` @desc Who took that action? One of: `s 492.3 the prosecutor`, `s 492.3 counsel representing the Attorney General of Canada`, `s 492.3 a person acting on behalf of the prosecutor or the Attorney General of Canada`, `s 492.3 someone else` (choose this one too if there was no such action)
      `an application was made under subsection 18.1(4) of the Canadian Security Intelligence Service Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was an application made under subsection 18.1(4) of the Canadian Security Intelligence Service Act?
      `the day the application was made` IS A DATE @desc On what day was the application made?
      `the application has been finally determined` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the application been finally determined?
      `the day the application was finally determined` IS A DATE @desc On what day was it finally determined? (If it has not been, enter any date: it is ignored.)
      `the day I am asking about` IS A DATE @desc Which day are you asking about?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 492.29, is the court barred from taking this day into account in deciding unreasonable delay, under the rule for an application made under subsection 18.1(4) of the Canadian Security Intelligence Service Act` MEANS
    `s 492.29 the court shall not take the day into account`
        `the court is deciding whether there has been or will be unreasonable delay`
        `the answer of s 492.3`
        `an application was made under subsection 18.1(4) of the Canadian Security Intelligence Service Act`
        `the day the application was made`
        `the day the application was finally determined, if it has been`
        `the day I am asking about`
    WHERE
        `the answer of s 492.3` MEANS
            `s 492.3: the court must take into account a frivolous or dilatory action or an action not made in good faith`
                TRUE
                `a frivolous or dilatory action, or an action not made in good faith, was taken in relation to this application`
                `who took that action`
        `the day the application was finally determined, if it has been` MEANS
            IF `the application has been finally determined`
            THEN JUST `the day the application was finally determined`
            ELSE NOTHING

-- s 492.3: a duty of the court, in relation to the applications and objections of ss 492.27 to 492.29, as to actions taken by the prosecutor, counsel for the Attorney General of Canada or someone acting for either.
@ref Criminal Code s 492.3
@export Under section 492.3, must the court take into account a frivolous or dilatory action, or an action not made in good faith, in determining the days that are not to be taken into account?
GIVEN `the days being determined are days in relation to an application or objection referred to in sections 492.27 to 492.29` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are the days being determined days in relation to an application or objection of the kinds in sections 492.27 to 492.29 (section 276.01, 278.12, 278.21 or 278.3 applications; objections or applications under the Canada Evidence Act; applications under the Canadian Security Intelligence Service Act)?
      `the action is frivolous or dilatory, or is an action not made in good faith` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the action frivolous or dilatory, or an action not made in good faith?
      `who took the action` IS A `s 492.3 who took the action` @desc Who took the action? One of: `s 492.3 the prosecutor`, `s 492.3 counsel representing the Attorney General of Canada`, `s 492.3 a person acting on behalf of the prosecutor or the Attorney General of Canada`, `s 492.3 someone else`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 492.3, must the court take into account a frivolous or dilatory action, or an action not made in good faith, in determining the days that are not to be taken into account` MEANS
    `s 492.3: the court must take into account a frivolous or dilatory action or an action not made in good faith` `the days being determined are days in relation to an application or objection referred to in sections 492.27 to 492.29` `the action is frivolous or dilatory, or is an action not made in good faith` `who took the action`

§ `Alternatives to a stay of proceedings (s 492.31)`
-- Arises only once unreasonable delay has been found, or the court is considering whether to stay your proceedings because of it.

-- s 492.31(1): TRUE = barred; FALSE = this subsection does not bar it (the court is satisfied), which is not a grant of a stay.
@ref Criminal Code s 492.31(1)
@export Under section 492.31(1), is the court barred from ordering this stay of proceedings as a result of a finding of unreasonable delay?
GIVEN `the stay of proceedings would be ordered as a result of a finding of unreasonable delay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Would the stay of proceedings be ordered as a result of a finding of unreasonable delay (and not on some other ground)?
      `the court is satisfied that no other remedy would be appropriate and just in the circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the court satisfied that no other remedy would be appropriate and just in the circumstances?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 492.31(1), is the court barred from ordering this stay of proceedings as a result of a finding of unreasonable delay` MEANS
    `s 492.31(1) the court is barred from ordering a stay of proceedings` `the stay of proceedings would be ordered as a result of a finding of unreasonable delay` `the court is satisfied that no other remedy would be appropriate and just in the circumstances`

-- s 492.31(2): the five factors, which include the prejudice you have suffered (paragraph (c)).
@ref Criminal Code s 492.31(2)
@export Under section 492.31(2), must the court take this factor into account in deciding whether a remedy other than a stay of proceedings is appropriate and just?
GIVEN `the court is determining whether a remedy other than a stay of proceedings is appropriate and just` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the court deciding whether a remedy other than a stay of proceedings is appropriate and just?
      factor IS A `s 492.31(2) factor` @desc Which factor? One of: `s 492.31(2)(a) the stage of the proceedings during which the finding of unreasonable delay is made or during which the delay became unreasonable`, `s 492.31(2)(b) the impact that a stay of proceedings is likely to have on any victim of the offence`, `s 492.31(2)(c) the prejudice that has been or would be suffered by the accused or offender as a result of unreasonable delay`, `s 492.31(2)(d) the public’s confidence in the administration of justice`, `s 492.31(2)(e) the interest that society has in having a final decision on the merits`, `s 492.31(2) another factor, not named in paragraphs (a) to (e)`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 492.31(2), must the court take this factor into account in deciding whether a remedy other than a stay of proceedings is appropriate and just` MEANS
    `s 492.31(2) the court must take the factor into account` `the court is determining whether a remedy other than a stay of proceedings is appropriate and just` factor

-- ====================================================================================================
-- seat questions from part ccc20 (merged by the lead on 2026-11-12; unchanged text follows)
-- ====================================================================================================
-- ccc20-seat.l4 -- THE SEAT LAYER for Part XVI of the Criminal Code (Canada), R.S.C., 1985, c. C-46, sections 493 to 529.5
-- (compelling appearance of an accused before a justice, and interim release).
--
-- SEAT: a person who is, or may be, accused of an offence under the Criminal Code, asking what applies to them from arrest to release or detention.
-- (The brief's seat is wider, running from arrest to appeal; this layer asks only what Part XVI answers, which is the stage from arrest to release or detention.)
--
-- Every question takes plain inputs, one for each fact the person can state, each with a @desc worded as the person would be asked. The layer-1 modules ccc20-part16-*.l4 hold the rules; this layer only
-- builds their arguments from the answers and calls them, and restates no rule. Every question name begins "Under section N," with the Act's own numbering, so that no two parts' questions share a name.
-- A question answers JUST TRUE (yes), JUST FALSE (the text says no in its own words) or NOTHING (not covered by the text: not "no"); a caller never turns NOTHING into "no".
-- A question about a bar or a duty is titled as the bar or the duty, in the direction its function answers; a permission is titled as a permission.
-- Provisions that address only an official (the forms and directions of a justice or judge, the custodian's delivery of a prisoner) are in layer 1 only; the seat asks only what they mean to the person.
-- The comment above each question paraphrases the section (no quotation of it: the layer-1 modules carry the quoted text and its source lines).
-- Where the person cannot be expected to know the answer of another section (the reverse onus, for example) the question asks yes / no / I do not know and carries "I do not know" as NOTHING.

DECLARE `Under section 493.1 who decides` IS ONE OF
    `Under section 493.1 a peace officer decides`
    `Under section 493.1 a justice decides`
    `Under section 493.1 a judge decides`

DECLARE `Under section 515 an answer the person may not know` IS ONE OF
    `Under section 515 yes`
    `Under section 515 no`
    `Under section 515 I do not know`

-- helpers: the person's statement as the MAYBE BOOLEAN answer of another section (an input the person states is not a rule's answer)
GIVEN who IS AN `Under section 493.1 who decides`
GIVETH A Party
`Under section 493.1 helper: the decision-maker as a Party` MEANS
    CONSIDER who
    WHEN `Under section 493.1 a peace officer decides` THEN `a peace officer`
    WHEN `Under section 493.1 a justice decides` THEN `a justice`
    WHEN `Under section 493.1 a judge decides` THEN `a judge`

GIVEN answer IS AN `Under section 515 an answer the person may not know`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under section 515 helper: the answer of a section the person may not know` MEANS
    CONSIDER answer
    WHEN `Under section 515 yes` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `Under section 515 no` THEN JUST FALSE
    WHEN `Under section 515 I do not know` THEN NOTHING

GIVEN fact IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Under Part XVI helper: a stated fact as an answer` MEANS
    IF fact THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

§ `The accused's questions on Part XVI of the Criminal Code (Canada)`

§§ `Definitions and the principles every decision-maker must follow (ss 493 to 493.2)`

-- s 493, the definition of accused. In Part XVI an accused includes a person to whom a peace officer has issued an appearance notice under s 497 and a person arrested for a criminal offence. JUST TRUE: you are one of those. NOTHING: neither applies; the definition says includes, so the text does not say that you are not an accused.
@export Under section 493, am I an accused within the meaning of Part XVI?
GIVEN `a peace officer has issued me an appearance notice under section 497` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a peace officer issued you an appearance notice under section 497?
      `I was arrested for a criminal offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you arrested for a criminal offence?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 493, am I an accused within the meaning of Part XVI` IS
    `s 493 the person is an accused within the definition` `a peace officer has issued me an appearance notice under section 497` `I was arrested for a criminal offence`

-- ss 493.1 and 493.11(2)(c). In a decision under Part XVI the peace officer, justice or judge must give primary consideration to release at the earliest reasonable opportunity and on the least onerous conditions that are appropriate. A justice or judge must not do so where subsection 515(6), 522(2), 523(2.1) or 524(4) applies to the accused. JUST TRUE: yes. JUST FALSE: no decision under this Part is being made, or a justice or judge faces a reverse-onus case. NOTHING: whether a reverse-onus rule applies is not known.
@export Under section 493.1, must the peace officer, justice or judge give primary consideration to my release at the earliest reasonable opportunity and on the least onerous conditions that are appropriate in the circumstances?
GIVEN `who is making the decision about me` IS AN `Under section 493.1 who decides` @desc Who is making the decision about your arrest, release or detention: a peace officer, a justice or a judge?
      `a decision about me is being made under Part XVI` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is a decision about your arrest, release or detention being made under Part XVI of the Criminal Code?
      `whether a reverse onus under section 515(6), 522(2), 523(2.1) or 524(4) applies to me` IS AN `Under section 515 an answer the person may not know` @desc Does one of the reverse-onus rules (subsection 515(6), 522(2), 523(2.1) or 524(4)) apply to you?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 493.1, must the peace officer, justice or judge give primary consideration to my release at the earliest reasonable opportunity and on the least onerous conditions that are appropriate in the circumstances` IS
    `s 493.1 the peace officer, justice or judge must give primary consideration to the release of the accused at the earliest reasonable opportunity and on the least onerous conditions that are appropriate in the circumstances` maker `a decision about me is being made under Part XVI` (`s 493.11(2)(c) the justice or judge is barred from giving primary consideration to the release of the accused at the earliest reasonable opportunity` maker (LIST (`Under section 515 helper: the answer of a section the person may not know` `whether a reverse onus under section 515(6), 522(2), 523(2.1) or 524(4) applies to me`)))
    WHERE
        maker MEANS `Under section 493.1 helper: the decision-maker as a Party` `who is making the decision about me`

-- s 493.11(2)(e). A justice or judge must not order release if detention is justified under subsection 515(10). JUST TRUE: barred. JUST FALSE: none of the three grounds holds, so the bar is not engaged. NOTHING: the decision-maker is a peace officer, to whom this paragraph does not speak.
@export Under section 493.11(2)(e), is the justice or judge barred from ordering that I be released?
GIVEN `who is making the decision about me` IS AN `Under section 493.1 who decides` @desc Who is making the decision about your arrest, release or detention: a peace officer, a justice or a judge?
      `my detention is necessary to ensure my attendance in court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your detention necessary to ensure that you attend court to be dealt with according to law?
      `my detention is necessary for the protection or safety of the public` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your detention necessary for the protection or safety of the public, including any victim of or witness to the offence?
      `my detention is necessary to maintain confidence in the administration of justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your detention necessary to maintain confidence in the administration of justice?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 493.11(2)(e), is the justice or judge barred from ordering that I be released` IS
    `s 493.11(2)(e) the justice or judge is barred from ordering that the accused be released` maker (`s 515(10) the detention of the accused in custody can be justified on one of the three grounds` `my detention is necessary to ensure my attendance in court` `my detention is necessary for the protection or safety of the public` `my detention is necessary to maintain confidence in the administration of justice`)
    WHERE
        maker MEANS `Under section 493.1 helper: the decision-maker as a Party` `who is making the decision about me`

-- s 493.2. In making any decision under Part XVI a peace officer, justice or judge must give particular attention to the circumstances of Aboriginal accused, and of accused who belong to a vulnerable population that is overrepresented in the criminal justice system and disadvantaged in obtaining release. JUST TRUE: the duty is owed to you. JUST FALSE: no decision under this Part is being made. NOTHING: you are in neither group, which the text does not address.
@export Under section 493.2, must the peace officer, justice or judge give particular attention to my circumstances?
GIVEN `who is making the decision about me` IS AN `Under section 493.1 who decides` @desc Who is making the decision about your arrest, release or detention: a peace officer, a justice or a judge?
      `a decision about me is being made under Part XVI` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is a decision about your arrest, release or detention being made under Part XVI of the Criminal Code?
      `I am an Aboriginal accused` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you an Aboriginal accused?
      `I belong to a vulnerable population that is overrepresented in the criminal justice system and disadvantaged in obtaining release` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you belong to a vulnerable population that is overrepresented in the criminal justice system and that is disadvantaged in obtaining release under Part XVI?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 493.2, must the peace officer, justice or judge give particular attention to my circumstances` IS
    `s 493.2 the peace officer, justice or judge must give particular attention to the circumstances of the accused` (`Under section 493.1 helper: the decision-maker as a Party` `who is making the decision about me`) `a decision about me is being made under Part XVI` (`s 493.2(a) the accused is an Aboriginal accused, to whose circumstances particular attention must be given` `I am an Aboriginal accused`) (`s 493.2(b) the accused belongs to a vulnerable population, to whose circumstances particular attention must be given` `I belong to a vulnerable population that is overrepresented in the criminal justice system and disadvantaged in obtaining release`)

§§ `Arrest without a warrant and release from custody (ss 494 to 499)`

-- s 494(1). Any one may arrest without warrant a person found committing an indictable offence, or a person believed on reasonable grounds to have committed a criminal offence and to be escaping from, and freshly pursued by, persons with lawful authority to arrest. JUST TRUE: yes. NOTHING: neither limb is met; the grant is limited to those two cases and the text does not say that no other arrest is allowed.
@export Under section 494(1), may any one arrest me without a warrant?
GIVEN `the kind of offence I am said to have committed` IS A `Class of offence` @desc What kind of offence is it said to be: an indictable offence, an offence punishable on summary conviction, or an offence that the section makes both indictable and punishable on summary conviction?
      `the prosecutor has elected to proceed by way of summary conviction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the prosecutor elected to proceed against you by way of summary conviction?
      `I was found committing the offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the person arresting you find you committing the offence?
      `the arrester believes on reasonable grounds that I have committed a criminal offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the person arresting you believe on reasonable grounds that you have committed a criminal offence?
      `the arrester believes on reasonable grounds that I am escaping from and freshly pursued by persons who have lawful authority to arrest me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the person arresting you believe on reasonable grounds that you are escaping from, and freshly pursued by, persons who have lawful authority to arrest you for that offence?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 494(1), may any one arrest me without a warrant` IS
    `s 494(1) any one may arrest the person without warrant` offence `I was found committing the offence` `the arrester believes on reasonable grounds that I have committed a criminal offence` `the arrester believes on reasonable grounds that I am escaping from and freshly pursued by persons who have lawful authority to arrest me`
    WHERE
        offence MEANS `s 493.01 the offence charged` OF `the kind of offence I am said to have committed`, `the prosecutor has elected to proceed by way of summary conviction`

-- s 495(1). A peace officer may arrest without warrant a person who has committed, or is believed on reasonable grounds to have committed or to be about to commit, an indictable offence; a person found committing a criminal offence; or a person for whom a warrant of arrest or committal is believed on reasonable grounds to be in force where found. JUST TRUE: yes. NOTHING: none of the three limbs is met.
@export Under section 495(1), may a peace officer arrest me without a warrant?
GIVEN `the kind of offence I am said to have committed` IS A `Class of offence` @desc What kind of offence is it said to be: an indictable offence, an offence punishable on summary conviction, or an offence that the section makes both indictable and punishable on summary conviction?
      `the prosecutor has elected to proceed by way of summary conviction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the prosecutor elected to proceed against you by way of summary conviction?
      `I have committed the offence, or the officer believes on reasonable grounds that I have committed it or am about to commit it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you committed the offence, or does the officer believe on reasonable grounds that you have committed it or are about to commit it?
      `the officer finds me committing a criminal offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the officer find you committing a criminal offence?
      `the officer has reasonable grounds to believe that a warrant for my arrest or committal is in force where I am found` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the officer have reasonable grounds to believe that a warrant for your arrest or committal is in force where you are found?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 495(1), may a peace officer arrest me without a warrant` IS
    `s 495(1) the peace officer may arrest the person without warrant` offence `I have committed the offence, or the officer believes on reasonable grounds that I have committed it or am about to commit it` `the officer finds me committing a criminal offence` `the officer has reasonable grounds to believe that a warrant for my arrest or committal is in force where I am found`
    WHERE
        offence MEANS `s 493.01 the offence charged` OF `the kind of offence I am said to have committed`, `the prosecutor has elected to proceed by way of summary conviction`

-- s 495(2). A peace officer must not arrest without warrant for an indictable offence mentioned in s 553, an offence that may be prosecuted by indictment or punished on summary conviction, or an offence punishable on summary conviction, if the officer believes on reasonable grounds that the public interest may be satisfied without arresting and has no reasonable grounds to believe the person will fail to attend court. JUST TRUE: barred. JUST FALSE: one of the two conditions fails, so the bar is not engaged. NOTHING: the offence is an indictable offence not mentioned in s 553, which the three limbs do not reach.
@export Under section 495(2), is the peace officer barred from arresting me without a warrant?
GIVEN `the kind of offence I am said to have committed` IS A `Class of offence` @desc What kind of offence is it said to be: an indictable offence, an offence punishable on summary conviction, or an offence that the section makes both indictable and punishable on summary conviction?
      `the offence is an indictable offence mentioned in section 553` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the offence one of the indictable offences mentioned in section 553?
      `the officer believes on reasonable grounds that the public interest may be satisfied without arresting me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the officer believe on reasonable grounds that the public interest (the need to establish your identity, to secure or preserve evidence, or to prevent the offence from continuing or being repeated) may be satisfied without arresting you?
      `the officer has no reasonable grounds to believe that I will fail to attend court if not arrested` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the officer have no reasonable grounds to believe that you will fail to attend court if you are not arrested?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 495(2), is the peace officer barred from arresting me without a warrant` IS
    `s 495(2) the peace officer is barred from arresting the person without warrant` `the kind of offence I am said to have committed` (`Under Part XVI helper: a stated fact as an answer` `the offence is an indictable offence mentioned in section 553`) `the officer believes on reasonable grounds that the public interest may be satisfied without arresting me` `the officer has no reasonable grounds to believe that I will fail to attend court if not arrested`

-- s 497. If by virtue of s 495(2) a peace officer does not arrest a person, the officer may issue an appearance notice if the offence is one of the three kinds in s 495(2)(a) to (c). JUST TRUE: yes. NOTHING: s 495(2) does not bar the arrest (so the condition of the permission fails), or the offence is not one of the three kinds.
@export Under section 497, may a peace officer who is barred by section 495(2) from arresting me issue me an appearance notice?
GIVEN `the kind of offence I am said to have committed` IS A `Class of offence` @desc What kind of offence is it said to be: an indictable offence, an offence punishable on summary conviction, or an offence that the section makes both indictable and punishable on summary conviction?
      `the offence is an indictable offence mentioned in section 553` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the offence one of the indictable offences mentioned in section 553?
      `the officer believes on reasonable grounds that the public interest may be satisfied without arresting me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the officer believe on reasonable grounds that the public interest may be satisfied without arresting you?
      `the officer has no reasonable grounds to believe that I will fail to attend court if not arrested` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the officer have no reasonable grounds to believe that you will fail to attend court if you are not arrested?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 497, may a peace officer who is barred by section 495(2) from arresting me issue me an appearance notice` IS
    `s 497 the peace officer may issue an appearance notice to the person` (`s 495(2) the peace officer is barred from arresting the person without warrant` `the kind of offence I am said to have committed` (`Under Part XVI helper: a stated fact as an answer` `the offence is an indictable offence mentioned in section 553`) `the officer believes on reasonable grounds that the public interest may be satisfied without arresting me` `the officer has no reasonable grounds to believe that I will fail to attend court if not arrested`) `the kind of offence I am said to have committed` (`Under Part XVI helper: a stated fact as an answer` `the offence is an indictable offence mentioned in section 553`)

-- s 498(1), with 498(1.01) and (2). A peace officer must release as soon as practicable a person arrested without warrant for an offence other than one listed in s 469, who has not been taken before a justice or released under another provision of Part XVI, if the officer intends to compel appearance by summons, issues an appearance notice, or the person gives an undertaking. The duty is subject to s 498(1.1), and does not apply to an offence described in s 503(3). JUST TRUE: yes. JUST FALSE: the text says no. NOTHING is not an answer here: you state whether the offence is listed in s 469, and no other input leaves the answer open.
@export Under section 498(1), must a peace officer release me as soon as practicable, subject to subsection 498(1.1)?
GIVEN `I was arrested without warrant, or was delivered to a peace officer under subsection 494(3) or placed in the custody of a peace officer under subsection 163.5(3) of the Customs Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you arrested without a warrant (or delivered to a peace officer after an arrest by a private person, or placed in a peace officer's custody under subsection 163.5(3) of the Customs Act)?
      `the offence I am charged with is one listed in section 469` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the offence you are charged with one of those listed in section 469 (for example murder, treason, piracy)?
      `I have already been taken before a justice or released from custody under another provision of this Part` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you already been taken before a justice, or released from custody under another provision of this Part?
      `how the release is to be effected` IS A `s 498(1) how the release is to be effected` @desc How would the release be effected: the officer intends to compel your appearance by summons, the officer issues you an appearance notice, you give an undertaking to the officer, or none of those?
      `the offence is one described in subsection 503(3)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the offence one described in subsection 503(3): an indictable offence alleged to have been committed in Canada outside the territorial division where you were arrested?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 498(1), must a peace officer release me as soon as practicable, subject to subsection 498(1.1)` IS
    `s 498(1) a peace officer must, as soon as practicable, release the person, subject to subsection (1.1)` `I was arrested without warrant, or was delivered to a peace officer under subsection 494(3) or placed in the custody of a peace officer under subsection 163.5(3) of the Customs Act` (`Under Part XVI helper: a stated fact as an answer` `the offence I am charged with is one listed in section 469`) `I have already been taken before a justice or released from custody under another provision of this Part` `how the release is to be effected` `the offence is one described in subsection 503(3)`

-- s 498(1.1). The peace officer must not release the person if the officer believes on reasonable grounds that it is necessary in the public interest that the person be detained or that release be dealt with under another provision, or that the person will fail to attend court if released. JUST TRUE: must not release. JUST FALSE: neither belief is held, so the bar is not engaged.
@export Under section 498(1.1), must the peace officer not release me?
GIVEN `the officer believes on reasonable grounds that it is necessary in the public interest that I be detained or that my release be dealt with under another provision` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the officer believe on reasonable grounds that it is necessary in the public interest that you be detained in custody, or that your release be dealt with under another provision of this Part?
      `the officer believes on reasonable grounds that I will fail to attend court if released` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the officer believe on reasonable grounds that you will fail to attend court if you are released?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 498(1.1), must the peace officer not release me` IS
    `s 498(1.1) the peace officer must not release the person` `the officer believes on reasonable grounds that it is necessary in the public interest that I be detained or that my release be dealt with under another provision` `the officer believes on reasonable grounds that I will fail to attend court if released`

§§ `Being taken before a justice (s 503)`

-- s 503(1). A peace officer who arrests a person with or without warrant, and has not released the person under another provision of Part XVI or under s 705.1, must detain the person and take the person before a justice. JUST TRUE: yes. JUST FALSE: the text says no.
@export Under section 503(1), must the peace officer cause me to be taken before a justice?
GIVEN `the peace officer arrested me, with or without warrant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did a peace officer arrest you, with or without a warrant?
      `the peace officer has released me under another provision of this Part or under section 705.1` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the peace officer already released you under another provision of Part XVI or under section 705.1?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 503(1), must the peace officer cause me to be taken before a justice` IS
    `s 503(1) the peace officer must cause the person to be taken before a justice` `the peace officer arrested me, with or without warrant` `the peace officer has released me under another provision of this Part or under section 705.1`

-- s 503(1)(a) and (b). Where a justice is available within 24 hours after the arrest, the person is to be taken before one without unreasonable delay and in any event within that period; otherwise as soon as possible. JUST TRUE: the requirement was met. JUST FALSE: it was not.
@export Under section 503(1), was I taken before a justice within the time the subsection requires?
GIVEN `a justice is available within 24 hours after my arrest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was a justice available within 24 hours after you were arrested?
      `the hours between the start of the period and my being taken before a justice` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours passed between the start of the 24-hour period (see section 503(2)) and the time you were taken before a justice?
      `I was taken before a justice without unreasonable delay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you taken before a justice without unreasonable delay?
      `I was taken before a justice as soon as possible` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you taken before a justice as soon as possible?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 503(1), was I taken before a justice within the time the subsection requires` IS
    `s 503(1) the person was taken before a justice as paragraph (a) or (b) requires` `a justice is available within 24 hours after my arrest` `the hours between the start of the period and my being taken before a justice` `I was taken before a justice without unreasonable delay` `I was taken before a justice as soon as possible`

-- s 503(1.1). Before the time in s 503(1)(a) or (b) expires, a peace officer who is satisfied that continued detention for an offence not listed in s 469 is no longer necessary must release the person if the officer issues an appearance notice or the person gives an undertaking. JUST TRUE: yes. JUST FALSE: the text says no. NOTHING is not an answer here: you state whether the offence is listed in s 469, and no other input leaves the answer open.
@export Under section 503(1.1), must the peace officer release me?
GIVEN `the time for taking me before a justice has not yet expired` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the time within which you must be taken before a justice (section 503(1)) not yet expired?
      `the peace officer is satisfied that my continued detention is no longer necessary` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the peace officer satisfied that your continued detention in custody is no longer necessary (having regard to the grounds in section 498(1.1))?
      `the offence I am charged with is one listed in section 469` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the offence you are charged with one of those listed in section 469 (for example murder, treason, piracy)?
      `the peace officer issues me an appearance notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the peace officer issue you an appearance notice?
      `I give an undertaking to the peace officer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you give an undertaking to the peace officer?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 503(1.1), must the peace officer release me` IS
    `s 503(1.1) the peace officer must release the person` `the time for taking me before a justice has not yet expired` `the peace officer is satisfied that my continued detention is no longer necessary` (`Under Part XVI helper: a stated fact as an answer` `the offence I am charged with is one listed in section 469`) `the peace officer issues me an appearance notice` `I give an undertaking to the peace officer`

-- s 503(2). Where the person was delivered to a peace officer under s 494(3) or placed in a peace officer's custody under s 163.5(3) of the Customs Act, the 24-hour period begins after the delivery; otherwise it begins with the arrest. The answer is the number of hours since the period began, which may exceed 24.
@export Under section 503(2), how many hours of the 24-hour period have run against me?
GIVEN `I was delivered to the peace officer under subsection 494(3) or placed in the officer's custody under subsection 163.5(3) of the Customs Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you delivered to a peace officer by a private person who arrested you, or placed in a peace officer's custody under subsection 163.5(3) of the Customs Act?
      `the hours since my arrest` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours have passed since you were arrested?
      `the hours since I was delivered to the officer` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours have passed since you were delivered to the peace officer? (0 if you were not delivered)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `Under section 503(2), how many hours of the 24-hour period have run against me` IS
    `s 503(2) the hours that run against the 24-hour period` `I was delivered to the peace officer under subsection 494(3) or placed in the officer's custody under subsection 163.5(3) of the Customs Act` `the hours since my arrest` `the hours since I was delivered to the officer`

§§ `Starting proceedings by information (ss 504 to 514)`

-- s 507.1(5). If the judge or designated justice does not issue a summons or warrant, the information is endorsed accordingly, and unless the informant commences proceedings to compel issue not later than six months after the endorsement, the information is deemed never to have been laid. The answer is the last day, counting the six months by calendar month (fork F-month-end).
@export Under section 507.1(5), what is the last day for commencing proceedings to compel the issue of a summons or warrant?
GIVEN `the date of the endorsement` IS A DATE @desc On what date did the judge or designated justice endorse the information that no process was to be issued?
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `Under section 507.1(5), what is the last day for commencing proceedings to compel the issue of a summons or warrant` IS
    `s 507.1(5) the last day for commencing proceedings to compel` `the date of the endorsement`

§§ `Interim release by a justice (ss 515 to 519.1)`

-- s 515(1). When an accused charged with an offence other than one listed in s 469 is taken before a justice, the justice must, unless a plea of guilty is accepted and unless the prosecutor shows cause why detention is justified or why another order should be made, make a release order without conditions. JUST TRUE: yes. JUST FALSE: the text says no. NOTHING is not an answer here: you state whether the offence is listed in s 469, and no other input leaves the answer open.
@export Under section 515(1), must the justice make a release order without conditions?
GIVEN `the offence I am charged with is one listed in section 469` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the offence you are charged with one of those listed in section 469 (for example murder, treason, piracy)?
      `I am taken before a justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you been taken before a justice?
      `a plea of guilty by me is accepted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is a plea of guilty by you accepted?
      `the prosecutor shows cause why my detention is justified or why another order should be made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the prosecutor, given a reasonable opportunity, shown cause why your detention in custody is justified, or why an order under another provision of section 515 should be made?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 515(1), must the justice make a release order without conditions` IS
    `s 515(1) the justice must make a release order without conditions` (`Under Part XVI helper: a stated fact as an answer` `the offence I am charged with is one listed in section 469`) `I am taken before a justice` `a plea of guilty by me is accepted` `the prosecutor shows cause why my detention is justified or why another order should be made`

-- s 515(6). Where the accused is charged as paragraphs (a) to (d) describe, the justice must order detention in custody unless the accused shows cause why detention is not justified. JUST TRUE: yes. JUST FALSE: the reverse onus does not apply, or cause is shown. NOTHING: whether the reverse onus applies is not known.
@export Under section 515(6), must the justice order that I be detained in custody?
GIVEN `whether the reverse onus of subsection 515(6) applies to me` IS AN `Under section 515 an answer the person may not know` @desc Does the reverse onus of subsection 515(6) apply to you (are you charged as one of paragraphs (a) to (d) describes)?
      `I, having been given a reasonable opportunity, show cause why my detention in custody is not justified by clearly demonstrating that my proposed release plan addresses the risks` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you, given a reasonable opportunity, shown cause why your detention in custody is not justified, by clearly demonstrating that your proposed release plan addresses the risks you pose as they relate to the grounds in subsection 515(10)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 515(6), must the justice order that I be detained in custody` IS
    `s 515(6) the justice must order that the accused be detained in custody` (`Under section 515 helper: the answer of a section the person may not know` `whether the reverse onus of subsection 515(6) applies to me`) `I, having been given a reasonable opportunity, show cause why my detention in custody is not justified by clearly demonstrating that my proposed release plan addresses the risks`

-- s 515(6)(b). One of the kinds of accused to whom the reverse onus of s 515(6) applies (paragraph (b)): charged with an indictable offence, other than one listed in s 469, and not ordinarily resident in Canada. JUST TRUE: the limb applies. JUST FALSE: the text says no. NOTHING is not an answer here: you state whether the offence is listed in s 469, and no other input leaves the answer open.
@export Under section 515(6)(b), am I charged with an indictable offence, other than one listed in section 469, and not ordinarily resident in Canada?
GIVEN `the kind of offence I am said to have committed` IS A `Class of offence` @desc What kind of offence is it said to be: an indictable offence, an offence punishable on summary conviction, or an offence that the section makes both indictable and punishable on summary conviction?
      `the prosecutor has elected to proceed by way of summary conviction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the prosecutor elected to proceed against you by way of summary conviction?
      `the offence I am charged with is one listed in section 469` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the offence you are charged with one of those listed in section 469 (for example murder, treason, piracy)?
      `I am not ordinarily resident in Canada` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you not ordinarily resident in Canada?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 515(6)(b), am I charged with an indictable offence, other than one listed in section 469, and not ordinarily resident in Canada` IS
    `s 515(6)(b) the accused is charged with an indictable offence, other than an offence listed in section 469, and is not ordinarily resident in Canada` offence (`Under Part XVI helper: a stated fact as an answer` `the offence I am charged with is one listed in section 469`) `I am not ordinarily resident in Canada`
    WHERE
        offence MEANS `s 493.01 the offence charged` OF `the kind of offence I am said to have committed`, `the prosecutor has elected to proceed by way of summary conviction`

-- s 515(10). Detention of an accused in custody is justified only on one or more of three grounds: necessary to ensure attendance in court, necessary for the protection or safety of the public, or necessary to maintain confidence in the administration of justice. JUST TRUE: a ground holds. JUST FALSE: none holds, and the text says detention is justified only on those grounds.
@export Under section 515(10), can my detention in custody be justified on one of the three grounds?
GIVEN `my detention is necessary to ensure my attendance in court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your detention necessary to ensure that you attend court in order to be dealt with according to law?
      `my detention is necessary for the protection or safety of the public` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your detention necessary for the protection or safety of the public, including any victim of or witness to the offence, or any person under the age of 18 years?
      `my detention is necessary to maintain confidence in the administration of justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your detention necessary to maintain confidence in the administration of justice, having regard to all the circumstances (section 515(10)(c) lists the apparent strength of the prosecution's case, the gravity of the offence, how it was committed, the penalty you face, and any outstanding charges from separate events)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 515(10), can my detention in custody be justified on one of the three grounds` IS
    `s 515(10) the detention of the accused in custody can be justified on one of the three grounds` `my detention is necessary to ensure my attendance in court` `my detention is necessary for the protection or safety of the public` `my detention is necessary to maintain confidence in the administration of justice`

§§ `Review of an order by a judge, and release by a judge (ss 520 to 523)`

-- s 520(1). Where a judge or justice makes an order under s 515(2), (5), (6), (7) or (12), or vacates, varies or makes an order under s 523(2)(b), the accused may at any time before the trial apply to a judge for a review of the order. JUST TRUE: yes. JUST FALSE: the application is not made before the trial. NOTHING: the order is not one of those named.
@export Under section 520(1), may I apply to a judge for a review of the order?
GIVEN `the order against me is one made under subsection 515(2), (5), (6), (7) or (12), or a vacating, varying or making of an order under paragraph 523(2)(b)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the order against you one made under subsection 515(2), (5), (6), (7) or (12), or an order vacated, varied or made under paragraph 523(2)(b)?
      `my application is made before the trial of the charge` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your application made before the trial of the charge?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 520(1), may I apply to a judge for a review of the order` IS
    `s 520(1) the accused may apply to a judge for a review of the order` `the order against me is one made under subsection 515(2), (5), (6), (7) or (12), or a vacating, varying or making of an order under paragraph 523(2)(b)` `my application is made before the trial of the charge`

-- s 520(2). Unless the prosecutor consents, the application must not be heard unless the accused has given the prosecutor at least two clear days' notice in writing. JUST TRUE: it may be heard. JUST FALSE: the text says no.
@export Under section 520(2), may my application be heard by a judge?
GIVEN `the clear days of notice in writing I gave the prosecutor` IS A NUMBER @desc How many clear days of notice in writing did you give the prosecutor of the application?
      `the prosecutor otherwise consents` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the prosecutor otherwise consent to the application being heard?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 520(2), may my application be heard by a judge` IS
    `s 520(2) the application may be heard by a judge` `the clear days of notice in writing I gave the prosecutor` `the prosecutor otherwise consents`

-- s 520(8). Once an application under s 520, 521 or 525 has been heard, a further application by the same accused must not be made, except with leave of a judge, before 30 days from the date of the decision have expired. JUST TRUE: barred. JUST FALSE: not barred. The 30 days run from the day of the decision as day 0 (fork F-30-days).
@export Under section 520(8), is a further application by me barred?
GIVEN `a previous application under section 520, 521 or 525 has been heard` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a previous application under section 520, 521 or 525 been heard?
      `the date of the decision of the judge who heard the previous application` IS A DATE @desc On what date did the judge decide the previous application?
      `the date of my further application` IS A DATE @desc On what date do you make your further application?
      `the judge has given me leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a judge given you leave to make a further application?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 520(8), is a further application by me barred` IS
    `s 520(8) the further application is barred` `a previous application under section 520, 521 or 525 has been heard` `the date of the decision of the judge who heard the previous application` `the date of my further application` `the judge has given me leave`

-- s 522(1). Where an accused is charged with an offence listed in s 469, no court, judge or justice, other than a judge of or presiding in a superior court of criminal jurisdiction for the province, may release the accused. JUST TRUE: barred. JUST FALSE: not barred. NOTHING is not an answer here: you state whether the offence is listed in s 469, and no other input leaves the answer open.
@export Under section 522(1), is the court, judge or justice barred from releasing me?
GIVEN `the offence I am charged with is one listed in section 469` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the offence you are charged with one of those listed in section 469 (for example murder, treason, piracy)?
      `the person who would release me is a judge of, or a judge presiding in, a superior court of criminal jurisdiction for the province where I am charged` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the person who would release you a judge of, or a judge presiding in, a superior court of criminal jurisdiction for the province in which you are charged?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 522(1), is the court, judge or justice barred from releasing me` IS
    `s 522(1) the court, judge or justice is barred from releasing the accused` (`Under Part XVI helper: a stated fact as an answer` `the offence I am charged with is one listed in section 469`) `the person who would release me is a judge of, or a judge presiding in, a superior court of criminal jurisdiction for the province where I am charged`

§§ `Failure to comply with release conditions, and delay (ss 523.1 to 526)`

-- s 523.1(3). If the judge or justice who hears the matter is satisfied that the accused failed to comply with a summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order or to attend court, and that the failure did not cause a victim physical or emotional harm, property damage or economic loss, the judge or justice must review the conditions of release. JUST TRUE: yes. JUST FALSE: the text says no.
@export Under section 523.1(3), must the judge or justice review the conditions of my release?
GIVEN `the judge or justice is satisfied that I failed to comply with a summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order or to attend court as required` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the judge or justice satisfied that you failed to comply with a summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order, or to attend court as required?
      `the judge or justice is satisfied that the failure did not cause a victim physical or emotional harm, property damage or economic loss` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the judge or justice satisfied that the failure did not cause a victim physical or emotional harm, property damage or economic loss?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 523.1(3), must the judge or justice review the conditions of my release` IS
    `s 523.1(3) the judge or justice must review any conditions of release that have been imposed on the accused` `the judge or justice is satisfied that I failed to comply with a summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order or to attend court as required` `the judge or justice is satisfied that the failure did not cause a victim physical or emotional harm, property damage or economic loss`

-- s 523.1(5). If the judge or justice makes a decision under s 523.1(3), no information may be laid and no indictment preferred against the accused for the failure. JUST TRUE: barred. JUST FALSE: the bar is not engaged.
@export Under section 523.1(5), is the laying of an information or the preferring of an indictment against me for the failure barred?
GIVEN `the judge or justice makes a decision under subsection (3)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the judge or justice made a decision under section 523.1(3) about your failure to comply?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 523.1(5), is the laying of an information or the preferring of an indictment against me for the failure barred` IS
    `s 523.1(5) the laying of an information or the preferring of an indictment against the accused for the failure referred to in paragraph (2)(a) is barred` `the judge or justice makes a decision under subsection (3)`

-- s 524(3). The judge or justice who hears the matter must cancel the summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order if the judge or justice finds that the accused contravened it or had been about to, or that there are reasonable grounds to believe the accused committed an offence while subject to it. JUST TRUE: yes. JUST FALSE: neither finding.
@export Under section 524(3), must the judge or justice cancel my summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order?
GIVEN `the judge or justice finds that I have contravened or had been about to contravene the summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the judge or justice find that you have contravened, or had been about to contravene, the summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order?
      `the judge or justice finds that there are reasonable grounds to believe that I have committed an offence while subject to it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the judge or justice find that there are reasonable grounds to believe that you committed an offence while subject to it?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 524(3), must the judge or justice cancel my summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order` IS
    `s 524(3) the judge or justice must cancel the summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order` `the judge or justice finds that I have contravened or had been about to contravene the summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order` `the judge or justice finds that there are reasonable grounds to believe that I have committed an offence while subject to it`

-- s 524(4). If the judge or justice cancels the document, the judge or justice must order detention in custody in three circumstances, (a) a finding that the accused contravened it, (b) a finding of reasonable grounds to believe the accused committed an indictable offence while subject to it, or (c) an outstanding charge for an offence referred to in s 515(6) or 522(2), unless the accused shows cause why detention is not justified under s 515(10). JUST TRUE: yes. JUST FALSE: the text says no. NOTHING: whether (c) applies is not known.
@export Under section 524(4), must the judge or justice order that I be detained in custody?
GIVEN `the judge or justice cancels the summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the judge or justice cancel the summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order?
      `the judge or justice found that I have contravened or had been about to contravene it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the judge or justice find under paragraph 524(3)(a) that you contravened, or had been about to contravene, it?
      `the judge or justice found that there are reasonable grounds to believe that I committed an offence while subject to it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the judge or justice find under paragraph 524(3)(b) that there are reasonable grounds to believe that you committed an offence while subject to it?
      `the kind of offence I am believed to have committed while subject to it` IS A `Class of offence` @desc What kind of offence are you believed to have committed while subject to the summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order: an indictable offence, an offence punishable on summary conviction, or an offence that the section makes both indictable and punishable on summary conviction?
      `the prosecutor has elected to proceed by way of summary conviction for the offence I am believed to have committed while subject to it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the prosecutor elected to proceed by way of summary conviction for that offence?
      `whether an outstanding charge against me is an offence referred to in subsection 515(6) or 522(2)` IS AN `Under section 515 an answer the person may not know` @desc Is any of the outstanding charges against you an offence referred to in subsection 515(6) or 522(2)?
      `I, having been given a reasonable opportunity, show cause why my detention is not justified under subsection 515(10) by clearly demonstrating that my release plan addresses the risks` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you, given a reasonable opportunity, shown cause why your detention in custody is not justified under subsection 515(10), by clearly demonstrating that your proposed release plan addresses the risks you pose?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 524(4), must the judge or justice order that I be detained in custody` IS
    `s 524(4) the judge or justice must order that the accused be detained in custody` `the judge or justice cancels the summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order` (`s 524(4) the circumstances of paragraph (a), (b) or (c) exist` `the judge or justice found that I have contravened or had been about to contravene it` `the judge or justice found that there are reasonable grounds to believe that I committed an offence while subject to it` offence (LIST (`Under section 515 helper: the answer of a section the person may not know` `whether an outstanding charge against me is an offence referred to in subsection 515(6) or 522(2)`))) `I, having been given a reasonable opportunity, show cause why my detention is not justified under subsection 515(10) by clearly demonstrating that my release plan addresses the risks`
    WHERE
        offence MEANS `s 493.01 the offence charged` OF `the kind of offence I am believed to have committed while subject to it`, `the prosecutor has elected to proceed by way of summary conviction for the offence I am believed to have committed while subject to it`

-- s 525(1). The person having custody of an accused who is charged with an offence other than one listed in s 469, is detained pending trial and is not required to be detained for any other matter, must apply to a judge to fix a date for a hearing if the trial has not commenced within 90 days (see 525(1.1) for the waiver). JUST TRUE: yes. JUST FALSE: the text says no. NOTHING is not an answer here: you state whether the offence is listed in s 469, and no other input leaves the answer open.
@export Under section 525(1), must the person having my custody apply to a judge to fix a date for a hearing?
GIVEN `the offence I am charged with is one listed in section 469` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the offence you are charged with one of those listed in section 469 (for example murder, treason, piracy)?
      `I am being detained in custody pending my trial for that offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you being detained in custody pending your trial for that offence?
      `I am required to be detained in custody in respect of any other matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you required to be detained in custody in respect of any other matter?
      `the day from which the 90 days are counted` IS A DATE @desc On what date do the 90 days begin (see the question on the day from which they are counted)?
      `the trial has commenced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has your trial commenced?
      `the day on which the trial commenced` IS A DATE @desc On what date did your trial commence? (any date if it has not)
      `the day in question` IS A DATE @desc What is today's date?
      `I have waived in writing my right to a hearing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you waived in writing your right to a hearing?
      `the judge has received the waiver before the 90 days expired` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the judge receive the waiver before the expiry of the 90-day period?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 525(1), must the person having my custody apply to a judge to fix a date for a hearing` IS
    `s 525(1) the person having the custody of the accused must apply to a judge to fix a date for a hearing` (`Under Part XVI helper: a stated fact as an answer` `the offence I am charged with is one listed in section 469`) `I am being detained in custody pending my trial for that offence` `I am required to be detained in custody in respect of any other matter` (`s 525(1) the trial has not commenced within 90 days from the day` `the day from which the 90 days are counted` `the trial has commenced` `the day on which the trial commenced` `the day in question` EQUALS JUST TRUE) (`s 525(1.1) the person having the custody of the accused is not required to make the application` `I have waived in writing my right to a hearing` `the judge has received the waiver before the 90 days expired` EQUALS JUST TRUE)

-- s 525(1)(a) and (b). The 90 days run from the day the accused was taken before a justice under s 503 or, where an order for detention has been made under s 521, s 523.1(3)(b)(ii) or s 524 or a decision made on a review under s 520, from the later of the day the accused was taken into custody under that order and the day of the decision (fork F-525-1-ab). The answer is a date.
@export Under section 525(1), from which day are the 90 days counted?
GIVEN `the day on which I was taken before a justice under section 503` IS A DATE @desc On what date were you taken before a justice under section 503?
      `an order that I be detained was made under section 521, paragraph 523.1(3)(b)(ii) or section 524, or a decision was made on a review under section 520` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was an order that you be detained in custody made under section 521, paragraph 523.1(3)(b)(ii) or section 524, or a decision made on a review under section 520?
      `the day I was taken into custody under that order` IS A DATE @desc On what date were you taken into custody under that order? (any date if there was no such order)
      `the day of the decision` IS A DATE @desc On what date was the decision made? (any date if there was no such decision)
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `Under section 525(1), from which day are the 90 days counted` IS
    `s 525(1) the day from which the 90 days are counted` `the day on which I was taken before a justice under section 503` `an order that I be detained was made under section 521, paragraph 523.1(3)(b)(ii) or section 524, or a decision was made on a review under section 520` `the day I was taken into custody under that order` `the day of the decision`

-- s 525(1.1). The person having custody of the accused is not required to make the application if the accused has waived in writing the right to a hearing and the judge has received the waiver before the 90 days expire. JUST TRUE: not required. JUST FALSE: not excused by this subsection.
@export Under section 525(1.1), is the person having my custody not required to make the application?
GIVEN `I have waived in writing my right to a hearing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you waived in writing your right to a hearing?
      `the judge has received the waiver before the 90 days expired` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the judge receive the waiver before the expiry of the 90-day period?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 525(1.1), is the person having my custody not required to make the application` IS
    `s 525(1.1) the person having the custody of the accused is not required to make the application` `I have waived in writing my right to a hearing` `the judge has received the waiver before the 90 days expired`

§§ `Being brought before a court from prison, endorsement of a warrant, and entry to a dwelling-house to arrest (ss 527 to 529.5)`

-- s 527(1). A judge of a superior court of criminal jurisdiction may order in writing that a person confined in a prison be brought before the court, judge, justice or provincial court judge, if the applicant sets out the facts in an affidavit and produces the warrant (if any) and the judge is satisfied that the ends of justice require it. JUST TRUE: yes. NOTHING: a gate or the limit fails; the text does not say that no order may be made.
@export Under section 527(1), may a judge order that I, confined in a prison, be brought before the court?
GIVEN `the person who would make the order is a judge of a superior court of criminal jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the person who would make the order a judge of a superior court of criminal jurisdiction?
      `I am confined in a prison` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you confined in a prison (as section 2 defines it)?
      `the applicant sets out the facts in an affidavit and produces the warrant, if any` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the applicant for the order set out the facts of the case in an affidavit and produce the warrant, if any?
      `the judge is satisfied that the ends of justice require that an order be made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the judge satisfied that the ends of justice require that an order be made?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 527(1), may a judge order that I, confined in a prison, be brought before the court` IS
    `s 527(1) the judge may order in writing that a person who is confined in a prison be brought before the court, judge, justice or provincial court judge` `the person who would make the order is a judge of a superior court of criminal jurisdiction` (`Under Part XVI helper: a stated fact as an answer` `I am confined in a prison`) `the applicant sets out the facts in an affidavit and produces the warrant, if any` `the judge is satisfied that the ends of justice require that an order be made`

-- s 528(1). Where a warrant for the arrest or committal of an accused cannot be executed under s 514 or 703, a justice within whose jurisdiction the accused is or is believed to be must, on application and proof on oath or by affidavit of the signature of the justice who issued the warrant, authorize the arrest within that jurisdiction by an endorsement. JUST TRUE: yes. JUST FALSE: the text says no.
@export Under section 528(1), must a justice authorize my arrest within the justice's jurisdiction by endorsing the warrant?
GIVEN `the warrant is a warrant for my arrest or committal in a form set out in Part XXVIII` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the warrant a warrant for your arrest or committal, in a form set out in Part XXVIII?
      `the warrant cannot be executed in accordance with section 514 or 703` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can the warrant not be executed in accordance with section 514 or 703?
      `the justice is one within whose jurisdiction I am or am believed to be` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the justice one within whose jurisdiction you are, or are believed to be?
      `an application has been made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has an application been made to the justice?
      `proof has been made on oath or by affidavit of the signature of the justice who issued the warrant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has proof been made, on oath or by affidavit, of the signature of the justice who issued the warrant?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 528(1), must a justice authorize my arrest within the justice's jurisdiction by endorsing the warrant` IS
    `s 528(1) the justice must authorize the arrest of the accused within the justice's jurisdiction by making an endorsement on the warrant` `the warrant is a warrant for my arrest or committal in a form set out in Part XXVIII` `the warrant cannot be executed in accordance with section 514 or 703` `the justice is one within whose jurisdiction I am or am believed to be` `an application has been made` `proof has been made on oath or by affidavit of the signature of the justice who issued the warrant`

-- s 529(1). A warrant to arrest or apprehend a person issued by a judge or justice under an Act of Parliament may authorize a peace officer to enter a dwelling-house described in the warrant, if the judge or justice is satisfied by information on oath in writing that there are reasonable grounds to believe the person is or will be present there. JUST TRUE: yes. NOTHING: a gate fails; the text does not say that no authorization may be given.
@export Under section 529(1), may a warrant authorize a peace officer to enter a dwelling-house to arrest or apprehend me?
GIVEN `the warrant is a warrant to arrest or apprehend me issued by a judge or justice under an Act of Parliament` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the warrant a warrant to arrest or apprehend you, issued by a judge or justice under an Act of Parliament?
      `the place described in the warrant is a dwelling-house` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the place described in the warrant a dwelling-house (as section 2 defines it)?
      `the judge or justice is satisfied by information on oath in writing that there are reasonable grounds to believe that I am or will be present in the dwelling-house` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the judge or justice satisfied, by information on oath in writing, that there are reasonable grounds to believe that you are or will be present in the dwelling-house?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 529(1), may a warrant authorize a peace officer to enter a dwelling-house to arrest or apprehend me` IS
    `s 529(1) the warrant may authorize a peace officer to enter a dwelling-house described in the warrant for the purpose of arresting or apprehending the person` `the warrant is a warrant to arrest or apprehend me issued by a judge or justice under an Act of Parliament` `the place described in the warrant is a dwelling-house` `the judge or justice is satisfied by information on oath in writing that there are reasonable grounds to believe that I am or will be present in the dwelling-house`

-- s 529(2). An authorization under s 529(1) is subject to the condition that the peace officer may not enter unless, immediately before entering, the officer has reasonable grounds to believe the person is present. JUST TRUE: barred. JUST FALSE: not barred. NOTHING: there is no authorization under s 529(1), so the condition does not speak.
@export Under section 529(2), is the peace officer barred from entering the dwelling-house?
GIVEN `an authorization to enter a dwelling-house has been granted under subsection 529(1)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has an authorization to enter the dwelling-house been granted under subsection 529(1)?
      `the peace officer has, immediately before entering, reasonable grounds to believe that I am present in the dwelling-house` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the peace officer have, immediately before entering, reasonable grounds to believe that you are present in the dwelling-house?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 529(2), is the peace officer barred from entering the dwelling-house` IS
    `s 529(2) the peace officer is barred from entering the dwelling-house` `an authorization to enter a dwelling-house has been granted under subsection 529(1)` `the peace officer has, immediately before entering, reasonable grounds to believe that I am present in the dwelling-house`

-- s 529.3(1). Without limiting any other power, a peace officer may enter a dwelling-house to arrest or apprehend a person without a warrant if the officer has reasonable grounds to believe the person is present and the conditions for a warrant under s 529.1 exist but exigent circumstances make it impracticable to obtain one. JUST TRUE: yes. NOTHING: a gate fails; other powers to enter are kept by the opening words.
@export Under section 529.3(1), may a peace officer enter the dwelling-house without a warrant to arrest or apprehend me?
GIVEN `the peace officer has reasonable grounds to believe that I am present in the dwelling-house` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the peace officer have reasonable grounds to believe that you are present in the dwelling-house?
      `the conditions for obtaining a warrant under section 529.1 exist` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do the conditions for obtaining a warrant under section 529.1 exist?
      `by reason of exigent circumstances it would be impracticable to obtain a warrant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc By reason of exigent circumstances, would it be impracticable to obtain a warrant?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 529.3(1), may a peace officer enter the dwelling-house without a warrant to arrest or apprehend me` IS
    `s 529.3(1) the peace officer may enter the dwelling-house for the purpose of arresting or apprehending a person without a warrant` `the peace officer has reasonable grounds to believe that I am present in the dwelling-house` `the conditions for obtaining a warrant under section 529.1 exist` `by reason of exigent circumstances it would be impracticable to obtain a warrant`

-- s 529.4(3). A peace officer who enters a dwelling-house without a warrant under s 529.3 may not enter without prior announcement unless, immediately before entering, the officer has reasonable grounds to suspect that announcing would expose any person to imminent bodily harm or death, or reasonable grounds to believe that it would result in the imminent loss or destruction of evidence of an indictable offence. JUST TRUE: barred. JUST FALSE: not barred. NOTHING: the officer is not entering under s 529.3.
@export Under section 529.4(3), is the peace officer barred from entering the dwelling-house without prior announcement?
GIVEN `the peace officer enters the dwelling-house without a warrant under section 529.3` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the peace officer entering the dwelling-house without a warrant, under section 529.3?
      `the peace officer has, immediately before entering, reasonable grounds to suspect that prior announcement would expose a person to imminent bodily harm or death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the peace officer have, immediately before entering, reasonable grounds to suspect that prior announcement would expose the officer or any other person to imminent bodily harm or death?
      `the peace officer has, immediately before entering, reasonable grounds to believe that prior announcement would result in the imminent loss or destruction of evidence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the peace officer have, immediately before entering, reasonable grounds to believe that prior announcement would result in the imminent loss or imminent destruction of evidence relating to the commission of an offence?
      `the kind of offence the evidence relates to` IS A `Class of offence` @desc What kind of offence does the evidence relate to: an indictable offence, an offence punishable on summary conviction, or an offence that the section makes both indictable and punishable on summary conviction?
      `the prosecutor has elected to proceed by way of summary conviction for the offence the evidence relates to` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the prosecutor elected to proceed by way of summary conviction for that offence?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 529.4(3), is the peace officer barred from entering the dwelling-house without prior announcement` IS
    `s 529.4(3) the peace officer is barred from entering the dwelling-house without prior announcement` `the peace officer enters the dwelling-house without a warrant under section 529.3` `the peace officer has, immediately before entering, reasonable grounds to suspect that prior announcement would expose a person to imminent bodily harm or death` `the peace officer has, immediately before entering, reasonable grounds to believe that prior announcement would result in the imminent loss or destruction of evidence` offence
    WHERE
        offence MEANS `s 493.01 the offence charged` OF `the kind of offence the evidence relates to`, `the prosecutor has elected to proceed by way of summary conviction for the offence the evidence relates to`

-- ====================================================================================================
-- seat questions from part ccc21 (merged by the lead on 2026-11-12; unchanged text follows)
-- ====================================================================================================
-- ccc21-seat.l4 -- THE SEAT LAYER over Parts XVII to XIX of the Criminal Code (Canada), R.S.C., 1985, c. C-46 (sections 530 to 572).
-- SEAT: a person who is, or may be, accused of an offence under the Criminal Code (the accused), asking on facts they can state what the language, preliminary inquiry,
-- election, re-election and mode-of-trial provisions mean for them. (Assumed, not ruled: the maintainer's instruction of 2026-11-09 that Singapore's Penal Code is the model; this is a plain seat layer, with no charge-generator contract.)
-- Every question calls a rule of the layer-1 modules and restates none. Each question takes plain inputs, one per fact the person can state, each with an @desc worded as the person would be asked;
-- the records the rules expect (the charge, the section 553 facts, the re-election facts) are built here from those answers by the helper functions below, which restate no rule.
-- A question's name begins "Under section N," and asks what its body answers, in the same direction: "must the court ..." over a rule that imposes a duty, "may ..." over a rule that grants a power,
-- "is ..." over a rule that states a status, "on what footing ..." over a rule that returns a footing. A question never turns a bar into a grant or the reverse.
-- How an answer reads: JUST TRUE is yes on the facts as stated; JUST FALSE is no, where the text says no in its own words; NOTHING is "not covered": the text does not answer on these facts (see NOTES.md, Reading a result).
-- Two answers are asked as plain facts that other parts of the Code decide, and taken as JUST of the fact: whether the offence is one listed in section 469, and whether an indictment has been preferred on a consent or order under section 577.
-- Not asked (NOTES.md, "Seat coverage (ccc21)", lists every layer-1 rule that no question reaches, by section): the other powers and duties of a justice, a judge, the court, a clerk, the sheriff,
-- the Chief Justice or a case management judge in running the proceeding, which the person is not asked about; what the prosecutor, counsel, a stenographer or a witness does, apart from the questions below;
-- rules that name no actor; definitions, deeming rules, the fixed words the court must say (the election, the re-election, the address to the unrepresented accused) and repeals;
-- and Part XIX's rules for an accused that is an organization (section 556), beyond the one question at section 570(5).

§ `Helpers: records built from the person's answers (they restate no rule)`


-- A placeholder for the section 553 facts, for the rules that never read them (section 535, 536(2), 536(4) and the like read the class, the mode and the most the offence allows, not the section 553 list).
`s 553 facts left blank because the rule called does not read them` MEANS
    `s 553 the charge` WITH
        `s 553 the accused is charged in an information` IS TRUE
        `s 553 the offence charged` IS `Provision of the Code` WITH `section` IS 0, `subsection` IS ""
        `s 553 the offence charged, as paragraph (a) names it` IS `s 553(a) an offence that paragraph (a) does not name`
        `s 553 the theft is theft of cattle` IS FALSE
        `s 553 the subject-matter of the offence is a testamentary instrument` IS FALSE
        `s 553 the alleged value of the subject-matter of the offence, in dollars` IS 0
        `s 553 how the accused is charged` IS `s 553(b) with the offence itself`
        `s 553 the offence is under paragraph 4(4)(a) of the Controlled Drugs and Substances Act` IS FALSE

-- The section 553 facts, from what the person states.
GIVEN info IS A BOOLEAN
      sec IS A NUMBER
      sub IS A STRING
      kind IS A `s 553(a) the offence charged, as paragraph (a) names it`
      cattle IS A BOOLEAN
      will IS A BOOLEAN
      value IS A NUMBER
      how IS A `s 553(b) how the accused is charged`
      cdsa IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `s 553 the charge`
`s 553 the charge as the accused states it` MEANS
    `s 553 the charge` WITH
        `s 553 the accused is charged in an information` IS info
        `s 553 the offence charged` IS `Provision of the Code` WITH `section` IS sec, `subsection` IS sub
        `s 553 the offence charged, as paragraph (a) names it` IS kind
        `s 553 the theft is theft of cattle` IS cattle
        `s 553 the subject-matter of the offence is a testamentary instrument` IS will
        `s 553 the alleged value of the subject-matter of the offence, in dollars` IS value
        `s 553 how the accused is charged` IS how
        `s 553 the offence is under paragraph 4(4)(a) of the Controlled Drugs and Substances Act` IS cdsa

-- The charge for Parts XVIII and XIX, from what the person states. "Whole years" and no "less a day": the most the offence allows is asked in years, and 0 years is taken as "no imprisonment is stated".
GIVEN class IS A `Class of offence`
      mode IS A `Mode of proceeding`
      life IS A BOOLEAN
      years IS A NUMBER
      facts IS A `s 553 the charge`
GIVETH A `s 535 the charge`
`s 535 the charge as the accused states it` MEANS
    `s 535 the charge` WITH
        `s 535 the class of the offence charged` IS class
        `s 535 the mode in which the prosecutor proceeds` IS mode
        `s 535 the maximum imprisonment for the offence on indictment` IS
            IF life
            THEN `imprisonment for life`
            ELSE IF years EQUALS 0
                 THEN `no imprisonment is stated`
                 ELSE `imprisonment for a term of` (`Length of time` OF years, 0, 0, FALSE)
        `s 535 the facts that bear on absolute jurisdiction` IS facts

-- The most the offence allows, alone (section 568 asks only that).
GIVEN life IS A BOOLEAN
      years IS A NUMBER
GIVETH AN Imprisonment
`s 568 the most the offence allows, as the accused states it` MEANS
    IF life
    THEN `imprisonment for life`
    ELSE IF years EQUALS 0
         THEN `no imprisonment is stated`
         ELSE `imprisonment for a term of` (`Length of time` OF years, 0, 0, FALSE)

-- What the accused answered when put to the election.
GIVEN elected IS A BOOLEAN
      mode IS A `s 536(2) mode of trial`
GIVETH A `s 536(2) the accused's answer when put to an election`
`s 536(2) the answer the accused gave, as the accused states it` MEANS
    IF elected
    THEN `s 536(2) elects` mode
    ELSE `s 536(2) does not elect`

-- The re-election facts, from what the person states. "The date the preliminary inquiry was completed" is read only if it has been completed.
GIVEN elected IS A `s 536(2) mode of trial`
      wish IS A `s 536(2) mode of trial`
      entitled IS A BOOLEAN
      requested IS A BOOLEAN
      completed IS A BOOLEAN
      completedOn IS A DATE
      reelectOn IS A DATE
      trialDay IS A DATE
      consent IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `s 561 the re-election facts`
`s 561 the re-election facts as the accused states them` MEANS
    `s 561 the re-election facts` WITH
        `s 561 the mode elected or deemed elected` IS elected
        `s 561 the mode the accused wishes to re-elect` IS wish
        `s 561 the accused is entitled to request a preliminary inquiry` IS entitled
        `s 561 a preliminary inquiry has been requested under subsection 536(4)` IS requested
        `s 561 the date the preliminary inquiry was completed` IS
            IF completed
            THEN JUST completedOn
            ELSE NOTHING
        `s 561 the date of the re-election` IS reelectOn
        `s 561 the day first appointed for the trial` IS trialDay
        `s 561 the prosecutor has consented in writing` IS consent


§ `PART XVII -- Language of the accused (sections 530 to 534)`


-- Situation: Applies where you ask to be tried before a justice or judge who speaks your official language, or the court considers it.


§§ `Sections 530 to 530.2 -- An order that you be tried in your official language`

-- section 530(1)
-- JUST TRUE: the court must grant it. JUST FALSE: your language is not an official language, or you applied late or not at all. NOTHING: not covered.
@export Under section 530(1), must the court grant an order that I be tried before a justice or judge who speaks my official language?
GIVEN `my language is one of the official languages of Canada` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your language one of the official languages of Canada (English or French)?
      `I have applied for an order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you applied for an order that you be tried before a justice or judge who speaks your language?
      `the date I applied` IS A DATE @desc On what date did you apply for the order?
      `the date of the appearance at which my trial date is set` IS A DATE @desc On what date is the appearance at which your trial date is set (or was it set)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 530(1), must the court grant an order that I be tried before a justice or judge who speaks my official language` IS
    `s 530(1) the judge, provincial court judge or justice shall grant the order` `my language is one of the official languages of Canada` `I have applied for an order` `the date I applied` `the date of the appearance at which my trial date is set`

-- section 530(2)
-- For an accused whose language is not an official language of Canada. JUST TRUE: the court may grant it (the text gives no more than "may"). NOTHING: outside the limit of the grant.
@export Under section 530(2), may the court grant an order that I be tried before a justice or judge who speaks the official language in which I can best give testimony?
GIVEN `my language is one of the official languages of Canada` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your language one of the official languages of Canada (English or French)?
      `I have applied for an order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you applied for an order that you be tried before a justice or judge who speaks your language?
      `the date I applied` IS A DATE @desc On what date did you apply for the order?
      `the date of the appearance at which my trial date is set` IS A DATE @desc On what date is the appearance at which your trial date is set (or was it set)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 530(2), may the court grant an order that I be tried before a justice or judge who speaks the official language in which I can best give testimony` IS
    `s 530(2) the judge, provincial court judge or justice may grant the order` `my language is one of the official languages of Canada` `I have applied for an order` `the date I applied` `the date of the appearance at which my trial date is set`

-- section 530(3)
@export Under section 530(3), must the court before which I first appear ensure that I am advised of my right to apply for an order and of the time before which I must apply?
GIVEN `I am appearing before this court for the first time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is this the first time you appear before this judge, provincial court judge, judge of the Nunavut Court of Justice or justice of the peace?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 530(3), must the court before which I first appear ensure that I am advised of my right to apply for an order and of the time before which I must apply` IS
    `s 530(3) the judge, provincial court judge, judge of the Nunavut Court of Justice or justice of the peace shall ensure that the accused is advised of the right and of the time` `I am appearing before this court for the first time`

-- section 530(4)
@export Under section 530(4), may the court by order remand me to be tried before a justice or judge who speaks that language although I failed to apply?
GIVEN `I have not applied for an order under section 530(1) or (2)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you failed to apply for an order under section 530(1) or (2)?
      `the court is satisfied that it is in the best interests of justice that I be tried before a justice or judge who speaks that language` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the court satisfied that it is in the best interests of justice that you be tried before a justice or judge who speaks your language?
      `the court speaks that language` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the court that is to try you speak your language (or, if it is not an official language, the official language in which you can best give testimony)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 530(4), may the court by order remand me to be tried before a justice or judge who speaks that language although I failed to apply` IS
    `s 530(4) the court may by order remand the accused to be tried before a justice or judge who speaks that language` `I have not applied for an order under section 530(1) or (2)` `the court is satisfied that it is in the best interests of justice that I be tried before a justice or judge who speaks that language` `the court speaks that language`


-- Situation: Applies only once an order has been granted under section 530.


§§ `Sections 530.01 to 530.1 -- Translation, and your rights once an order is granted`

-- section 530.01(1)(a)
@export Under section 530.01(1)(a), must the prosecutor, on my application, cause the portions of the information or indictment that are not in my language to be translated?
GIVEN `an order has been granted that I be tried in my official language` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has an order been granted under section 530 that you be tried before a justice or judge who speaks your official language?
      `the prosecutor is a private prosecutor` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the prosecutor a private prosecutor?
      `I have applied for the translation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you applied for the translation of the information or indictment?
      `a portion of the information or indictment is in an official language that is not mine` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is a portion of the information or indictment in an official language that is not yours (or not the one in which you can best give testimony)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 530.01(1)(a), must the prosecutor, on my application, cause the portions of the information or indictment that are not in my language to be translated` IS
    `s 530.01(1)(a) the prosecutor shall cause the portions to be translated` `an order has been granted that I be tried in my official language` `the prosecutor is a private prosecutor` `I have applied for the translation` `a portion of the information or indictment is in an official language that is not mine`

-- section 530.01(1)(b)
@export Under section 530.01(1)(b), must the prosecutor provide me with a written copy of the translated text at the earliest possible time?
GIVEN `an order has been granted that I be tried in my official language` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has an order been granted under section 530 that you be tried before a justice or judge who speaks your official language?
      `the prosecutor is a private prosecutor` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the prosecutor a private prosecutor?
      `I have applied for the translation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you applied for the translation of the information or indictment?
      `a portion of the information or indictment is in an official language that is not mine` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is a portion of the information or indictment in an official language that is not yours (or not the one in which you can best give testimony)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 530.01(1)(b), must the prosecutor provide me with a written copy of the translated text at the earliest possible time` IS
    `s 530.01(1)(b) the prosecutor shall provide the accused with a written copy of the translated text` (`s 530.01(1)(a) the prosecutor shall cause the portions to be translated` `an order has been granted that I be tried in my official language` `the prosecutor is a private prosecutor` `I have applied for the translation` `a portion of the information or indictment is in an official language that is not mine`)

-- section 530.1(a)
@export Under section 530.1(a), do I and my counsel have the right to use either official language for all purposes during the preliminary inquiry and trial?
GIVEN `an order has been granted that I be tried in my official language` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has an order been granted under section 530 that you be tried before a justice or judge who speaks your official language?
      `the stage of the proceedings` IS A `Stage of the proceedings` @desc At what stage are the proceedings against you?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 530.1(a), do I and my counsel have the right to use either official language for all purposes during the preliminary inquiry and trial` IS
    `s 530.1(a) the accused and counsel have the right to use either official language for all purposes` `an order has been granted that I be tried in my official language` `the stage of the proceedings`

-- section 530.1(b)
@export Under section 530.1(b), may I and my counsel use either official language in written pleadings or other documents?
GIVEN `an order has been granted that I be tried in my official language` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has an order been granted under section 530 that you be tried before a justice or judge who speaks your official language?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 530.1(b), may I and my counsel use either official language in written pleadings or other documents` IS
    `s 530.1(b) the accused and counsel may use either official language in written pleadings or other documents` `an order has been granted that I be tried in my official language`

-- section 530.1(c)
@export Under section 530.1(c), may any witness give evidence in either official language during my preliminary inquiry or trial?
GIVEN `an order has been granted that I be tried in my official language` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has an order been granted under section 530 that you be tried before a justice or judge who speaks your official language?
      `the stage of the proceedings` IS A `Stage of the proceedings` @desc At what stage are the proceedings against you?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 530.1(c), may any witness give evidence in either official language during my preliminary inquiry or trial` IS
    `s 530.1(c) any witness may give evidence in either official language` `an order has been granted that I be tried in my official language` `the stage of the proceedings`

-- section 530.1(d)
@export Under section 530.1(d), do I have a right to have a justice presiding over my preliminary inquiry who speaks my official language or both official languages?
GIVEN `an order has been granted that I be tried in my official language` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has an order been granted under section 530 that you be tried before a justice or judge who speaks your official language?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 530.1(d), do I have a right to have a justice presiding over my preliminary inquiry who speaks my official language or both official languages` IS
    `s 530.1(d) the accused has a right to have a justice presiding over the preliminary inquiry who speaks the official language of the accused or both official languages` `an order has been granted that I be tried in my official language`

-- section 530.1(e)
@export Under section 530.1(e), do I have a right to have a prosecutor, other than a private prosecutor, who speaks my official language or both official languages?
GIVEN `an order has been granted that I be tried in my official language` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has an order been granted under section 530 that you be tried before a justice or judge who speaks your official language?
      `the prosecutor is a private prosecutor` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the prosecutor a private prosecutor?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 530.1(e), do I have a right to have a prosecutor, other than a private prosecutor, who speaks my official language or both official languages` IS
    `s 530.1(e) the accused has a right to have a prosecutor who speaks the official language of the accused or both official languages` `an order has been granted that I be tried in my official language` `the prosecutor is a private prosecutor`

-- section 530.1(f)
@export Under section 530.1(f), must the court make interpreters available to assist me, my counsel or any witness during the preliminary inquiry or trial?
GIVEN `an order has been granted that I be tried in my official language` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has an order been granted under section 530 that you be tried before a justice or judge who speaks your official language?
      `the stage of the proceedings` IS A `Stage of the proceedings` @desc At what stage are the proceedings against you?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 530.1(f), must the court make interpreters available to assist me, my counsel or any witness during the preliminary inquiry or trial` IS
    `s 530.1(f) the court shall make interpreters available` `an order has been granted that I be tried in my official language` `the stage of the proceedings`

-- section 530.1(h)
@export Under section 530.1(h), must the court make the written trial judgment available in my official language?
GIVEN `an order has been granted that I be tried in my official language` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has an order been granted under section 530 that you be tried before a justice or judge who speaks your official language?
      `the trial judgment is issued in writing in either official language` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the trial judgment, with any reasons, issued in writing in either official language?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 530.1(h), must the court make the written trial judgment available in my official language` IS
    `s 530.1(h) the court shall make the written trial judgment available in the official language of the accused` `an order has been granted that I be tried in my official language` `the trial judgment is issued in writing in either official language`


-- Situation: Section 531 applies only once an order has been made under section 530.


§§ `Sections 531 and 532 -- Where the trial is held; other language rights`

-- section 531
@export Under section 531, must the court order that my trial be held in another territorial division in the same province?
GIVEN `an order has been granted that I be tried in my official language` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has an order been granted under section 530 that you be tried before a justice or judge who speaks your official language?
      `the order cannot be conveniently complied with in the territorial division where the offence would otherwise be tried` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Can the order not be conveniently complied with in the territorial division in which the offence would otherwise be tried?
      `the province or territory in which that territorial division lies` IS A `Province or territory` @desc In which province or territory does that territorial division lie?
      `a regulation made under section 533 governs the case` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a regulation made under section 533 govern your case?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 531, must the court order that my trial be held in another territorial division in the same province` IS
    `s 531 the court shall order that the trial be held in another territorial division in the same province` `an order has been granted that I be tried in my official language` `the order cannot be conveniently complied with in the territorial division where the offence would otherwise be tried` `the province or territory in which that territorial division lies` `a regulation made under section 533 governs the case`

-- section 532
@export Under section 532, do Part XVII and the Official Languages Act leave in place a right I have under a law of a province about the language of proceedings or testimony in criminal matters?
GIVEN `the right is afforded by a law of a province` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the right you are asking about afforded by a law of a province?
      `the law was in force when Part XVII came into force in that province or came into force after it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was that law in force when Part XVII came into force in the province, or did it come into force after it?
      `the right relates to the language of proceedings or testimony in criminal matters` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the right relate to the language of proceedings or testimony in criminal matters?
      `the right is inconsistent with Part XVII or the Official Languages Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the right inconsistent with Part XVII of the Criminal Code or with the Official Languages Act?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 532, do Part XVII and the Official Languages Act leave in place a right I have under a law of a province about the language of proceedings or testimony in criminal matters` IS
    `s 532 this Part and the Official Languages Act do not derogate from the right` `the right is afforded by a law of a province` `the law was in force when Part XVII came into force in that province or came into force after it` `the right relates to the language of proceedings or testimony in criminal matters` `the right is inconsistent with Part XVII or the Official Languages Act`


§ `PART XVIII -- Procedure on preliminary inquiry (sections 535 to 551)`


-- Situation: Applies where you are charged with an indictable offence and appear before a justice.


§§ `Sections 535 to 536.5 -- The inquiry, your election and your requests`

-- section 535
@export Under section 535, must the justice inquire into the charge against me?
GIVEN `the class of the offence I am charged with` IS A `Class of offence` @desc Is the offence you are charged with an indictable offence, an offence punishable on summary conviction, or one that the section makes both?
      `how the prosecutor is proceeding` IS A `Mode of proceeding` @desc Is the prosecutor proceeding by indictment or on summary conviction?
      `the most the offence allows on indictment is imprisonment for life` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the most that the offence you are charged with allows on indictment imprisonment for life?
      `the most years of imprisonment the offence allows on indictment` IS A NUMBER @desc If the most is not life, what is the most the offence allows on indictment, in whole years? (Give 0 if the section states no imprisonment.)
      `I am before a justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you before a justice?
      `a request has been made for a preliminary inquiry under subsection 536(4) or 536.1(3)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you or the prosecutor requested a preliminary inquiry?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 535, must the justice inquire into the charge against me` IS
    `s 535 the justice shall inquire into the charge` `my charge` `I am before a justice` `a request has been made for a preliminary inquiry under subsection 536(4) or 536.1(3)`
    WHERE
        `my charge` MEANS `s 535 the charge as the accused states it` `the class of the offence I am charged with` `how the prosecutor is proceeding` `the most the offence allows on indictment is imprisonment for life` `the most years of imprisonment the offence allows on indictment` `s 553 facts left blank because the rule called does not read them`

-- section 536(2)
@export Under section 536(2), must the justice put me to an election in the words of subsection (2)?
GIVEN `the class of the offence I am charged with` IS A `Class of offence` @desc Is the offence you are charged with an indictable offence, an offence punishable on summary conviction, or one that the section makes both?
      `how the prosecutor is proceeding` IS A `Mode of proceeding` @desc Is the prosecutor proceeding by indictment or on summary conviction?
      `the most the offence allows on indictment is imprisonment for life` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the most that the offence you are charged with allows on indictment imprisonment for life?
      `the most years of imprisonment the offence allows on indictment` IS A NUMBER @desc If the most is not life, what is the most the offence allows on indictment, in whole years? (Give 0 if the section states no imprisonment.)
      `the person I am before` IS A `s 536(1) who the accused is before` @desc Before whom are you: a justice other than a provincial court judge, a provincial court judge, or someone who is not a justice?
      `the offence I am charged with is one listed in section 469` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the offence you are charged with one listed in section 469 of the Criminal Code?
      `the information has been read to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the information been read to you?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 536(2), must the justice put me to an election in the words of subsection (2)` IS
    `s 536(2) the justice shall put the accused to an election in the words of subsection (2)` (`s 536(2) the accused is one referred to in subsection (2)` `my charge` `the person I am before` `the answer about section 469`) `the information has been read to me`
    WHERE
        `my charge` MEANS `s 535 the charge as the accused states it` `the class of the offence I am charged with` `how the prosecutor is proceeding` `the most the offence allows on indictment is imprisonment for life` `the most years of imprisonment the offence allows on indictment` `s 553 facts left blank because the rule called does not read them`
        `the answer about section 469` MEANS JUST `the offence I am charged with is one listed in section 469`

-- section 536(2.1)
@export Under section 536(2.1), must the justice put me to an election in the words of subsection (2.1)?
GIVEN `the class of the offence I am charged with` IS A `Class of offence` @desc Is the offence you are charged with an indictable offence, an offence punishable on summary conviction, or one that the section makes both?
      `how the prosecutor is proceeding` IS A `Mode of proceeding` @desc Is the prosecutor proceeding by indictment or on summary conviction?
      `the most the offence allows on indictment is imprisonment for life` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the most that the offence you are charged with allows on indictment imprisonment for life?
      `the most years of imprisonment the offence allows on indictment` IS A NUMBER @desc If the most is not life, what is the most the offence allows on indictment, in whole years? (Give 0 if the section states no imprisonment.)
      `I am charged in an information` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you charged in an information?
      `the section of the Code that creates the offence` IS A NUMBER @desc Which section of the Criminal Code creates the offence you are charged with? (Give the section number, with any decimal part.)
      `the subsection of that section` IS A STRING @desc Which subsection of that section creates it? (For example (1); leave empty if none.)
      `the offence as paragraph 553(a) names it` IS A `s 553(a) the offence charged, as paragraph (a) names it` @desc Is the offence one that paragraph 553(a) names (theft, obtaining by false pretences, possessing the proceeds of an indictable offence, defrauding, or mischief under subsection 430(4)), and if so which?
      `the theft is theft of cattle` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are charged with theft, is it theft of cattle?
      `the subject-matter of the offence is a testamentary instrument` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the subject-matter of the offence a testamentary instrument?
      `the alleged value of the subject-matter of the offence in dollars` IS A NUMBER @desc What is the alleged value of the subject-matter of the offence, in dollars?
      `how I am charged` IS A `s 553(b) how the accused is charged` @desc Are you charged with the offence itself, or with counselling it, conspiring or attempting to commit it, or being an accessory after the fact to it?
      `the offence is under paragraph 4(4)(a) of the Controlled Drugs and Substances Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the offence one under paragraph 4(4)(a) of the Controlled Drugs and Substances Act?
      `the person I am before` IS A `s 536(1) who the accused is before` @desc Before whom are you: a justice other than a provincial court judge, a provincial court judge, or someone who is not a justice?
      `the offence I am charged with is one listed in section 469` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the offence you are charged with one listed in section 469 of the Criminal Code?
      `the information has been read to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the information been read to you?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 536(2.1), must the justice put me to an election in the words of subsection (2.1)` IS
    `s 536(2.1) the justice shall put the accused to an election in the words of subsection (2.1)` (`s 536(2.1) the accused is one referred to in subsection (2.1)` `my charge` `the person I am before` `the answer about section 469`) `the information has been read to me`
    WHERE
        `my section 553 facts` MEANS `s 553 the charge as the accused states it` `I am charged in an information` `the section of the Code that creates the offence` `the subsection of that section` `the offence as paragraph 553(a) names it` `the theft is theft of cattle` `the subject-matter of the offence is a testamentary instrument` `the alleged value of the subject-matter of the offence in dollars` `how I am charged` `the offence is under paragraph 4(4)(a) of the Controlled Drugs and Substances Act`
        `my charge` MEANS `s 535 the charge as the accused states it` `the class of the offence I am charged with` `how the prosecutor is proceeding` `the most the offence allows on indictment is imprisonment for life` `the most years of imprisonment the offence allows on indictment` `my section 553 facts`
        `the answer about section 469` MEANS JUST `the offence I am charged with is one listed in section 469`

-- section 536(4)
@export Under section 536(4), must the justice hold a preliminary inquiry into the charge against me?
GIVEN `the class of the offence I am charged with` IS A `Class of offence` @desc Is the offence you are charged with an indictable offence, an offence punishable on summary conviction, or one that the section makes both?
      `how the prosecutor is proceeding` IS A `Mode of proceeding` @desc Is the prosecutor proceeding by indictment or on summary conviction?
      `the most the offence allows on indictment is imprisonment for life` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the most that the offence you are charged with allows on indictment imprisonment for life?
      `the most years of imprisonment the offence allows on indictment` IS A NUMBER @desc If the most is not life, what is the most the offence allows on indictment, in whole years? (Give 0 if the section states no imprisonment.)
      `the person I am before` IS A `s 536(1) who the accused is before` @desc Before whom are you: a justice other than a provincial court judge, a provincial court judge, or someone who is not a justice?
      `the offence I am charged with is one listed in section 469` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the offence you are charged with one listed in section 469 of the Criminal Code?
      `I elected a mode of trial when put to my election` IS A BOOLEAN @desc When you were put to your election, did you elect a mode of trial? (No, if you did not elect.)
      `the mode of trial I elected` IS A `s 536(2) mode of trial` @desc Which mode of trial did you elect: a provincial court judge, a judge without a jury, or a court composed of a judge and jury?
      `I am deemed under paragraph 565(1)(a) to have elected to be tried by a court composed of a judge and jury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was your election or re-election declined (section 567) so that you are deemed to have elected a court composed of a judge and jury?
      `I or the prosecutor made a request for a preliminary inquiry in time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you or the prosecutor make a request for a preliminary inquiry when put to the election or within the period fixed?
      `an indictment has been preferred against me on the basis of a consent or order given under section 577` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has an indictment been preferred against you on the basis of a consent or order given under section 577?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 536(4), must the justice hold a preliminary inquiry into the charge against me` IS
    `s 536(4) the justice shall hold a preliminary inquiry into the charge` `my charge` `the person I am before` `the answer about section 469` `my answer when put to the election` `I am deemed under paragraph 565(1)(a) to have elected to be tried by a court composed of a judge and jury` `I or the prosecutor made a request for a preliminary inquiry in time` `the answer about section 577`
    WHERE
        `my charge` MEANS `s 535 the charge as the accused states it` `the class of the offence I am charged with` `how the prosecutor is proceeding` `the most the offence allows on indictment is imprisonment for life` `the most years of imprisonment the offence allows on indictment` `s 553 facts left blank because the rule called does not read them`
        `the answer about section 469` MEANS JUST `the offence I am charged with is one listed in section 469`
        `my answer when put to the election` MEANS `s 536(2) the answer the accused gave, as the accused states it` `I elected a mode of trial when put to my election` `the mode of trial I elected`
        `the answer about section 577` MEANS JUST `an indictment has been preferred against me on the basis of a consent or order given under section 577`

