-- ====================================================================================================
-- seat questions from part ccc24 (merged by the lead on 2026-11-12; unchanged text follows)
-- ====================================================================================================
-- ccc24-seat.l4 -- the Criminal Code (Canada), R.S.C., 1985, c. C-46, sections 673 to 715.27 -- THE SEAT LAYER (part agent ccc24).
--
-- SEAT: a person who is, or may be, accused of an offence under the Criminal Code (the accused), asking on facts they can state.
-- This layer asks, from that position, the questions the modules imported above answer about appeals, a Minister's review of a conviction,
-- being summoned or held as a witness, and evidence taken away from the trial. Nothing here is new law: each question calls a layer-1 rule and restates none.
--
-- PLAIN INPUTS. Every question takes plain inputs (yes or no, a number, a section number, or a choice from the module's own list), one for each fact the person can state,
-- each with an @desc worded as the person would be asked. Where a layer-1 rule takes a fact that another part of the Code decides (the section 553 answer in subsections 686(5) and (5.01),
-- the section 743.6 and section 745.51(1) orders in subsections 675(2.1) and (2.3)), the question asks it as a plain yes or no and passes it on as JUST of that answer.
-- A layer-1 input that the rule called never reads is given a fixed placeholder, with a comment saying so (section 683(1), section 683(2)).
--
-- HOW TO READ AN ANSWER. Every question answers JUST TRUE (the text's words are met on the facts given), JUST FALSE (the text says no in its own words) or NOTHING (not covered by the text).
-- Questions that ask a bar ("barred", "does the section bar") answer JUST TRUE where the bar applies. NOTHING is never the same as no (see NOTES.md, "Reading a result: FALSE and NOTHING").
-- The two flat negatives ("does not apply", "does not invalidate") are asked positively and answer JUST FALSE.
-- No seat is written for an official (a court, a judge, the Minister, the Commission, the Attorney General): their powers appear only as what may be done to the person in the seat.


§ `Criminal Code -- appeals, review of a conviction, attending as a witness and giving evidence (sections 673 to 715.27): the questions of a person who is, or may be, accused`

§§ `Appealing a conviction or sentence (Part XXI, sections 673 to 677)`
-- Arises once you have been convicted, sentenced or acquitted at trial; ask "Have I been convicted or sentenced by a trial court?" before showing the group.

-- answers: s 673 sentence
@export Under section 673, is this order or disposition part of my "sentence" for the purposes of the appeal rules?
GIVEN `the section of the Criminal Code that the order or disposition was made under` IS A NUMBER @desc Under which section number of the Criminal Code was the order or disposition made? (for example 109 or 738)
      `the subsection of that section, if it is named` IS A STRING @desc Under which subsection? (for example (1) or (3); leave empty if none is named)
      `the order was made under subsection 16(1) of the Controlled Drugs and Substances Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the order made under subsection 16(1) of the Controlled Drugs and Substances Act?
      `the order was made under subsection 94(1) of the Cannabis Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the order made under subsection 94(1) of the Cannabis Act?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 673, is this order or disposition part of my "sentence" for the purposes of the appeal rules` IS
    `s 673 sentence` (`Provision of the Code` OF `the section of the Criminal Code that the order or disposition was made under`, `the subsection of that section, if it is named`) `the order was made under subsection 16(1) of the Controlled Drugs and Substances Act` `the order was made under subsection 94(1) of the Cannabis Act`

-- answers: s 674 the proceedings are barred as an appeal not authorized by Part XXI or Part XXVI
@export Under section 674, are the proceedings I propose barred as an appeal that Part XXI or Part XXVI does not authorize?
GIVEN `my proceedings are by way of appeal in proceedings in respect of an indictable offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you proposing to take proceedings by way of appeal in proceedings about an indictable offence?
      `my proceedings are authorized by Part XXI or Part XXVI` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does Part XXI (indictable appeals) or Part XXVI (extraordinary remedies) authorize the proceedings you propose?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 674, are the proceedings I propose barred as an appeal that Part XXI or Part XXVI does not authorize` IS
    `s 674 the proceedings are barred as an appeal not authorized by Part XXI or Part XXVI` `my proceedings are by way of appeal in proceedings in respect of an indictable offence` `my proceedings are authorized by Part XXI or Part XXVI`

-- answers: s 675(1)(a) the convicted person may appeal to the court of appeal against the conviction on the ground
@export Under section 675(1)(a), may I appeal to the court of appeal against my conviction on this ground?
GIVEN `I was convicted by a trial court in proceedings by indictment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you convicted by a trial court in proceedings by indictment?
      `the ground of my appeal` IS A `s 675(1)(a) ground of appeal against conviction` @desc What is the ground of your appeal: a question of law alone, a question of fact or of mixed law and fact, or some other ground?
      `the court of appeal has granted me leave to appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the court of appeal granted you leave to appeal?
      `a judge of the court of appeal has granted me leave to appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a judge of the court of appeal granted you leave to appeal?
      `the trial judge has certified that my case is a proper case for appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the trial judge certified that your case is a proper case for appeal?
      `the court of appeal considers my ground a sufficient ground of appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the court of appeal consider your ground a sufficient ground of appeal?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 675(1)(a), may I appeal to the court of appeal against my conviction on this ground` IS
    `s 675(1)(a) the convicted person may appeal to the court of appeal against the conviction on the ground` `I was convicted by a trial court in proceedings by indictment` `the ground of my appeal` (`s 675(1)(a) the conditions that the ground carries are met` `the ground of my appeal` `the court of appeal has granted me leave to appeal` `a judge of the court of appeal has granted me leave to appeal` `the trial judge has certified that my case is a proper case for appeal` `the court of appeal considers my ground a sufficient ground of appeal`)

-- answers: s 675(1)(b) the convicted person may appeal to the court of appeal against the sentence passed by the trial court
@export Under section 675(1)(b), may I appeal to the court of appeal against my sentence?
GIVEN `I was convicted by a trial court in proceedings by indictment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you convicted by a trial court in proceedings by indictment?
      `the court of appeal or a judge of it has granted me leave to appeal my sentence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the court of appeal, or a judge of it, granted you leave to appeal your sentence?
      `my sentence is one fixed by law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your sentence one fixed by law (for example, life imprisonment for murder)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 675(1)(b), may I appeal to the court of appeal against my sentence` IS
    `s 675(1)(b) the convicted person may appeal to the court of appeal against the sentence passed by the trial court` `I was convicted by a trial court in proceedings by indictment` `the court of appeal or a judge of it has granted me leave to appeal my sentence` `my sentence is one fixed by law`

-- answers: s 675(1.1) the person may appeal a summary conviction or its sentence to the court of appeal as if it had been a conviction in proceedings by indictment
@export Under section 675(1.1), may I appeal my summary conviction or its sentence to the court of appeal as if it had been a conviction by indictment?
GIVEN `the court of appeal or a judge of it has granted me leave to appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the court of appeal, or a judge of it, granted you leave to appeal?
      `there has been no appeal with respect to my summary conviction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it true that no appeal has been taken with respect to your summary conviction?
      `my summary conviction offence was tried together with an indictable offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the offence you were summarily convicted of tried together with an indictable offence?
      `there is an appeal in respect of the indictable offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is there an appeal in respect of the indictable offence?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 675(1.1), may I appeal my summary conviction or its sentence to the court of appeal as if it had been a conviction by indictment` IS
    `s 675(1.1) the person may appeal a summary conviction or its sentence to the court of appeal as if it had been a conviction in proceedings by indictment` `the court of appeal or a judge of it has granted me leave to appeal` `there has been no appeal with respect to my summary conviction` `my summary conviction offence was tried together with an indictable offence` `there is an appeal in respect of the indictable offence`

-- answers: s 675(2) the person may appeal to the court of appeal against the number of years in excess of 10 of the imprisonment without eligibility for parole
@export Under section 675(2), may I appeal against the number of years beyond ten without eligibility for parole that I was given?
GIVEN `the kind of homicide offence I was convicted of` IS A `Kind of homicide offence` @desc Which kind of homicide offence were you convicted of?
      `my manslaughter was in the circumstances set out in paragraphs 236(2)(a) to (d)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you were convicted of manslaughter, was it in the circumstances set out in paragraphs 236(2)(a) to (d) (a firearm, or by a person in a criminal organization)?
      `I was sentenced to life imprisonment without eligibility for parole for a specified number of years` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you sentenced to imprisonment for life without eligibility for parole for a specified number of years?
      `the specified number of years without eligibility for parole` IS A NUMBER @desc How many years without eligibility for parole were specified?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 675(2), may I appeal against the number of years beyond ten without eligibility for parole that I was given` IS
    `s 675(2) the person may appeal to the court of appeal against the number of years in excess of 10 of the imprisonment without eligibility for parole` `the kind of homicide offence I was convicted of` `my manslaughter was in the circumstances set out in paragraphs 236(2)(a) to (d)` `I was sentenced to life imprisonment without eligibility for parole for a specified number of years` `the specified number of years without eligibility for parole`

-- answers: s 675(2.1) the person against whom an order under section 743.6 has been made may appeal to the court of appeal against the order
@export Under section 675(2.1), may I appeal to the court of appeal against an order delaying my eligibility for parole?
GIVEN `the court ordered a delay in my eligibility for parole under section 743.6` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the sentencing court order, under section 743.6, that you serve more of your sentence before being eligible for parole?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 675(2.1), may I appeal to the court of appeal against an order delaying my eligibility for parole` IS
    `s 675(2.1) the person against whom an order under section 743.6 has been made may appeal to the court of appeal against the order` (JUST `the court ordered a delay in my eligibility for parole under section 743.6`)

-- answers: s 675(2.2) the person may appeal to the court of appeal against the number of years in excess of the minimum number of years without eligibility for parole
@export Under section 675(2.2), if I was under 18 when I did it, may I appeal against the years beyond the minimum that I must serve before parole?
GIVEN `I was under the age of 18 when I committed the offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you under the age of 18 at the time you committed the offence?
      `the kind of homicide offence I was convicted of` IS A `Kind of homicide offence` @desc Which kind of homicide offence were you convicted of?
      `my manslaughter was in the circumstances set out in paragraphs 236(2)(a) to (d)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you were convicted of manslaughter, was it in the circumstances set out in paragraphs 236(2)(a) to (d)?
      `I was sentenced to life imprisonment without eligibility for parole until I have served a period specified by the trial judge` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you sentenced to imprisonment for life without eligibility for parole until you have served the period specified by the judge presiding at the trial?
      `the specified period is more than the minimum I must serve` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is that specified period more than the minimum number of years you are required to serve?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 675(2.2), if I was under 18 when I did it, may I appeal against the years beyond the minimum that I must serve before parole` IS
    `s 675(2.2) the person may appeal to the court of appeal against the number of years in excess of the minimum number of years without eligibility for parole` `I was under the age of 18 when I committed the offence` `the kind of homicide offence I was convicted of` `my manslaughter was in the circumstances set out in paragraphs 236(2)(a) to (d)` `I was sentenced to life imprisonment without eligibility for parole until I have served a period specified by the trial judge` `the specified period is more than the minimum I must serve`

-- answers: s 675(2.3) the person against whom an order under subsection 745.51(1) has been made may appeal to the court of appeal against the order
@export Under section 675(2.3), may I appeal to the court of appeal against an order of the court under subsection 745.51(1)?
GIVEN `the court made an order against me under subsection 745.51(1)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the court make an order against you under subsection 745.51(1) (parole ineligibility for multiple murders)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 675(2.3), may I appeal to the court of appeal against an order of the court under subsection 745.51(1)` IS
    `s 675(2.3) the person against whom an order under subsection 745.51(1) has been made may appeal to the court of appeal against the order` (JUST `the court made an order against me under subsection 745.51(1)`)

-- answers: s 675(3) the person may appeal to the court of appeal against the verdict
@export Under section 675(3), may I appeal against a verdict that I am unfit to stand trial or not criminally responsible?
GIVEN `the verdict rendered in respect of me` IS A `Decision on the charge` @desc What was the verdict or decision in your case?
      `the ground of my appeal` IS A `s 675(1)(a) ground of appeal against conviction` @desc What is the ground of your appeal: a question of law alone, a question of fact or of mixed law and fact, or some other ground?
      `the court of appeal has granted me leave to appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the court of appeal granted you leave to appeal?
      `a judge of the court of appeal has granted me leave to appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a judge of the court of appeal granted you leave to appeal?
      `the trial judge has certified that my case is a proper case for appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the trial judge certified that your case is a proper case for appeal?
      `the court of appeal considers my ground a sufficient ground of appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the court of appeal consider your ground a sufficient ground of appeal?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 675(3), may I appeal against a verdict that I am unfit to stand trial or not criminally responsible` IS
    `s 675(3) the person may appeal to the court of appeal against the verdict` `the verdict rendered in respect of me` (`s 675(1)(a) the conditions that the ground carries are met` `the ground of my appeal` `the court of appeal has granted me leave to appeal` `a judge of the court of appeal has granted me leave to appeal` `the trial judge has certified that my case is a proper case for appeal` `the court of appeal considers my ground a sufficient ground of appeal`)

-- answers: s 675(4) the appellant may have the application for leave to appeal determined by the court of appeal
@export Under section 675(4), may I have the court of appeal decide my application for leave to appeal after a judge refused it?
GIVEN `a judge of the court of appeal refused me leave to appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did a judge of the court of appeal refuse you leave to appeal?
      `the refusal was of leave to appeal my sentence under paragraph 675(1)(b)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the refusal a refusal of leave to appeal your sentence (under paragraph 675(1)(b))?
      `I have filed notice in writing with the court of appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you filed notice in writing with the court of appeal that you want the application determined by the court?
      `the number of days from the refusal to my filing the notice, or to today if I have not filed it` IS A NUMBER @desc How many days passed between the refusal and your filing the notice (or, if you have not filed it, between the refusal and today)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 675(4), may I have the court of appeal decide my application for leave to appeal after a judge refused it` IS
    `s 675(4) the appellant may have the application for leave to appeal determined by the court of appeal` `a judge of the court of appeal refused me leave to appeal` `the refusal was of leave to appeal my sentence under paragraph 675(1)(b)` `I have filed notice in writing with the court of appeal` `the number of days from the refusal to my filing the notice, or to today if I have not filed it`

-- answers: s 676(1)(a) the Attorney General may appeal to the court of appeal against the acquittal or verdict
@export Under section 676(1)(a), may the Attorney General appeal against my acquittal or my verdict of not criminally responsible?
GIVEN `the appeal is brought by the Attorney General or counsel instructed by the Attorney General for the purpose` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the appeal brought by the Attorney General, or by counsel the Attorney General instructed for the purpose?
      `the decision is my acquittal, or a verdict that I am not criminally responsible on account of mental disorder, by a trial court in proceedings by indictment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the decision your acquittal, or a verdict that you are not criminally responsible on account of mental disorder, by a trial court in proceedings by indictment?
      `the ground of the appeal involves a question of law alone` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the ground of the appeal involve a question of law alone?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 676(1)(a), may the Attorney General appeal against my acquittal or my verdict of not criminally responsible` IS
    `s 676(1)(a) the Attorney General may appeal to the court of appeal against the acquittal or verdict` `the appeal is brought by the Attorney General or counsel instructed by the Attorney General for the purpose` `the decision is my acquittal, or a verdict that I am not criminally responsible on account of mental disorder, by a trial court in proceedings by indictment` `the ground of the appeal involves a question of law alone`

-- answers: s 676(1)(b) the Attorney General may appeal to the court of appeal against the order of a superior court of criminal jurisdiction
@export Under section 676(1)(b), may the Attorney General appeal against an order of a superior court that quashed my indictment or refused to exercise jurisdiction on it?
GIVEN `the appeal is brought by the Attorney General or counsel instructed by the Attorney General for the purpose` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the appeal brought by the Attorney General, or by counsel the Attorney General instructed for the purpose?
      `the order is an order of a superior court of criminal jurisdiction that quashes my indictment or in any manner refuses or fails to exercise jurisdiction on it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the order one of a superior court of criminal jurisdiction that quashes your indictment or in any manner refuses or fails to exercise jurisdiction on it?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 676(1)(b), may the Attorney General appeal against an order of a superior court that quashed my indictment or refused to exercise jurisdiction on it` IS
    `s 676(1)(b) the Attorney General may appeal to the court of appeal against the order of a superior court of criminal jurisdiction` `the appeal is brought by the Attorney General or counsel instructed by the Attorney General for the purpose` `the order is an order of a superior court of criminal jurisdiction that quashes my indictment or in any manner refuses or fails to exercise jurisdiction on it`

-- answers: s 676(1)(c) the Attorney General may appeal to the court of appeal against the order of a trial court
@export Under section 676(1)(c), may the Attorney General appeal against an order of the trial court that stayed my proceedings or quashed my indictment?
GIVEN `the appeal is brought by the Attorney General or counsel instructed by the Attorney General for the purpose` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the appeal brought by the Attorney General, or by counsel the Attorney General instructed for the purpose?
      `the order is an order of the trial court that stays proceedings on my indictment or quashes my indictment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the order one of the trial court that stays the proceedings on your indictment, or quashes your indictment?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 676(1)(c), may the Attorney General appeal against an order of the trial court that stayed my proceedings or quashed my indictment` IS
    `s 676(1)(c) the Attorney General may appeal to the court of appeal against the order of a trial court` `the appeal is brought by the Attorney General or counsel instructed by the Attorney General for the purpose` `the order is an order of the trial court that stays proceedings on my indictment or quashes my indictment`

-- answers: s 676(1)(d) the Attorney General may appeal to the court of appeal against the sentence passed by a trial court
@export Under section 676(1)(d), may the Attorney General appeal against my sentence?
GIVEN `the appeal is brought by the Attorney General or counsel instructed by the Attorney General for the purpose` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the appeal brought by the Attorney General, or by counsel the Attorney General instructed for the purpose?
      `the appeal is against the sentence passed by a trial court in proceedings by indictment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the appeal against the sentence a trial court passed on you in proceedings by indictment?
      `the court of appeal or a judge of it has granted leave to appeal the sentence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the court of appeal, or a judge of it, granted leave to appeal your sentence?
      `my sentence is one fixed by law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your sentence one fixed by law (for example, life imprisonment for murder)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under section 676(1)(d), may the Attorney General appeal against my sentence` IS
    `s 676(1)(d) the Attorney General may appeal to the court of appeal against the sentence passed by a trial court` `the appeal is brought by the Attorney General or counsel instructed by the Attorney General for the purpose` `the appeal is against the sentence passed by a trial court in proceedings by indictment` `the court of appeal or a judge of it has granted leave to appeal the sentence` `my sentence is one fixed by law`

-- answers: s 676(3) the Attorney General may appeal to the court of appeal against a verdict that an accused is unfit to stand trial
@export Under section 676(3), may the Attorney General appeal against a verdict that I am unfit to stand trial?
GIVEN `the appeal is brought by the Attorney General or counsel instructed by the Attorney General for the purpose` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the appeal brought by the Attorney General, or by counsel the Attorney General instructed for the purpose?
      `the appeal is against a verdict that I am unfit to stand trial` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the appeal against a verdict that you are unfit to stand trial?
